window.__F=window.__F||{};window.__F["ep-20.html"]="<div class=\"ac-info\"><p class=\"ac-i-main\">الشرح الأصلي بالإنكليزية.</p><p class=\"ac-i-tip\"><b>متى تستعمله:</b> ابحث بالرمز بخانة البحث. ابدأ دائماً بـ«الفحص المسبق» للمحرك قبل الرمز.</p><div class=\"ac-chips\"><span class=\"en-chip\">P = نقل الحركة: 70</span></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong>كيف تقرأ كود العطل</strong><small>الرقم بعد الشرطة يدل على نوع العطل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"ac-legend\"><div><b dir=\"ltr\">-11</b><span>قصر على الأرضي</span></div><div><b dir=\"ltr\">-12</b><span>قصر على البطارية</span></div><div><b dir=\"ltr\">-13</b><span>دائرة مفتوحة</span></div><div><b dir=\"ltr\">-15</b><span>قصر على البطارية أو دائرة مفتوحة</span></div><div><b dir=\"ltr\">-16</b><span>الفولتية أقل من الحد</span></div><div><b dir=\"ltr\">-17</b><span>الفولتية أعلى من الحد</span></div><div><b dir=\"ltr\">-2A</b><span>مفتاح عالق Stuck</span></div><div><b dir=\"ltr\">-84 / 85</b><span>الإشارة أقل / أعلى من المدى</span></div><div><b dir=\"ltr\">-92</b><span>أداء ضعيف أو تشغيل غير صحيح</span></div><div><b dir=\"ltr\">-00</b><span>عطل عام أو بدون نوع محدد</span></div></div><p class=\"bcm-note\">الحرف الأول: B = كهرباء الجسم · C = الهيكل والحساسات · P = نقل الحركة · U = اتصال الشبكة. نفّذ دائماً الفحص المسبق Pre-Diagnostic قبل خطوات أي كود.</p></div></details></div><input type=\"search\" class=\"ec-search acc-q\" placeholder=\"ابحث: كود العطل أو كلمة من الوصف\" aria-label=\"بحث\"><p class=\"bcm-note acc-cnt\">70 كود عطل. اضغط على الكود لترى شرحه.</p><div class=\"acc-list\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p000b bank 1 camshaft 2 position slow response\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P000B</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">BANK 1 CAMSHAFT 2 POSITION SLOW RESPONSE</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Variable Camshaft Timing (VCT) system continuously varies the Camshaft target position based on the engine operating parameters. The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) calculates or models the optimum Camshaft angular position and makes adjustments using the Variable Camshaft Timing (VCT) Solenoid. The VCT Solenoid is energized to move the internal Oil Control Valve to direct the flow of oil to a hydraulic actuator (Phaser) on the Camshaft Sprocket to change the Camshaft position relative to the Crankshaft position. The CMP Sensor is used to monitor the position of the Camshaft.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">A VCT diagnostic is performed to detect a Target Error and a Slow Response of the Camshaft, problems which could potentially increase tailpipe emissions. The diagnostic is primarily performed by comparing the actual Camshaft position to that of a calculated model for the Camshaft position. This diagnostic is continuous once the enable conditions are met, however the Slow Response and Target Error conditions are not performed simultaneously.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">The Target Error diagnostic evaluates the actual Camshaft position versus the modeled position when the Camshaft is in a relatively stable position. The difference between the two is considered to be the amount of &quot;error&quot;. If the error amount is greater than a calibrated threshold, it is considered failing. The Slow Response diagnostic is only performed when the modeled Camshaft position is moving (phasing) in a particular direction, advancing or retarding. The modeled Camshaft phasing position is created to match only the worst possible case of a passing camshaft position. If the actual camshaft phasing operates slower than the model by a calibrated threshold, it is considered failing.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">If either of these diagnostics fail, the Camshaft Position Slow Response DTC will set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Engine speed is at least 150 rpm. No CMP Sensor or CKP Sensor plausibility errors. This is performed even when phasing control is not enabled, to verify the actuator is keeping the camshaft at the default (lockpin) phase.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The actual measured camshaft position does not match the desired camshaft position set point.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>ENGINE OIL DIRTY OR CONTAMINATED</li><li>INCORRECT ENGINE OIL VISCOSITY</li><li>INSUFFICIENT ENGINE OIL PRESSURE</li><li>VARIABLE CAM TIMING (VCT) CONTROL CIRCUIT OPEN OR SHORTED</li><li>GROUND CIRCUIT OPEN OR HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>VARIABLE CAM TIMING SOLENOID</li><li>CAMSHAFT PHASER</li><li>IMPROPER ASSEMBLY OF CAMSHAFT/CRANKSHAFT TIMING CHAIN AND SPROCKETS</li><li>ENGINE OIL PRESSURE SENSOR OR CIRCUIT</li><li>ENGINE OIL TEMPERATURE SENSOR OR CIRCUIT</li><li>HIGH FRICTION IN CAMSHAFT OR OTHER VALVETRAIN COMPONENTS</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           If DTC P0013 is active, diagnose and repair that DTC before continuing before continuing with this test procedure.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The most likely cause for this DTC to set is high friction in the valvetrain components such as the camshaft and valve springs.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           Diagnose and repair any system voltage DTCs before continuing with this test.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read and record all DTCs and freeze frame data in the PCM for future reference.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>With the scan tool, erase DTCs in the PCM.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Test drive the vehicle under the conditions in which the DTC was set.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Is the DTC active or pending at this time?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>ENGINE OIL/ENGINE MECHANICAL</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>The following items should be checked before continuing with this procedure. Failure to do so may lead to misdiagnosis. LOW ENGINE OIL LEVEL INCORRECT ENGINE OIL FILTER (DOES NOT MEET OEM SPECIFICATIONS) DETERIORATED OR DIRTY OIL CONTAMINATED ENGINE OIL INCORRECT ENGINE OIL VISCOSITY</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">AERATED ENGINE OIL</p><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           If the engine oil is contaminated, inspect the Camshaft Position Solenoid for contamination, foreign material or any condition that would restrict movement of the valve. The solenoid must be replaced if damaged or if valve movement is restricted due to contamination.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any of the above conditions present?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE ENGINE OIL PRESSURE AND ENGINE MECHANICAL CONDITION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Install an oil pressure gauge and check the engine oil pressure in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to CHECKING ENGINE OIL PRESSURE .</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           With the scan tool, compare the engine oil pressure reading on the gauge with the scan tool reading at different engine speeds. If the pressure reading on the scan tool is not close to what the gauge displays, perform the diagnostic procedure for P0521-ENGINE OIL PRESSURE SENSOR PERFORMANCE.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Check the engine mechanical condition (i.e. proper oil drain back, oil sludge, etc.)</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the appropriate repairs.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>VARIABLE CAM TIMING (VCT) SOLENOID CONTROL ACTUATION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The VCT Solenoid actuates whenever the ignition key is turned on for cleaning purposes. This actuation also take place when the engine stops. So if you are testing the solenoid with a test light it is possible that you will see the solenoid actuate at key on and engine stop.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, actuate the VCT Solenoid control to toggle on and off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Listen for the VCT Solenoid to click open and closed during the actuation.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the Variable Cam Timing Solenoid click open and closed during the actuation?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 9</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>CHECK THE (K442) VCT CONTROL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO GROUND</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          The VCT Slow Response monitor should be suspended when a VCT Control circuit fault is detected. However it is possible for the monitors to overlap and fail. Therefore the circuits should be checked before condemning and replacing any of the VCT components.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the VCT Solenoid harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Check for continuity between ground and the (K442) VCT Control circuit at the VCT Solenoid harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the continuity between ground and the (K442) VCT Control circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K442) VCT Control circuit for a short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Go To 6</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>CHECK THE (K442) VCT CONTROL CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:       Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K442) VCT Control circuit between the VCT Solenoid harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Go To 7</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Repair the (K442) VCT Control circuit for a short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>CHECK THE GROUND CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Measure the resistance of the Ground circuit at the VCT Solenoid harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Go To 8</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Repair the excessive resistance or open in the Ground circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">8</span><span>VARIABLE CAM TIMING (VCT) SOLENOID</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Inspect the wiring and connectors between the VCT Solenoid and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the VCT Solenoid and Powertrain Control Module connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Remove the VCT Solenoid in accordance with the Service Information.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>Inspect the VCT Solenoid for contamination, foreign material, or any condition that would restrict movement of the valve.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The solenoid must be replaced if it is damaged or if valve movement is restricted due to contamination.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Replace the VCT Solenoid in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">9</span><span>CAMSHAFT PHASER</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Remove the Camshaft Phaser in accordance with the Service information. Refer to CHAIN AND SPROCKETS, TIMING, REMOVAL .</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Inspect the Phaser for contamination, foreign material, or any condition that would restrict operation of the Phaser.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Inspect the Phaser oil passages for signs of sludge or foreign material.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The Phaser should not be disassembled. If the Phaser is damaged, it must be replaced.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found with the Camshaft Phaser?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Replace the Camshaft Phaser in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Go To 10</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">10</span><span>CHECK FOR HIGH FRICTION IN THE VALVETRAIN COMPONENTS</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Remove the Camshaft in accordance with the Service Information.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>While removing the components to remove the Camshaft inspect the rocker arms, valve springs, and pushrods that may cause an increase in the torque to rotate the camshaft.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Inspect the camshaft and camshaft bearings for any signs of excessive wear.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found with the camshaft or any other valvetrain components?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Replace the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0013 bank 1 camshaft 2 position actuator circuit open\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0013</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">BANK 1 CAMSHAFT 2 POSITION ACTUATOR CIRCUIT OPEN</small><small>العطل: دائرة مفتوحة</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Variable Camshaft Timing (VCT) system continuously varies the Camshaft target position based on the engine operating parameters. The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) calculates or models the optimum Camshaft angular position and makes adjustments using the Variable Camshaft Timing (VCT) Solenoid. The VCT Solenoid is energized to move the internal Oil Control Valve to direct the flow of oil to a hydraulic actuator (Phaser) on the Camshaft Sprocket to change the Camshaft position relative to the Crankshaft position. The CMP Sensor is used to monitor the position of the Camshaft.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">A VCT diagnostic is performed to detect a Target Error and a Slow Response of the Camshaft, problems which could potentially increase tailpipe emissions. The diagnostic is primarily performed by comparing the actual Camshaft position to that of a calculated model for the Camshaft position. This diagnostic is continuous once the enable conditions are met, however the Slow Response and Target Error conditions are not performed simultaneously.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">The Target Error diagnostic evaluates the actual Camshaft position versus the modeled position when the Camshaft is in a relatively stable position. The difference between the two is considered to be the amount of &quot;error&quot;. If the error amount is greater than a calibrated threshold, it is considered failing. The Slow Response diagnostic is only performed when the modeled Camshaft position is moving (phasing) in a particular direction, advancing or retarding. The modeled Camshaft phasing position is created to match only the worst possible case of a passing camshaft position. If the actual camshaft phasing operates slower than the model by a calibrated threshold, it is considered failing.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">If either of these diagnostics fail, the Camshaft Position Slow Response DTC will set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the engine running. Battery voltage is between 11.0 and 18.0 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The PCM detects that the actual voltage of the Variable Cam Timing (VCT) Solenoid Control circuit does not match the intended state.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>VCT CONTROL CIRCUIT OPEN OR HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>VCT CONTROL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO GROUND</li><li>VCT CONTROL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>GROUND CIRCUIT OPEN OR HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>VARIABLE CAM TIMING (VCT) SOLENOID</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, record the related Freeze Fame Data and erase DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Start the engine and allow it to reach normal operating temperature. Hold engine speed greater than 1000 rpm for at least one minute. The DTC will not set unless cam phasing becomes active, which may not occur if speed never leaves idle.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">WARNING:        When the engine is operating, do not stand in direct line with the fan. Do not put your hands near the pulleys, belts, or fan. Do not wear loose clothing. Failure to follow these instructions can result possible serious or fatal injury.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CAMSHAFT POSITION SOLENOID CLEANING</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>With the scan tool perform the Exhaust Phaser 1 Cleaning function.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Start the engine and allow it to reach normal operating temperature. Hold engine speed greater than 1000 rpm for at least one minute.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Did the DTC return?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Repair complete.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K442) VCT CONTROL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO VOLTAGE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the VCT Solenoid harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:       Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Check for continuity between the (K442) VCT Control circuit and all other power supply circuits at the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between the (K442) VCT Control circuit and any other power supply circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the short between the (K442) VCT Control circuit and the circuit that had continuity.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE (K442) VCT CONTROL CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K442) VCT Control circuit between the VCT Solenoid harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K442) VCT Control circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>CHECK THE (K442) VCT CONTROL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for continuity between ground and the (K442) VCT Control circuit at the VCT Solenoid harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between ground and the (K442) VCT Control circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K442) VCT Control circuit for a short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>CHECK THE VCT SOLENOID GROUND CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance between ground and the VCT Solenoid Ground circuit at the VCT Solenoid harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 7</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the VCT Solenoid ground control circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>VCT SOLENOID</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Reconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Measure the voltage between ground and the (K442) VCT Control circuit at the VCT Solenoid harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          The test light should be off except for a brief period immediately after turning ignition on.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          The VVT Solenoid Driver circuit is a 12 volt PWM circuit. The voltage reading will vary depending on the actuation percentage that is selected for the solenoid.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there voltage present and does it match approximately to the percentage selected?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the VCT Solenoid and Powertrain Control Module connectors. If no problems are found, replace the VCT Solenoid in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 8</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">8</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the VCT solenoid and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the VCT solenoid and Powertrain Control Module harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0016 crankshaft/camshaft timing misalignment - bank 1 sensor 1\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0016</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">CRANKSHAFT/CAMSHAFT TIMING MISALIGNMENT - BANK 1 SENSOR 1</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">For proper engine control and combustion stability the Camshaft (CMP) and Crankshaft (CKP) positions must be aligned and known so that fuel injection and spark timing can be introduced at the intended Piston and Intake/Exhaust Valve positions. During vehicle manufacturing, at end of line, or if a Cam/Crank Relearn is performed, the system learns the difference between the Camshaft and Crankshaft position in terms of engine degrees and stores it in the EEPROM. During every following driving cycle, the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) uses this value to compare.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Within a driving cycle, once the CMP position and CKP position are known and verified they are considered &quot;locked.&quot; This diagnostic will only run when both sensor signals are in the &quot;locked&quot; state. Engine position is constantly tracked as the CMP Sensor and CKP Sensor signals are monitored by the PCM. An engine rotating at a constant speed would produce engine signals that match the nominal pattern exactly. With engine acceleration and deceleration the engine signals are compressed or stretched in time relative to nominal timing. The PCM takes into account and adjusts for any acceleration/deceleration rates that are in effect.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The misalignment fault accounts for angular deviation between the CMP Sensor and CKP Sensor signals due to miss builds, chain stretch, or skipped tooth. This fault results from the difference between the positions as indicated by the CMP Sensor and CKP Sensor signals. The result is only valid if both positions are &quot;locked&quot;.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Battery voltage above 11.0 volts. Engine speed between approximately 500 - 5500 rpm, depending on PCM calibration. Engine coolant temperature above 10°C (50°F). Engine oil temperature is above between 5°C (41°F) and 10°C (50°F), depending on PCM calibration. Engine oil pressure above approximately 220 kPa (31.9 psi).</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The difference between the Bank 1 Exhaust Camshaft position and the Crankshaft position is more than 10.0 degrees.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate. If the vehicle is equipped with the stop/start feature, the system will be disabled when this DTC is active.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>INCORRECT ENGINE OIL VISCOSITY OR OIL FILTER</li><li>ENGINE OIL AERATED OR CONTAMINATED</li><li>INSUFFICIENT OIL PRESSURE DUE TO A MECHANICAL FAILURE IN THE OILING SYSTEM</li><li>CAMSHAFT 1/2 OIL CONTROL VALVE FAULTY</li><li>CAMSHAFT 1/2 PHASER/TARGET WHEEL</li><li>TIMING CHAIN/SPROCKET ALIGNMENT</li><li>BROKEN OR FAULTY TIMING CHAIN TENSIONER OR GUIDES</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           Perform any Service Bulletins or Star Cases relating to this fault before continuing with this test procedure.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           Diagnose and repair any Camshaft Oil Control Valve Circuit or Slow Response DTCs before continuing with this diagnostic test.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           If a Cam Phaser or Camshaft Oil Control Valve is stuck, there may also be a Camshaft Position Slow Response and/or Multiple Cylinder Misfire DTC present.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Record the related Freeze Frame data.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, erase DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Start the engine and allow it to idle. It maybe necessary to tests drive the vehicle and allow cam phasing to occur in order to reset the fault.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">WARNING:        When the engine is operating, do not stand in direct line with the fan. Do not put your hands near the pulleys, belts or fan. Do not wear loose clothing. Failure to follow these instructions may result in possible serious or fatal injury.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Yes, for vehicles without Variable Cam Timing</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Yes, for vehicles with Variable Cam Timing</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CAMSHAFT POSITION SOLENOID CLEANING</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>With the scan tool perform the Exhaust Phaser 1 Cleaning function.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, erase DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Start the engine and allow it to idle. It maybe necessary to tests drive the vehicle and allow cam phasing to occur in order to reset the fault.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Did the DTC return?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>ENGINE OIL/ENGINE MECHANICAL</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The following items should be checked before continuing with this procedure. Failure to do so may lead to misdiagnosis.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">ENGINE MECHANICAL TOLERANCES OUT OF SPECIFICATION ENGINE OIL PRESSURE OUT OF SPECIFICATION LOW ENGINE OIL LEVEL INCORRECT ENGINE OIL FILTER (DOES NOT MEET OEM SPECIFICATIONS) DETERIORATED OR DIRTY OIL CONTAMINATED ENGINE OIL INCORRECT ENGINE OIL VISCOSITY AERATED ENGINE OIL</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>If any of the above conditions are found, repair as necessary.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any of the above conditions present?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the engine oiling or mechanical condition (i.e. improper oil drain back, oil sludge, damaged oil pump, pick-up tube or seal, etc.).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE CAM PHASER OPERATION</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           It may be necessary to drive the vehicle to allow the Cam Phaser to operate normally and then return to lock pin at idle. The purpose is to check for a phaser that may not be responding quick enough and causing this DTC. The phaser may not</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>With the scan tool, monitor and compare the Camshaft Position Sensor desired position value with the Camshaft Position Sensor actual position value with the engine running at idle. After comparing the two values at idle, check and compare the values at several different engine speeds. The best way to do this is to raise the engine speed quickly and hold steady for up to 30 seconds and monitor the actual and desired values. Then lower the engine speed and hold steady at a level above idle (off of lock pin) for up to 30 seconds and monitor the actual and desired values. Repeat this a few times up and down monitoring the values and then allow the engine to come back to idle and monitor the actual position to verify that the phaser is coming back to lock pin position.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:            The purpose of this step is to determine if the Cam Phaser or Oil Control Valve is binding or dragging due to a mechanical or oiling system issue. We are looking for the actual position to respond and quickly mirror the desired position, not necessarily for it to achieve the desired position.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the actual Cam Phaser position changing quickly and mirroring the desired Cam Phaser position?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Check for proper alignment of the timing chain and sprockets in accordance with the Service Information. Also check the Timing Chain Tensioner and Guide for faulty or broken components. Perform the appropriate repair in accordance with the Service Information. The &quot;Cam/Crank Relearn&quot; Procedure must be performed every time there has been a repair/replacement made to a CMP Sensor and/or CKP Sensor, flywheel, valvetrain, timing chain or gears. Failure to do so can cause a misfire fault to set. With the scan tool, perform the &quot;Cam/Crank Relearn&quot; Procedure.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>CHECK THE ENGINE OIL PRESSURE AND ENGINE MECHANICAL CONDITION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the engine off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Install an oil pressure gauge on the engine.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Start the engine and allow it to idle.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the engine oil pressure reading on the gauge.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the oil pressure reading steady and above the engine specifications?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the engine oiling or mechanical condition (i.e. low engine oil, incorrect engine oil viscosity, contaminated or deteriorating engine oil, improper oil drain back or blocked screen in cylinder head, oil sludge, damaged oil pump, pick-up tube or seal, incorrect engine oil filter (DOES NOT MEET OEM SPECIFICATIONS) etc.).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>CHECK THE CAMSHAFT OIL CONTROL VALVE OPERATION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Camshaft Oil Control Valve harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Remove the Camshaft Oil Control Valve.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Reconnect the Camshaft Oil Control Valve harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>With the scan tool, actuate the Camshaft Oil Control Valve on and off several times.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the Camshaft Oil Control Valve move freely during actuation?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 7</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Camshaft Oil Control Valve in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>CHECK THE ENGINE TIMING ALIGNMENT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for proper alignment of the timing chain and sprocket in accordance with the Service Information. Also check the Cam Sensor Target Wheel, Timing Chain Tensioner and Guide for faulty or broken components.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any issue found with the timing alignment or components?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the appropriate repair in accordance with the Service Information. The &quot;Cam/Crank Relearn&quot; Procedure must be performed every time there has been a repair/replacement made to a CMP Sensor and/or CKP Sensor, flywheel, valvetrain, timing chain or gears. Failure to do so can cause a misfire fault to set. With the scan tool, perform the &quot;Cam/Crank Relearn&quot; Procedure.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">The failure is caused by a lack of oil pressure in the Cylinder Head or a faulty Cam Phaser assembly. Perform the appropriate repair in accordance with the Service Information. The &quot;Cam/Crank Relearn&quot; Procedure must be performed every time there has been a repair/replacement made to a CMP Sensor and/or CKP Sensor, Flywheel, valvetrain, timing chain or gears. Failure to do so can cause a misfire fault to set. With the scan tool, perform the &quot;Cam/Crank Relearn&quot; Procedure.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0031 o2 sensor 1/1 heater circuit low\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0031</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 SENSOR 1/1 HEATER CIRCUIT LOW</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The primary objective of the Oxygen (O2) Sensor Heater Control is to attain the fastest possible temperature rise, and to control the steady state temperature of the O2 Sensor. A Positive Temperature Coefficient (PTC) element inside the O2 Sensor heats up as current passes through it. This allows the system to enter Closed Loop quickly and meet the OBD II requirements for continuous monitoring and to deliver accurate readings. This is accomplished by the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) sending a Pulse Width Modulated (PWM) current to the O2 Sensors heater element. The PWM duty cycle is calculated, in response to the temperature measurements. The PCM uses the collected information to continually adjust the PWM to achieve a desired steady state temperature target.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The heater temperature is obtained by measuring the heater resistance and calculating the heater temperature using the resistance versus temperature characteristic. In order to measure the heater temperature, the transistor that controls the heater is momentarily disabled and current is passed through a precision sense resistor connected in series with the heater element. The voltage drop across the sense resistor provides the relationship to current which equates to a temperature value. Once the temperature is acquired, the sense current is turned off and normal operation is restored. The actual heater temperature is monitored continuously and the heating rate is adjusted to prevent damaging the heating element.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The circuit fault diagnostics for O2 sensors are algorithm based. This means that conditions such as a short or an open does not necessarily set a fault the same as a traditional circuit fault. The PCM determines O2 Sensor circuit faults based on pre-programmed variables that are used in a calculation to determine the state of an O2 Sensor. This requires the integrity of all circuits to be verified in order to ensure that the cause of the fault is not overlooked.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Continuously with the O2 Sensor heater commanded on. Battery voltage between 10.5 and 15.75 volts. DTC P0032 is not active.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) detects that the O2 Sensor heater circuit resistance is below approximately 2.0 Ohms.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>O2 SENSOR 1/1 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO GROUND</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/1 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE O2 SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/1</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Start the engine and allow it idle.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK THE (K99) O2 SENSOR 1/1 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the O2 Sensor 1/1 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Check for continuity between ground and the (K99) O2 Sensor 1/1 Heater Control circuit at the Oxygen Sensor 1/1 harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between ground and the (K99) O2 Sensor 1/1 Heater Control circuit at the Oxygen Sensor 1/1 harness connector?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K99) O2 Sensor 1/1 Heater Control circuit for a short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K99) O2 SENSOR 1/1 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO THE (Z703) SENSOR GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for continuity between the (K99) O2 Sensor 1/1 Heater Control circuit and the (Z703) Sensor Ground circuit at the Oxygen Sensor 1/1 harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between the (K99) O2 Sensor 1/1 Heater Control circuit and the (Z703) Sensor Ground circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the short between the (K99) O2 Sensor 1/1 Heater Control circuit and the (Z703) Sensor Ground circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE PCM HEATER CONTROL DRIVER FOR PROPER OPERATION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Reconnect the PCM harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>With the scan tool, actuate the Oxygen Sensor 1/1 control to the maximum allowable percentage.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Using a 12-volt test light connected to ground, probe the (K99) O2 Sensor 1/1 Heater Control circuit at the Oxygen Sensor 1/1 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The test light should be illuminated. The brightness will be dependent on the actuation percentage selected.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>With the scan tool, actuate the Oxygen Sensor 1/1 control to the OFF (0%) position.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Using a 12-volt test light connected to ground, probe the (K99) O2 Sensor 1/1 Heater Control circuit at the Oxygen Sensor 1/1 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The test light should not be illuminated.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the test light illuminate with the actuator ON and not illuminate with the actuator OFF?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the O2 Sensor 1/1 in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to SENSOR, OXYGEN, REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>CHECK RELATED HARNESS CONNECTIONS</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Disconnect all PCM harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect all related in-line harness connections (if equipped).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the related component harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Inspect harness connectors, component connectors, and all male and female terminals for the following conditions: Proper connector installation. Damaged connector locks. Corrosion. Other signs of water intrusion. Weather seal damage (if equipped). Bent terminals. Overheating due to a poor connection (terminal may be discolored due to excessive current draw). Terminals that have been pushed back into the connector cavity. Check for spread terminals and verify proper terminal tension.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Repair any conditions that are found.</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Connect all PCM harness connectors. Be certain that all harness connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Connect all in-line harness connectors (if equipped). Be certain that all connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>Connect all related component harness connectors. Be certain that all connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">8</span><span>With the scan tool, erase DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">9</span><span>Test drive or operate the vehicle in accordance with the when monitored and set conditions.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">10</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Did the DTC return?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the poor connections.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0032 o2 sensor 1/1 heater circuit high\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0032</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 SENSOR 1/1 HEATER CIRCUIT HIGH</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">O2 Sensor Heater Operation: For a typical Switching (4 Wire) Oxygen (O2) Sensor output signal to function the sense element must be heated to operating temperature. A resistive heater element is incorporated within the sensor to allow for rapid heating of the sensor to reach operating temperature faster and closed loop fuel control. The O2 Sensor Heater resistance is directly proportional to the heater temperature, meaning that as heater temperature increases, the heater resistance increases. The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) makes a calculation to determine the heater resistance and temperature. The heater resistance is calculate by measuring the Battery voltage and current draw on the heater control circuit, Then the heater temperature is determined using the resistance versus temperature characteristic. The heater temperature is monitored continuously and the heating rate is adjusted using Pulse Width Modulation (PWM) to prevent damaging the heating element. If the PCM detects a fault in the O2 Sensor heater circuitry the internal PCM driver is disabled during the current ignition cycle,</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">O2 Sensor Output Operation: Normal range of the O2 Sensor output is a 0 to 1.0 volt Analog to Digital (A/D) signal when the sensor is in normal operating temperature range. The output voltage is generated by comparing the Oxygen content in the atmosphere, collected in a reference chamber to the Oxygen content in the exhaust stream collected in a comparison chamber. When the oxygen content is high (caused by a lean air/fuel mixture) the sensor produces a low voltage. When the oxygen content is low (caused by a rich air/fuel mixture) it produces a higher voltage. In some instances a negative offset output of up to -1.0 volt may be introduced if the sensor&#x27;s reference chamber is contaminated. To allow for the negative voltage to be read, each O2 Sensor Return circuit has a 2.5 volt bias added to shift the signal voltage to between 2.5 volts and 3.5 volts.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">O2 Sensor Diagnostics: The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) performs diagnostics on the heater control, signal and sensor return circuits. Because of how the O2 Sensor operates, the circuit fault diagnostics for O2 Sensors are algorithm based. This means an open or short in one of the O2 Sensor circuits can cause a fault to set against another of the O2 Sensor circuits. Understanding how to interpret the combination of DTCs and O2 Sensor data provided can aid in narrowing down where the fault is in the system.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">On vehicle/engine combinations that use only the traditional switching O2 Sensors, both of the Upstream and Downstream O2 Sensor Return circuits originate from a single source within the PCM. Because of this, a short anywhere in the wiring in any of the O2 Sensor Return circuits will affect all of the Upstream and Downstream O2 Sensors. On vehicle/engine combinations that have wide-band Upstream O2 Sensors and switching Downstream O2 Sensors, the Upstream and Downstream O2 Sensor Return circuits are separate within the PCM. In this case a short anywhere in the wiring in the Downstream O2 Sensor Return circuit(s) will only affect the Downstream O2 Sensor(s). The PCM performs individual diagnostics on the O2 Sensor Return circuit for each wide-band Upstream O2 Sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">In data display on the scan tool, filtering on O2 in the search function will bring up the following info:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">O2 Sensor signal voltage - the raw signal voltage should switch between 2.5 and 3.5 volts on a normally functioning sensor. O2 Sensor (0-1) signal voltage - this is the differential signal voltage which is calculated using the raw signal voltage minus the 2.5 volt bias on the return circuit of a normally functioning O2 Sensor. Heater driver operation - displays the heater duty cycle. Heater temperature - this is determined by the total heater circuit resistance. Closed loop fuel control state - displays as open/closed.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Using the Scan Tool for Diagnostics: An open circuit or direct short to voltage or ground will cause the PCM to disable the heater PWM control. The heater will remain disabled for the remainder of that ignition cycle, even if the condition is intermittent and heals itself during that ignition cycle. Any of these conditions should set an O2 Sensor Heater Low or High DTC. Since the heater directly affects the output of sensor signal, and the heater control is disabled, this will cause the affected O2 Sensor raw signal voltage to read high (usually stays above 4.0 volts) and can also eventually set a Signal Circuit High DTC. It is not unusual to have a Heater Control Circuit High or Low DTC and a Sensor Signal High DTC to be set together. Important Note: A small amount of resistance (3-4 Ohms) may not set a Heater Control Circuit DTC but will affect heater operation and cause the sensor signal high DTC to set. Monitoring the heater duty cycle and temperature for a sensor that is reading high on the raw signal voltage can help in diagnosing this issue. A heater circuit with 4.0 - 5.0 Ohms resistance can increase the heater temperature reading on the scan tool for the affected sensor by as much as 700 degrees when compared to the other Switching (4 wire) O2 Sensors. As little as 2.0 Ohms can increase the temperature reading approximately 250 degrees on the scan tool.</p><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE: When a normally operating Switching O2 Sensor is heated to operating temperature the typical duty cycle percentage will be between approximately 30 and 50 percent. The O2 Sensor Heater Diagnosis:                       typical heater temperature will range between approximately 1200°F and 1400°F on the scan tool. If there is an issue with the O2 Sensor heater or circuitry the PCM will disable the heater driver and the duty cycle will be 0 percent. Any issues in the heater circuitry, even a small amount of resistance, will cause the temperature reading to be noticeably different. Monitoring the scan tool and starting the engine when the O2 Sensor Signal is shorted to ground, and when the O2 Sensor is cold, it will show the raw signal voltage at 0 volts but the (0-1) differential voltage signal will read -2.5 volts and increase toward O2 Sensor Signal Low Diagnosis:                   0.0 volts as the sensor heats up. When the sensor is warm the raw signal voltage reading and the (0-1) differential voltage reading will both read near 0.0 volts. It will also pull the sensor return bias voltage low through the O2 Sensor. This will cause the raw voltage signals to switch between the 0 -1 volt range on the other O2 Sensors. This may also cause the O2 Sensor Reference</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Using the Scan Tool for Diagnostics: (Return) Voltage Circuit Low DTC to set. The O2 Sensor Signal High diagnostic can fail due to several conditions. As mentioned above, an open, short or any resistance in the heater circuitry can cause the signal voltage to remain high. Looking for abnormalities in the heater duty cycle or heater temperature of the affected sensor would indicate a heater control issue causing the signal circuit high fault. An open in a sensor O2 Sensor Signal High Diagnosis: return circuit will cause the raw signal voltage to read high, near 5.0 volts, and the (0-1) differential voltage reading to be near 2.5 volts for the affected sensor or sensors. An open in the signal circuit will have the same effect on the sensor. If both Upstream or Downstream O2 Sensors are affected, it could indicate an open in the return circuit that is before the splice in the harness.</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p1621 o2 sensor reference (return) circuit the o2 sensor reference circuit low/high diagnostic is used to monitor the o2 sensor return circuits. if any of the return\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P1621</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 Sensor Reference (Return) Circuit The O2 Sensor Reference Circuit Low/High diagnostic is used to monitor the O2 Sensor Return circuits. If any of the return</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Low Diagnosis:                             circuits are shorted to ground the raw signal voltage readings for all O2 Sensors will read between 0-1 volts instead of 2.5-3.5 volts. This will happen because the bias voltage on the return circuit is pulled low for all sensors. As mentioned in the O2 Sensor Signal Low Diagnosis above, a short to ground in one of the sensor signal circuits will also pull the sensor return low for all sensors. However, this will cause the voltage reading on the sensor that has the shorted signal to read 0.0 volts on both the raw signal voltage and (0-1) differential voltage reading when the sensor is warm.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p1622 o2 sensor reference (return) circuit a short to voltage in any of the sensor return circuits will cause the fault to set. the affected o2 sensors will read 5.0 volts on\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P1622</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 Sensor Reference (Return) Circuit A short to voltage in any of the sensor return circuits will cause the fault to set. The affected O2 Sensors will read 5.0 volts on</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">High Diagnosis:                            the raw signal voltage. The (0-1) differential voltage reading will be 0.0 volts because the signal circuit voltage and return circuit voltage are both 5.0 volts. A short to voltage on one of the O2 Sensor signal circuits will cause the same fault condition and scan tool readings when the sensor is warm. If the condition is present when the engine is started and the sensor is cold, the (0-1) differential voltage reading will start at 2.5 volts and decrease to 0.0 volts as the sensor warms up.</p></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Battery voltage between 10.5 and 15.75 volts. O2 Sensor Heater commanded on. The O2 Sensor Heater circuit low fault is not active.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">When the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) detects that the O2 Sensor 1/1 Heater circuit resistance is above approximately 35.0 Ohms.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL will illuminate. The PCM will disable the O2 Sensor Heater driver for the remainder of this ignition cycle.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>O2 SENSOR 1/1 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/1 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>O2 SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>OXYGEN SENSOR 1/1</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR ANY SERVICE BULLETINS OR PCM SOFTWARE UPDATES</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for any applicable Service Bulletins or Flash updates related to the DTC.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Are there any applicable Service Bulletins or Flash updates?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the applicable repair.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>READ AND RECORD DTCS AND ENVIRONMENTAL DATA - ERASE DTCS AND CHECK FOR THE DTC TO RETURN</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs in all Electronic Control Units (ECUs) and record on the repair order.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>For future reference, with the scan tool, run and save a vehicle Scan Report and all related recorded data.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>With the scan tool, erase all DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Turn the ignition off for a minimum of 10.0 seconds.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Using the When Monitored and Set Conditions above and recorded data, operate the vehicle in the conditions that set the DTC.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Did the DTC return?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE O2 SENSOR (Z703) GROUND CIRCUIT FOR HIGH RESISTANCE BY LOAD TESTING THE CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          Read the CIRCUIT LOAD TESTING PROCEDURE for information on building a simple load test tool and for additional load testing</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">information and alternative methods of load testing or voltage drop testing a circuit. Refer to STANDARD PROCEDURE .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the O2 Sensor 1/1 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not load test any circuits with components still connected to the circuit.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Load test the (Z703) Ground circuit at the O2 Sensor 1/1 harness connector. Note: refer to the example illustration.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The bulb on the load test tool should be illuminated and bright if there is no resistance in the circuitry. Compare the brightness of the bulb in the load test tool to that of a direct connection across Battery.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Is the load test bulb illuminated and bright?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (Z703) Ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE O2 SENSOR 1/1 (K79) HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO ANOTHER CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Check for continuity between the O2 Sensor 1/1 (K79) Heater Control circuit and all other circuits at the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Is there continuity between the O2 Sensor 1/1 (K79) Heater Control circuit and any other circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the O2 Sensor 1/1 (K79) Heater Control circuit for a short to the circuit that showed continuity.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>ISOLATE AND LOAD TEST THE O2 SENSOR 1/1 (K79) HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT TO CHECK FOR HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:       Do not load test any circuits with components still connected to the circuit.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic) to the appropriate PCM connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           IMPORTANT - The GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor can add up to 1.5 Ohms of resistance to the circuit.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Load test the O2 Sensor 1/1 (K79) Heater Control circuit. Note: refer to the example illustration.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The bulb on the load test tool should be illuminated and bright if there is no resistance in the circuit. Compare the brightness of the bulb in the load test tool to that of a direct connection across Battery.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the load test bulb illuminated and bright?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the O2 Sensor 1/1 (K79) Heater Control circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>CHECK THE O2 SENSOR 1/1 HEATER CONTROL DRIVER FOR PROPER OPERATION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, actuate the O2 Sensor 1/1 control to the maximum allowable percentage.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Using a 12-volt test light connected to ground, check the O2 Sensor 1/1 (K79) Heater Control circuit at the O2 Sensor 1/1 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The test light should be illuminated. The brightness will be dependent on the actuation percentage selected.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>With the scan tool, actuate the O2 Sensor 1/1 control to the OFF (0%) position.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The test light should not be illuminated.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the test light illuminate with the actuator ON and not illuminated with the actuator OFF (0%)?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the O2 Sensor 1/1 in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to SENSOR, OXYGEN, REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 7</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>CHECK RELATED PCM AND COMPONENT CONNECTIONS</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that apply.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect all PCM harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect all related in-line harness connections (if equipped).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Disconnect the related component harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Inspect harness connectors, component connectors, and all male and female terminals for the following conditions: Proper connector installation. Damaged connector locks. Corrosion. Other signs of water intrusion. Weather seal damage (if equipped). Bent terminals.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Overheating due to a poor connection (terminal may be discolored due to excessive current draw). Terminals that have been pushed back into the connector cavity. Check for spread terminals and verify proper terminal tension.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Repair any conditions that are found.</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Reconnect all PCM harness connectors. Be certain that all harness connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>Reconnect all in-line harness connectors (if equipped). Be certain that all connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">8</span><span>Reconnect all related component harness connectors. Be certain that all connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">9</span><span>With the scan tool, erase DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">10</span><span>Test drive or operate the vehicle in accordance with the when monitored and set conditions.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">11</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Did the DTC return?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">The wiring or poor connection problem has been repaired.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0037 o2 sensor 1/2 heater circuit low\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0037</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 SENSOR 1/2 HEATER CIRCUIT LOW</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The primary objective of the Oxygen (O2) Sensor Heater Control is to attain the fastest possible temperature rise, and to control the steady state temperature of the O2 Sensor. A Positive Temperature Coefficient (PTC) element inside the O2 Sensor heats up as current passes through it. This allows the system to enter Closed Loop quickly and meet the OBD II requirements for continuous monitoring and to deliver accurate readings. This is accomplished by the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) sending a Pulse Width Modulated (PWM) current to the O2 Sensors heater element. The PWM duty cycle is calculated, in response to the temperature measurements. The PCM uses the collected information to continually adjust the PWM to achieve a desired steady state temperature target.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The heater temperature is obtained by measuring the heater resistance and calculating the heater temperature using the resistance versus temperature characteristic. In order to measure the heater temperature, the transistor that controls the heater is momentarily disabled and current is passed through a precision sense resistor connected in series with the heater element. The voltage drop across the sense resistor provides the relationship to current which equates to a temperature value. Once the temperature is acquired, the sense current is turned off and normal operation is restored. The actual heater temperature is monitored continuously and the heating rate is adjusted to prevent damaging the heating element.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The circuit fault diagnostics for O2 sensors are algorithm based. This means that conditions such as a short or an open does not necessarily set a fault the same as a traditional circuit fault. The PCM determines O2 Sensor circuit faults based on pre-programmed variables that are used in a calculation to determine the state of an O2 Sensor. This requires the integrity of all circuits to be verified in order to ensure that the cause of the fault is not overlooked.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the engine running. No active Downstream O2 Sensor Heater Circuit High DTCs. Battery voltage between 10.5 and 15.75 volts. During O2 Sensor heater operation. The ASD Relay is powered on.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The O2 Sensor heater element resistance is equal to or below 2.0 Ohms for more than approximately two to three seconds.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>O2 SENSOR 1/2 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO GROUND</li><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE O2 SENSOR 1/2 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/2</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Start the engine and allow it to idle for at least 60 seconds.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">WARNING:         When the engine is operating, do not stand in direct line with the fan. Do not put your hands near the pulleys, belts or fan. Do not wear loose clothing. Failure to follow these instructions may result in possible serious or fatal injury.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With a scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK THE O2 SENSOR 1/2 HEATER ELEMENT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:            Allow the O2 Sensor to cool down to room temperature.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the O2 Sensor 1/2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Measure the resistance across the O2 Sensor 1/2 Heater element, between the O2 Sensor 1/2 Heater Control terminal and the O2 Sensor 1/2 Heater ground terminal at the component.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:            O2 Heater Element resistance values should be measured at 21.1°C (70°F). The resistance value will vary with different temperature values.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the O2 Sensor Heater Element resistance between 2.0 and 30.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the O2 Sensor 1/2 and Powertrain Control Module connectors. If OK, replace the O2 Sensor. Refer to SENSOR, OXYGEN, REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K299) O2 SENSOR 1/2 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Check for continuity between ground and the (K299) O2 Sensor 1/2 Heater Control circuit at the O2 Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between ground and the (K299) O2 Sensor 1/2 Heater Control circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K299) O2 Sensor 1/2 Heater Control circuit for a short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K299) O2 SENSOR 1/2 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:       Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K299) O2 Sensor 1/2 Heater Control circuit between the O2 Sensor 1/2 harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 0.5 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the excessive resistance in the (K299) O2 Sensor 1/2 Heater Control circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Oxygen Sensor 1/2 and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals. Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the O2 Sensor 1/2 and Powertrain Control Module connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were there any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0038 o2 sensor 1/2 heater circuit high\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0038</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 SENSOR 1/2 HEATER CIRCUIT HIGH</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">O2 Sensor Heater Operation: For a typical Switching (4 Wire) Oxygen (O2) Sensor output signal to function the sense element must be heated to operating temperature. A resistive heater element is incorporated within the sensor to allow for rapid heating of the sensor to reach operating temperature faster and closed loop fuel control. The O2 Sensor Heater resistance is directly proportional to the heater temperature, meaning that as heater temperature increases, the heater resistance increases. The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) makes a calculation to determine the heater resistance and temperature. The heater resistance is calculate by measuring the Battery voltage and current draw on the heater control circuit, Then the heater temperature is determined using the resistance versus temperature characteristic. The heater temperature is monitored continuously and the heating rate is adjusted using Pulse Width Modulation (PWM) to prevent damaging the heating element. If the PCM detects a fault in the O2 Sensor heater circuitry the internal PCM driver is disabled during the current ignition cycle,</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">O2 Sensor Output Operation: Normal range of the O2 Sensor output is a 0 to 1.0 volt Analog to Digital (A/D) signal when the sensor is in normal operating temperature range. The output voltage is generated by comparing the Oxygen content in the atmosphere, collected in a reference chamber to the Oxygen content in the exhaust stream collected in a comparison chamber. When the oxygen content is high (caused by a lean air/fuel mixture) the sensor produces a low voltage. When the oxygen content is low (caused by a rich air/fuel mixture) it produces a higher voltage. In some instances a negative offset output of up to -1.0 volt may be introduced if the sensor&#x27;s reference chamber is contaminated. To allow for the negative voltage to be read, each O2 Sensor Return circuit has a 2.5 volt bias added to shift the signal voltage to between 2.5 volts and 3.5 volts.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">O2 Sensor Diagnostics: The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) performs diagnostics on the heater control, signal and sensor return circuits. Because of how the O2 Sensor operates, the circuit fault diagnostics for O2 Sensors are algorithm based. This means an open or short in one of the O2 Sensor circuits can cause a fault to set against another of the O2 Sensor circuits. Understanding how to interpret the combination of DTCs and O2 Sensor data provided can aid in narrowing down where the fault is in the system.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">On vehicle/engine combinations that use only the traditional switching O2 Sensors, both of the Upstream and Downstream O2 Sensor Return circuits originate from a single source within the PCM. Because of this, a short anywhere in the wiring in any of the O2 Sensor Return circuits will affect all of the Upstream and Downstream O2 Sensors. On vehicle/engine combinations that have wide-band Upstream O2 Sensors and switching Downstream O2 Sensors, the Upstream and Downstream O2 Sensor Return circuits are separate within the PCM. In this case a short anywhere in the wiring in the Downstream O2 Sensor Return circuit(s) will only affect the Downstream O2 Sensor(s). The PCM performs individual diagnostics on the O2 Sensor Return circuit for each wide-band Upstream O2 Sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">In data display on the scan tool, filtering on O2 in the search function will bring up the following info:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">O2 Sensor signal voltage - the raw signal voltage should switch between 2.5 and 3.5 volts on a normally functioning sensor. O2 Sensor (0-1) signal voltage - this is the differential signal voltage which is calculated using the raw signal voltage minus the 2.5 volt bias on the return circuit of a normally functioning O2 Sensor. Heater driver operation - displays the heater duty cycle. Heater temperature - this is determined by the total heater circuit resistance. Closed loop fuel control state - displays as open/closed.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Using the Scan Tool for Diagnostics: An open circuit or direct short to voltage or ground will cause the PCM to disable the heater PWM control. The heater will remain disabled for the remainder of that ignition cycle, even if the condition is intermittent and heals itself during that ignition cycle. Any of these conditions should set an O2 Sensor Heater Low or High DTC. Since the heater directly affects the output of sensor signal, and the heater control is disabled, this will cause the affected O2 Sensor raw signal voltage to read high (usually stays above 4.0 volts) and can also eventually set a Signal Circuit High DTC. It is not unusual to have a Heater Control Circuit High or Low DTC and a Sensor Signal High DTC to be set together. Important Note: A small amount of resistance (3-4 Ohms) may not set a Heater Control Circuit DTC but will affect heater operation and cause the sensor signal high DTC to set. Monitoring the heater duty cycle and temperature for a sensor that is reading high on the raw signal voltage can help in diagnosing this issue. A heater circuit with 4.0 - 5.0 Ohms resistance can increase the heater temperature reading on the scan tool for the affected sensor by as much as 700 degrees when compared to the other Switching (4 wire) O2 Sensors. As little as 2.0 Ohms can increase the temperature reading approximately 250 degrees on the scan tool.</p><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE: When a normally operating Switching O2 Sensor is heated to operating temperature the typical duty cycle percentage will be between approximately 30 and 50 percent. The O2 Sensor Heater Diagnosis:                       typical heater temperature will range between approximately 1200°F and 1400°F on the scan tool. If there is an issue with the O2 Sensor heater or circuitry the PCM will disable the heater driver and the duty cycle will be 0 percent. Any issues in the heater circuitry, even a small amount of resistance, will cause the temperature reading to be noticeably different. Monitoring the scan tool and starting the engine when the O2 Sensor Signal is shorted to ground, and when the O2 Sensor is cold, it will show the raw signal voltage at 0 volts but the (0-1) differential voltage signal will read -2.5 volts and increase toward O2 Sensor Signal Low Diagnosis:                   0.0 volts as the sensor heats up. When the sensor is warm the raw signal voltage reading and the (0-1) differential voltage reading will both read near 0.0 volts. It will also pull the sensor return bias voltage low through the O2 Sensor. This will cause the raw voltage signals to switch between the 0 -1 volt range on the other O2 Sensors. This may also cause the O2 Sensor Reference</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Using the Scan Tool for Diagnostics: (Return) Voltage Circuit Low DTC to set. The O2 Sensor Signal High diagnostic can fail due to several conditions. As mentioned above, an open, short or any resistance in the heater circuitry can cause the signal voltage to remain high. Looking for abnormalities in the heater duty cycle or heater temperature of the affected sensor would indicate a heater control issue causing the signal circuit high fault. An open in a sensor O2 Sensor Signal High Diagnosis: return circuit will cause the raw signal voltage to read high, near 5.0 volts, and the (0-1) differential voltage reading to be near 2.5 volts for the affected sensor or sensors. An open in the signal circuit will have the same effect on the sensor. If both Upstream or Downstream O2 Sensors are affected, it could indicate an open in the return circuit that is before the splice in the harness.</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p1621 o2 sensor reference (return) circuit the o2 sensor reference circuit low/high diagnostic is used to monitor the o2 sensor return circuits. if any of the return\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P1621</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 Sensor Reference (Return) Circuit The O2 Sensor Reference Circuit Low/High diagnostic is used to monitor the O2 Sensor Return circuits. If any of the return</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Low Diagnosis:                             circuits are shorted to ground the raw signal voltage readings for all O2 Sensors will read between 0-1 volts instead of 2.5-3.5 volts. This will happen because the bias voltage on the return circuit is pulled low for all sensors. As mentioned in the O2 Sensor Signal Low Diagnosis above, a short to ground in one of the sensor signal circuits will also pull the sensor return low for all sensors. However, this will cause the voltage reading on the sensor that has the shorted signal to read 0.0 volts on both the raw signal voltage and (0-1) differential voltage reading when the sensor is warm.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p1622 o2 sensor reference (return) circuit a short to voltage in any of the sensor return circuits will cause the fault to set. the affected o2 sensors will read 5.0 volts on\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P1622</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 Sensor Reference (Return) Circuit A short to voltage in any of the sensor return circuits will cause the fault to set. The affected O2 Sensors will read 5.0 volts on</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">High Diagnosis:                            the raw signal voltage. The (0-1) differential voltage reading will be 0.0 volts because the signal circuit voltage and return circuit voltage are both 5.0 volts. A short to voltage on one of the O2 Sensor signal circuits will cause the same fault condition and scan tool readings when the sensor is warm. If the condition is present when the engine is started and the sensor is cold, the (0-1) differential voltage reading will start at 2.5 volts and decrease to 0.0 volts as the sensor warms up.</p></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Battery voltage between 10.5 and 15.75 volts. O2 Sensor Heater commanded on. The O2 Sensor Heater circuit low fault is not active.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">When the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) detects that the O2 Sensor 1/2 Heater circuit resistance is above approximately 35.0 Ohms.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL will illuminate. The PCM will disable the O2 Sensor Heater driver for the remainder of this ignition cycle.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>O2 SENSOR 1/2 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/2 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>O2 SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>OXYGEN SENSOR 1/2</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR ANY SERVICE BULLETINS OR PCM SOFTWARE UPDATES</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for any applicable Service Bulletins or Flash updates related to the DTC.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Are there any applicable Service Bulletins or Flash updates?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the applicable repair.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>READ AND RECORD DTCS AND ENVIRONMENTAL DATA - ERASE DTCS AND CHECK FOR THE DTC TO RETURN</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs in all Electronic Control Units (ECUs) and record on the repair order.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>For future reference, with the scan tool, run and save a vehicle Scan Report and all related recorded data.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>With the scan tool, erase all DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Turn the ignition off for a minimum of 10.0 seconds.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Using the When Monitored and Set Conditions above and recorded data, operate the vehicle in the conditions that set the DTC.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Did the DTC return?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE O2 SENSOR (Z703) GROUND CIRCUIT FOR HIGH RESISTANCE BY LOAD TESTING THE CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          Read the CIRCUIT LOAD TESTING PROCEDURE for information on building a simple load test tool and for additional load testing</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">information and alternative methods of load testing or voltage drop testing a circuit. Refer to STANDARD PROCEDURE .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the O2 Sensor 1/2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not load test any circuits with components still connected to the circuit.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Load test the (Z703) Ground circuit at the O2 Sensor 1/2 harness connector. Note: refer to the example illustration.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The bulb on the load test tool should be illuminated and bright if there is no resistance in the circuitry. Compare the brightness of the bulb in the load test tool to that of a direct connection across Battery.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Is the load test bulb illuminated and bright?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (Z703) Ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE O2 SENSOR 1/2 (K299) HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO ANOTHER CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Check for continuity between the O2 Sensor 1/2 (K299) Heater Control circuit and all other circuits at the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Is there continuity between the O2 Sensor 1/2 (K299) Heater Control circuit and any other circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the O2 Sensor 1/2 (K299) Heater Control circuit for a short to the circuit that showed continuity.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>ISOLATE AND LOAD TEST THE O2 SENSOR 1/2 (K299) HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT TO CHECK FOR HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:       Do not load test any circuits with components still connected to the circuit.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic) to the appropriate PCM connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           IMPORTANT - The GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor can add up to 1.5 Ohms of resistance to the circuit.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Load test the O2 Sensor 1/2 (K299) Heater Control circuit. Note: refer to the example illustration.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The bulb on the load test tool should be illuminated and bright if there is no resistance in the circuit. Compare the brightness of the bulb in the load test tool to that of a direct connection across Battery.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the load test bulb illuminated and bright?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the O2 Sensor 1/2 (K299) Heater Control circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>CHECK THE O2 SENSOR 1/2 HEATER CONTROL DRIVER FOR PROPER OPERATION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, actuate the O2 Sensor 1/2 control to the maximum allowable percentage.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Using a 12-volt test light connected to ground, check the O2 Sensor 1/2 (K299) Heater Control circuit at the O2 Sensor 1/2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The test light should be illuminated. The brightness will be dependent on the actuation percentage selected.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>With the scan tool, actuate the O2 Sensor 1/2 control to the OFF (0%) position.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The test light should not be illuminated.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the test light illuminate with the actuator ON and not illuminated with the actuator OFF (0%)?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the O2 Sensor 1/2 in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to SENSOR, OXYGEN, REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 7</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>CHECK RELATED PCM AND COMPONENT CONNECTIONS</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that apply.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect all PCM harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect all related in-line harness connections (if equipped).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Disconnect the related component harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Inspect harness connectors, component connectors, and all male and female terminals for the following conditions: Proper connector installation. Damaged connector locks. Corrosion. Other signs of water intrusion. Weather seal damage (if equipped). Bent terminals.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Overheating due to a poor connection (terminal may be discolored due to excessive current draw). Terminals that have been pushed back into the connector cavity. Check for spread terminals and verify proper terminal tension.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Repair any conditions that are found.</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Reconnect all PCM harness connectors. Be certain that all harness connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>Reconnect all in-line harness connectors (if equipped). Be certain that all connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">8</span><span>Reconnect all related component harness connectors. Be certain that all connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">9</span><span>With the scan tool, erase DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">10</span><span>Test drive or operate the vehicle in accordance with the when monitored and set conditions.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">11</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Did the DTC return?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">The wiring or poor connection problem has been repaired.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0051 o2 sensor 2/1 heater circuit low\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0051</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 SENSOR 2/1 HEATER CIRCUIT LOW</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The primary objective of the Oxygen (O2) Sensor Heater Control is to attain the fastest possible temperature rise, and to control the steady state temperature of the O2 Sensor. A Positive Temperature Coefficient (PTC) element inside the O2 Sensor heats up as current passes through it. This allows the system to enter Closed Loop quickly and meet the OBD II requirements for continuous monitoring and to deliver accurate readings. This is accomplished by the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) sending a Pulse Width Modulated (PWM) current to the O2 Sensors heater element. The PWM duty cycle is calculated, in response to the temperature measurements. The PCM uses the collected information to continually adjust the PWM to achieve a desired steady state temperature target.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The heater temperature is obtained by measuring the heater resistance and calculating the heater temperature using the resistance versus temperature characteristic. In order to measure the heater temperature, the transistor that controls the heater is momentarily disabled and current is passed through a precision sense resistor connected in series with the heater element. The voltage drop across the sense resistor provides the relationship to current which equates to a temperature value. Once the temperature is acquired, the sense current is turned off and normal operation is restored. The actual heater temperature is monitored continuously and the heating rate is adjusted to prevent damaging the heating element.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The circuit fault diagnostics for O2 sensors are algorithm based. This means that conditions such as a short or an open does not necessarily set a fault the same as a traditional circuit fault. The PCM determines O2 Sensor circuit faults based on pre-programmed variables that are used in a calculation to determine the state of an O2 Sensor. This requires the integrity of all circuits to be verified in order to ensure that the cause of the fault is not overlooked.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Continuously with the O2 Sensor heater commanded on. Battery voltage between 10.5 and 15.75 volts. DTC P0052 is not active.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) detects that the O2 Sensor heater circuit resistance is below approximately 2.0 Ohms.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>O2 SENSOR 2/1 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO GROUND</li><li>O2 SENSOR 2/1 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE O2 SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>O2 SENSOR 2/1</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Start the engine and allow it to reach normal operating temperature.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK THE (K199) O2 SENSOR 2/1 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the O2 Sensor 2/1 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Check for continuity between ground and the (K199) O2 Sensor 2/1 Heater Control circuit at the O2 Sensor 2/1 harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between ground and the (K199) O2 Sensor 2/1 Heater Control circuit at the O2 Sensor 2/1 harness connector?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K199) O2 Sensor 2/1 Heater Control circuit for a short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK FOR THE (K199) O2 SENSOR 2/1 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE O2 SENSOR GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for continuity between the (K199) O2 Sensor 2/1 Heater Control circuit and the O2 Sensor Ground circuit at the O2 Sensor 2/1 harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between the (K199) O2 Sensor 2/1 Heater Control circuit and the O2 Sensor Ground circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K199) O2 Sensor 2/1 Heater Control circuit for a short to O2 Sensor Ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE PCM HEATER CONTROL DRIVER FOR PROPER OPERATION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Reconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>With the scan tool, actuate the O2 Sensor 2/1 control to the maximum allowable percentage.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Using a 12-volt test light connected to ground, probe the (K199) O2 Sensor 2/1 Heater Control circuit at the O2 Sensor 2/1 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The test light should be illuminated. The brightness will be dependent on the actuation percentage selected.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>With the scan tool, actuate the O2 Sensor 2/1 control to the OFF (0%) position.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Using a 12-volt test light connected to ground, probe the (K199) O2 Sensor 2/1 Heater Control circuit at the O2 Sensor 2/1 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The test light should not be illuminated.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the test light illuminate with the actuator ON and not illuminate with the actuator OFF?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the O2 Sensor 2/1 in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to SENSOR, OXYGEN, REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>CHECK RELATED HARNESS CONNECTIONS</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Disconnect all PCM harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect all related in-line harness connections (if equipped).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the related component harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Inspect harness connectors, component connectors, and all male and female terminals for the following conditions: Proper connector installation. Damaged connector locks. Corrosion. Other signs of water intrusion. Weather seal damage (if equipped). Bent terminals. Overheating due to a poor connection (terminal may be discolored due to excessive current draw). Terminals that have been pushed back into the connector cavity. Check for spread terminals and verify proper terminal tension.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Repair any conditions that are found.</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Connect all PCM harness connectors. Be certain that all harness connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Connect all in-line harness connectors (if equipped). Be certain that all connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>Connect all related component harness connectors. Be certain that all connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">8</span><span>With the scan tool, erase DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">9</span><span>Test drive or operate the vehicle in accordance with the when monitored and set conditions.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">10</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Did the DTC return?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the poor connections.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0052 o2 sensor 2/1 heater circuit high\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0052</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 SENSOR 2/1 HEATER CIRCUIT HIGH</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">O2 Sensor Heater Operation: For a typical Switching (4 Wire) Oxygen (O2) Sensor output signal to function the sense element must be heated to operating temperature. A resistive heater element is incorporated within the sensor to allow for rapid heating of the sensor to reach operating temperature faster and closed loop fuel control. The O2 Sensor Heater resistance is directly proportional to the heater temperature, meaning that as heater temperature increases, the heater resistance increases. The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) makes a calculation to determine the heater resistance and temperature. The heater resistance is calculate by measuring the Battery voltage and current draw on the heater control circuit, Then the heater temperature is determined using the resistance versus temperature characteristic. The heater temperature is monitored continuously and the heating rate is adjusted using Pulse Width Modulation (PWM) to prevent damaging the heating element. If the PCM detects a fault in the O2 Sensor heater circuitry the internal PCM driver is disabled during the current ignition cycle,</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">O2 Sensor Output Operation: Normal range of the O2 Sensor output is a 0 to 1.0 volt Analog to Digital (A/D) signal when the sensor is in normal operating temperature range. The output voltage is generated by comparing the Oxygen content in the atmosphere, collected in a reference chamber to the Oxygen content in the exhaust stream collected in a comparison chamber. When the oxygen content is high (caused by a lean air/fuel mixture) the sensor produces a low voltage. When the oxygen content is low (caused by a rich air/fuel mixture) it produces a higher voltage. In some instances a negative offset output of up to -1.0 volt may be introduced if the sensor&#x27;s reference chamber is contaminated. To allow for the negative voltage to be read, each O2 Sensor Return circuit has a 2.5 volt bias added to shift the signal voltage to between 2.5 volts and 3.5 volts.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">O2 Sensor Diagnostics: The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) performs diagnostics on the heater control, signal and sensor return circuits. Because of how the O2 Sensor operates, the circuit fault diagnostics for O2 Sensors are algorithm based. This means an open or short in one of the O2 Sensor circuits can cause a fault to set against another of the O2 Sensor circuits. Understanding how to interpret the combination of DTCs and O2 Sensor data provided can aid in narrowing down where the fault is in the system.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">On vehicle/engine combinations that use only the traditional switching O2 Sensors, both of the Upstream and Downstream O2 Sensor Return circuits originate from a single source within the PCM. Because of this, a short anywhere in the wiring in any of the O2 Sensor Return circuits will affect all of the Upstream and Downstream O2 Sensors. On vehicle/engine combinations that have wide-band Upstream O2 Sensors and switching Downstream O2 Sensors, the Upstream and Downstream O2 Sensor Return circuits are separate within the PCM. In this case a short anywhere in the wiring in the Downstream O2 Sensor Return circuit(s) will only affect the Downstream O2 Sensor(s). The PCM performs individual diagnostics on the O2 Sensor Return circuit for each wide-band Upstream O2 Sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">In data display on the scan tool, filtering on O2 in the search function will bring up the following info:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">O2 Sensor signal voltage - the raw signal voltage should switch between 2.5 and 3.5 volts on a normally functioning sensor. O2 Sensor (0-1) signal voltage - this is the differential signal voltage which is calculated using the raw signal voltage minus the 2.5 volt bias on the return circuit of a normally functioning O2 Sensor. Heater driver operation - displays the heater duty cycle. Heater temperature - this is determined by the total heater circuit resistance. Closed loop fuel control state - displays as open/closed.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Using the Scan Tool for Diagnostics: An open circuit or direct short to voltage or ground will cause the PCM to disable the heater PWM control. The heater will remain disabled for the remainder of that ignition cycle, even if the condition is intermittent and heals itself during that ignition cycle. Any of these conditions should set an O2 Sensor Heater Low or High DTC. Since the heater directly affects the output of sensor signal, and the heater control is disabled, this will cause the affected O2 Sensor raw signal voltage to read high (usually stays above 4.0 volts) and can also eventually set a Signal Circuit High DTC. It is not unusual to have a Heater Control Circuit High or Low DTC and a Sensor Signal High DTC to be set together. Important Note: A small amount of resistance (3-4 Ohms) may not set a Heater Control Circuit DTC but will affect heater operation and cause the sensor signal high DTC to set. Monitoring the heater duty cycle and temperature for a sensor that is reading high on the raw signal voltage can help in diagnosing this issue. A heater circuit with 4.0 - 5.0 Ohms resistance can increase the heater temperature reading on the scan tool for the affected sensor by as much as 700 degrees when compared to the other Switching (4 wire) O2 Sensors. As little as 2.0 Ohms can increase the temperature reading approximately 250 degrees on the scan tool.</p><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE: When a normally operating Switching O2 Sensor is heated to operating temperature the typical duty cycle percentage will be between approximately 30 and 50 percent. The O2 Sensor Heater Diagnosis:                       typical heater temperature will range between approximately 1200°F and 1400°F on the scan tool. If there is an issue with the O2 Sensor heater or circuitry the PCM will disable the heater driver and the duty cycle will be 0 percent. Any issues in the heater circuitry, even a small amount of resistance, will cause the temperature reading to be noticeably different. Monitoring the scan tool and starting the engine when the O2 Sensor Signal is shorted to ground, and when the O2 Sensor is cold, it will show the raw signal voltage at 0 volts but the (0-1) differential voltage signal will read -2.5 volts and increase toward O2 Sensor Signal Low Diagnosis:                   0.0 volts as the sensor heats up. When the sensor is warm the raw signal voltage reading and the (0-1) differential voltage reading will both read near 0.0 volts. It will also pull the sensor return bias voltage low through the O2 Sensor. This will cause the raw voltage signals to switch between the 0 -1 volt range on the other O2 Sensors. This may also cause the O2 Sensor Reference</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Using the Scan Tool for Diagnostics: (Return) Voltage Circuit Low DTC to set. The O2 Sensor Signal High diagnostic can fail due to several conditions. As mentioned above, an open, short or any resistance in the heater circuitry can cause the signal voltage to remain high. Looking for abnormalities in the heater duty cycle or heater temperature of the affected sensor would indicate a heater control issue causing the signal circuit high fault. An open in a sensor O2 Sensor Signal High Diagnosis: return circuit will cause the raw signal voltage to read high, near 5.0 volts, and the (0-1) differential voltage reading to be near 2.5 volts for the affected sensor or sensors. An open in the signal circuit will have the same effect on the sensor. If both Upstream or Downstream O2 Sensors are affected, it could indicate an open in the return circuit that is before the splice in the harness.</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p1621 o2 sensor reference (return) circuit the o2 sensor reference circuit low/high diagnostic is used to monitor the o2 sensor return circuits. if any of the return\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P1621</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 Sensor Reference (Return) Circuit The O2 Sensor Reference Circuit Low/High diagnostic is used to monitor the O2 Sensor Return circuits. If any of the return</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Low Diagnosis:                             circuits are shorted to ground the raw signal voltage readings for all O2 Sensors will read between 0-1 volts instead of 2.5-3.5 volts. This will happen because the bias voltage on the return circuit is pulled low for all sensors. As mentioned in the O2 Sensor Signal Low Diagnosis above, a short to ground in one of the sensor signal circuits will also pull the sensor return low for all sensors. However, this will cause the voltage reading on the sensor that has the shorted signal to read 0.0 volts on both the raw signal voltage and (0-1) differential voltage reading when the sensor is warm.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p1622 o2 sensor reference (return) circuit a short to voltage in any of the sensor return circuits will cause the fault to set. the affected o2 sensors will read 5.0 volts on\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P1622</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 Sensor Reference (Return) Circuit A short to voltage in any of the sensor return circuits will cause the fault to set. The affected O2 Sensors will read 5.0 volts on</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">High Diagnosis:                            the raw signal voltage. The (0-1) differential voltage reading will be 0.0 volts because the signal circuit voltage and return circuit voltage are both 5.0 volts. A short to voltage on one of the O2 Sensor signal circuits will cause the same fault condition and scan tool readings when the sensor is warm. If the condition is present when the engine is started and the sensor is cold, the (0-1) differential voltage reading will start at 2.5 volts and decrease to 0.0 volts as the sensor warms up.</p></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Battery voltage between 10.5 and 15.75 volts. O2 Sensor Heater commanded on. The O2 Sensor Heater circuit low fault is not active.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">When the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) detects that the O2 Sensor 1/2 Heater circuit resistance is above approximately 35.0 Ohms.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL will illuminate. The PCM will disable the O2 Sensor Heater driver for the remainder of this ignition cycle.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>O2 SENSOR 1/2 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/2 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>O2 SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>OXYGEN SENSOR 1/2</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR ANY SERVICE BULLETINS OR PCM SOFTWARE UPDATES</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for any applicable Service Bulletins or Flash updates related to the DTC.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Are there any applicable Service Bulletins or Flash updates?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the applicable repair.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>READ AND RECORD DTCS AND ENVIRONMENTAL DATA - ERASE DTCS AND CHECK FOR THE DTC TO RETURN</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs in all Electronic Control Units (ECUs) and record on the repair order.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>For future reference, with the scan tool, run and save a vehicle Scan Report and all related recorded data.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>With the scan tool, erase all DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Turn the ignition off for a minimum of 10.0 seconds.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Using the When Monitored and Set Conditions above and recorded data, operate the vehicle in the conditions that set the DTC.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Did the DTC return?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE O2 SENSOR (Z703) GROUND CIRCUIT FOR HIGH RESISTANCE BY LOAD TESTING THE CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          Read the CIRCUIT LOAD TESTING PROCEDURE for information on building a simple load test tool and for additional load testing</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">information and alternative methods of load testing or voltage drop testing a circuit. Refer to STANDARD PROCEDURE .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the O2 Sensor 2/1 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not load test any circuits with components still connected to the circuit.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Load test the (Z703) Ground circuit at the O2 Sensor 2/1 harness connector. Note: refer to the example illustration.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The bulb on the load test tool should be illuminated and bright if there is no resistance in the circuitry. Compare the brightness of the bulb in the load test tool to that of a direct connection across Battery.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Is the load test bulb illuminated and bright?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (Z703) Ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE O2 SENSOR 2/1 (K299) HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO ANOTHER CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Check for continuity between the O2 Sensor 2/1 (K299) Heater Control circuit and all other circuits at the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Is there continuity between the O2 Sensor 2/1 (K299) Heater Control circuit and any other circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the O2 Sensor 2/1 (K299) Heater Control circuit for a short to the circuit that showed continuity.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>ISOLATE AND LOAD TEST THE O2 SENSOR 2/1 (K299) HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT TO CHECK FOR HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:       Do not load test any circuits with components still connected to the circuit.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic) to the appropriate PCM connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           IMPORTANT - The GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor can add up to 1.5 Ohms of resistance to the circuit.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Load test the O2 Sensor 2/1 (K299) Heater Control circuit. Note: refer to the example illustration.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The bulb on the load test tool should be illuminated and bright if there is no resistance in the circuit. Compare the brightness of the bulb in the load test tool to that of a direct connection across Battery.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the load test bulb illuminated and bright?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the O2 Sensor 2/1 (K299) Heater Control circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>CHECK THE O2 SENSOR 2/1 HEATER CONTROL DRIVER FOR PROPER OPERATION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, actuate the O2 Sensor 2/1 control to the maximum allowable percentage.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Using a 12-volt test light connected to ground, check the O2 Sensor 2/1 (K299) Heater Control circuit at the O2 Sensor 2/1 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The test light should be illuminated. The brightness will be dependent on the actuation percentage selected.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>With the scan tool, actuate the O2 Sensor 2/1 control to the OFF (0%) position.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The test light should not be illuminated.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the test light illuminate with the actuator ON and not illuminated with the actuator OFF (0%)?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the O2 Sensor 2/1 in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to SENSOR, OXYGEN, REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 7</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>CHECK RELATED PCM AND COMPONENT CONNECTIONS</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that apply.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect all PCM harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect all related in-line harness connections (if equipped).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Disconnect the related component harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Inspect harness connectors, component connectors, and all male and female terminals for the following conditions: Proper connector installation. Damaged connector locks. Corrosion. Other signs of water intrusion. Weather seal damage (if equipped). Bent terminals.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Overheating due to a poor connection (terminal may be discolored due to excessive current draw). Terminals that have been pushed back into the connector cavity. Check for spread terminals and verify proper terminal tension.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Repair any conditions that are found.</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Reconnect all PCM harness connectors. Be certain that all harness connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>Reconnect all in-line harness connectors (if equipped). Be certain that all connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">8</span><span>Reconnect all related component harness connectors. Be certain that all connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">9</span><span>With the scan tool, erase DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">10</span><span>Test drive or operate the vehicle in accordance with the when monitored and set conditions.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">11</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Did the DTC return?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">The wiring or poor connection problem has been repaired.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0057 o2 sensor 2/2 heater circuit low\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0057</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 SENSOR 2/2 HEATER CIRCUIT LOW</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The primary objective of the Oxygen (O2) Sensor Heater Control is to attain the fastest possible temperature rise, and to control the steady state temperature of the O2 Sensor. A Positive Temperature Coefficient (PTC) element inside the O2 Sensor heats up as current passes through it. This allows the system to enter Closed Loop quickly and meet the OBD II requirements for continuous monitoring and to deliver accurate readings. This is accomplished by the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) sending a Pulse Width Modulated (PWM) current to the O2 Sensors heater element. The PWM duty cycle is calculated, in response to the temperature measurements. The PCM uses the collected information to continually adjust the PWM to achieve a desired steady state temperature target.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The heater temperature is obtained by measuring the heater resistance and calculating the heater temperature using the resistance versus temperature characteristic. In order to measure the heater temperature, the transistor that controls the heater is momentarily disabled and current is passed through a precision sense resistor connected in series with the heater element. The voltage drop across the sense resistor provides the relationship to current which equates to a temperature value. Once the temperature is acquired, the sense current is turned off and normal operation is restored. The actual heater temperature is monitored continuously and the heating rate is adjusted to prevent damaging the heating element.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The circuit fault diagnostics for O2 sensors are algorithm based. This means that conditions such as a short or an open does not necessarily set a fault the same as a traditional circuit fault. The PCM determines O2 Sensor circuit faults based on pre-programmed variables that are used in a calculation to determine the state of an O2 Sensor. This requires the integrity of all circuits to be verified in order to ensure that the cause of the fault is not overlooked.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Continuously with the O2 Sensor heater commanded on. Battery voltage between 10.5 and 15.75 volts. DTC P0058 is not active.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) detects that the O2 Sensor heater circuit resistance is below approximately 2.0 Ohms.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>O2 SENSOR 1/2 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO GROUND</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/2 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE O2 SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/2</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Start the engine and allow it to Idle.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK THE (K399) O2 SENSOR 2/2 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the O2 Sensor 2/2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Check for continuity between ground and the (K399) O2 Sensor 2/2 Heater Control circuit at the O2 Sensor 2/2 harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between ground and the (K399) O2 Sensor 2/2 Heater Control circuit at the O2 Sensor 2/2 harness connector?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K399) O2 Sensor 2/2 Heater Control circuit for a short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK FOR THE (K399) O2 SENSOR 2/2 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE O2 SENSOR GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for continuity between the O2 Sensor Ground circuit and the (K399) O2 Sensor 2/2 Heater Control circuit at the O2 Sensor 2/2 harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between the O2 Sensor Ground circuit and the (K399) O2 Sensor 2/2 Heater Control circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K399) O2 Sensor 2/2 Heater Control circuit for a short to O2 Sensor Ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE PCM HEATER CONTROL DRIVER FOR PROPER OPERATION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Reconnect the O2 Sensor 2/2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>With the scan tool, actuate the O2 Sensor 2/2 control to the maximum allowable percentage.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Using a 12-volt test light connected to ground, probe the (K399) O2 Sensor 2/2 Heater Control circuit at the O2 Sensor 2/2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The test light should be illuminated. The brightness will be dependent on the actuation percentage selected.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>With the scan tool, actuate the O2 Sensor 2/2 control to the OFF (0%) position.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Using a 12-volt test light connected to ground, probe the (K399) O2 Sensor 2/2 Heater Control circuit at the O2 Sensor 2/2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The test light should not be illuminated.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the test light flash with the actuator ON and not illuminate with the actuator OFF?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the O2 Sensor 2/2 in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to SENSOR, OXYGEN, REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>CHECK RELATED HARNESS CONNECTIONS</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Disconnect all PCM harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect all related in-line harness connections (if equipped).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the related component harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Inspect harness connectors, component connectors, and all male and female terminals for the following conditions: Proper connector installation. Damaged connector locks. Corrosion. Other signs of water intrusion. Weather seal damage (if equipped). Bent terminals. Overheating due to a poor connection (terminal may be discolored due to excessive current draw). Terminals that have been pushed back into the connector cavity. Check for spread terminals and verify proper terminal tension.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Repair any conditions that are found.</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Connect all PCM harness connectors. Be certain that all harness connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Connect all in-line harness connectors (if equipped). Be certain that all connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>Connect all related component harness connectors. Be certain that all connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">8</span><span>With the scan tool, erase DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">9</span><span>Test drive or operate the vehicle in accordance with the when monitored and set conditions.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">10</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Did the DTC return?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the poor connections.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0058 o2 sensor 2/2 heater circuit high\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0058</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 SENSOR 2/2 HEATER CIRCUIT HIGH</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">O2 Sensor Heater Operation: For a typical Switching (4 Wire) Oxygen (O2) Sensor output signal to function the sense element must be heated to operating temperature. A resistive heater element is incorporated within the sensor to allow for rapid heating of the sensor to reach operating temperature faster and closed loop fuel control. The O2 Sensor Heater resistance is directly proportional to the heater temperature, meaning that as heater temperature increases, the heater resistance increases. The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) makes a calculation to determine the heater resistance and temperature. The heater resistance is calculate by measuring the Battery voltage and current draw on the heater control circuit, Then the heater temperature is determined using the resistance versus temperature characteristic. The heater temperature is monitored continuously and the heating rate is adjusted using Pulse Width Modulation (PWM) to prevent damaging the heating element. If the PCM detects a fault in the O2 Sensor heater circuitry the internal PCM driver is disabled during the current ignition cycle,</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">O2 Sensor Output Operation: Normal range of the O2 Sensor output is a 0 to 1.0 volt Analog to Digital (A/D) signal when the sensor is in normal operating temperature range. The output voltage is generated by comparing the Oxygen content in the atmosphere, collected in a reference chamber to the Oxygen content in the exhaust stream collected in a comparison chamber. When the oxygen content is high (caused by a lean air/fuel mixture) the sensor produces a low voltage. When the oxygen content is low (caused by a rich air/fuel mixture) it produces a higher voltage. In some instances a negative offset output of up to -1.0 volt may be introduced if the sensor&#x27;s reference chamber is contaminated. To allow for the negative voltage to be read, each O2 Sensor Return circuit has a 2.5 volt bias added to shift the signal voltage to between 2.5 volts and 3.5 volts.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">O2 Sensor Diagnostics: The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) performs diagnostics on the heater control, signal and sensor return circuits. Because of how the O2 Sensor operates, the circuit fault diagnostics for O2 Sensors are algorithm based. This means an open or short in one of the O2 Sensor circuits can cause a fault to set against another of the O2 Sensor circuits. Understanding how to interpret the combination of DTCs and O2 Sensor data provided can aid in narrowing down where the fault is in the system.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">On vehicle/engine combinations that use only the traditional switching O2 Sensors, both of the Upstream and Downstream O2 Sensor Return circuits originate from a single source within the PCM. Because of this, a short anywhere in the wiring in any of the O2 Sensor Return circuits will affect all of the Upstream and Downstream O2 Sensors. On vehicle/engine combinations that have wide-band Upstream O2 Sensors and switching Downstream O2 Sensors, the Upstream and Downstream O2 Sensor Return circuits are separate within the PCM. In this case a short anywhere in the wiring in the Downstream O2 Sensor Return circuit(s) will only affect the Downstream O2 Sensor(s). The PCM performs individual diagnostics on the O2 Sensor Return circuit for each wide-band Upstream O2 Sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">In data display on the scan tool, filtering on O2 in the search function will bring up the following info:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">O2 Sensor signal voltage - the raw signal voltage should switch between 2.5 and 3.5 volts on a normally functioning sensor. O2 Sensor (0-1) signal voltage - this is the differential signal voltage which is calculated using the raw signal voltage minus the 2.5 volt bias on the return circuit of a normally functioning O2 Sensor. Heater driver operation - displays the heater duty cycle. Heater temperature - this is determined by the total heater circuit resistance. Closed loop fuel control state - displays as open/closed.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Using the Scan Tool for Diagnostics: An open circuit or direct short to voltage or ground will cause the PCM to disable the heater PWM control. The heater will remain disabled for the remainder of that ignition cycle, even if the condition is intermittent and heals itself during that ignition cycle. Any of these conditions should set an O2 Sensor Heater Low or High DTC. Since the heater directly affects the output of sensor signal, and the heater control is disabled, this will cause the affected O2 Sensor raw signal voltage to read high (usually stays above 4.0 volts) and can also eventually set a Signal Circuit High DTC. It is not unusual to have a Heater Control Circuit High or Low DTC and a Sensor Signal High DTC to be set together. Important Note: A small amount of resistance (3-4 Ohms) may not set a Heater Control Circuit DTC but will affect heater operation and cause the sensor signal high DTC to set. Monitoring the heater duty cycle and temperature for a sensor that is reading high on the raw signal voltage can help in diagnosing this issue. A heater circuit with 4.0 - 5.0 Ohms resistance can increase the heater temperature reading on the scan tool for the affected sensor by as much as 700 degrees when compared to the other Switching (4 wire) O2 Sensors. As little as 2.0 Ohms can increase the temperature reading approximately 250 degrees on the scan tool.</p><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE: When a normally operating Switching O2 Sensor is heated to operating temperature the typical duty cycle percentage will be between approximately 30 and 50 percent. The O2 Sensor Heater Diagnosis:                       typical heater temperature will range between approximately 1200°F and 1400°F on the scan tool. If there is an issue with the O2 Sensor heater or circuitry the PCM will disable the heater driver and the duty cycle will be 0 percent. Any issues in the heater circuitry, even a small amount of resistance, will cause the temperature reading to be noticeably different. Monitoring the scan tool and starting the engine when the O2 Sensor Signal is shorted to ground, and when the O2 Sensor is cold, it will show the raw signal voltage at 0 volts but the (0-1) differential voltage signal will read -2.5 volts and increase toward O2 Sensor Signal Low Diagnosis:                   0.0 volts as the sensor heats up. When the sensor is warm the raw signal voltage reading and the (0-1) differential voltage reading will both read near 0.0 volts. It will also pull the sensor return bias voltage low through the O2 Sensor. This will cause the raw voltage signals to switch between the 0 -1 volt range on the other O2 Sensors. This may also cause the O2 Sensor Reference</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Using the Scan Tool for Diagnostics: (Return) Voltage Circuit Low DTC to set. The O2 Sensor Signal High diagnostic can fail due to several conditions. As mentioned above, an open, short or any resistance in the heater circuitry can cause the signal voltage to remain high. Looking for abnormalities in the heater duty cycle or heater temperature of the affected sensor would indicate a heater control issue causing the signal circuit high fault. An open in a sensor O2 Sensor Signal High Diagnosis: return circuit will cause the raw signal voltage to read high, near 5.0 volts, and the (0-1) differential voltage reading to be near 2.5 volts for the affected sensor or sensors. An open in the signal circuit will have the same effect on the sensor. If both Upstream or Downstream O2 Sensors are affected, it could indicate an open in the return circuit that is before the splice in the harness.</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p1621 o2 sensor reference (return) circuit the o2 sensor reference circuit low/high diagnostic is used to monitor the o2 sensor return circuits. if any of the return\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P1621</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 Sensor Reference (Return) Circuit The O2 Sensor Reference Circuit Low/High diagnostic is used to monitor the O2 Sensor Return circuits. If any of the return</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Low Diagnosis:                             circuits are shorted to ground the raw signal voltage readings for all O2 Sensors will read between 0-1 volts instead of 2.5-3.5 volts. This will happen because the bias voltage on the return circuit is pulled low for all sensors. As mentioned in the O2 Sensor Signal Low Diagnosis above, a short to ground in one of the sensor signal circuits will also pull the sensor return low for all sensors. However, this will cause the voltage reading on the sensor that has the shorted signal to read 0.0 volts on both the raw signal voltage and (0-1) differential voltage reading when the sensor is warm.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p1622 o2 sensor reference (return) circuit a short to voltage in any of the sensor return circuits will cause the fault to set. the affected o2 sensors will read 5.0 volts on\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P1622</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 Sensor Reference (Return) Circuit A short to voltage in any of the sensor return circuits will cause the fault to set. The affected O2 Sensors will read 5.0 volts on</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">High Diagnosis:                            the raw signal voltage. The (0-1) differential voltage reading will be 0.0 volts because the signal circuit voltage and return circuit voltage are both 5.0 volts. A short to voltage on one of the O2 Sensor signal circuits will cause the same fault condition and scan tool readings when the sensor is warm. If the condition is present when the engine is started and the sensor is cold, the (0-1) differential voltage reading will start at 2.5 volts and decrease to 0.0 volts as the sensor warms up.</p></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Battery voltage between 10.5 and 15.75 volts. O2 Sensor Heater commanded on. The O2 Sensor Heater circuit low fault is not active.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) detects that the O2 Sensor 2/2 Heater circuit resistance is above approximately 35.0 Ohms.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL will illuminate. The PCM will disable the O2 Sensor Heater driver for the remainder of this ignition cycle.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>O2 SENSOR 2/2 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>O2 SENSOR 2/2 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>O2 SENSOR 2/2 HEATER GROUND CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>O2 SENSOR 2/2</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR ANY SERVICE BULLETINS OR PCM SOFTWARE UPDATES</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for any applicable Service Bulletins or Flash updates related to the DTC.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Are there any applicable Service Bulletins or Flash updates?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the applicable repair.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>READ AND RECORD DTCS AND ENVIRONMENTAL DATA - ERASE DTCS AND CHECK FOR THE DTC TO RETURN</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs in all Electronic Control Units (ECUs) and record on the repair order.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>For future reference, with the scan tool, run and save a vehicle Scan Report and all related recorded data.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>With the scan tool, erase all DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Turn the ignition off for a minimum of 10.0 seconds.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Using the When Monitored and Set Conditions above and recorded data, operate the vehicle in the conditions that set the DTC.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Did the DTC return?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE O2 SENSOR (Z703) GROUND CIRCUIT FOR HIGH RESISTANCE BY LOAD TESTING THE CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          Read the CIRCUIT LOAD TESTING PROCEDURE for information on building a simple load test tool and for additional load testing</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">information and alternative methods of load testing or voltage drop testing a circuit. Refer to STANDARD PROCEDURE .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the O2 Sensor 2/2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not load test any circuits with components still connected to the circuit.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Load test the (Z703) Ground circuit at the O2 Sensor 2/2 harness connector. Note: refer to the example illustration.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The bulb on the load test tool should be illuminated and bright if there is no resistance in the circuitry. Compare the brightness of the bulb in the load test tool to that of a direct connection across Battery.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Is the load test bulb illuminated and bright?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (Z703) Ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE O2 SENSOR 2/2 (K399) HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO ANOTHER CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Check for continuity between the O2 Sensor 2/2 (K399) Heater Control circuit and all other circuits at the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Is there continuity between the O2 Sensor 2/2 (K399) Heater Control circuit and any other circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the O2 Sensor 2/2 (K399) Heater Control circuit for a short to the circuit that showed continuity.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>ISOLATE AND LOAD TEST THE O2 SENSOR 2/2 (K399) HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT TO CHECK FOR HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:       Do not load test any circuits with components still connected to the circuit.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic) to the appropriate PCM connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           IMPORTANT - The GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor can add up to 1.5 Ohms of resistance to the circuit.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Load test the O2 Sensor 2/2 (K399) Heater Control circuit. Note: refer to the example illustration.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The bulb on the load test tool should be illuminated and bright if there is no resistance in the circuit. Compare the brightness of the bulb in the load test tool to that of a direct connection across Battery.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the load test bulb illuminated and bright?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the O2 Sensor 2/2 (K399) Heater Control circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>CHECK THE O2 SENSOR 2/2 HEATER CONTROL DRIVER FOR PROPER OPERATION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, actuate the O2 Sensor 2/2 control to the maximum allowable percentage.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Using a 12-volt test light connected to ground, check the O2 Sensor 2/2 (K399) Heater Control circuit at the O2 Sensor 2/2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The test light should be illuminated. The brightness will be dependent on the actuation percentage selected.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>With the scan tool, actuate the O2 Sensor 2/2 control to the OFF (0%) position.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The test light should not be illuminated.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the test light illuminate with the actuator ON and not illuminated with the actuator OFF (0%)?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the O2 Sensor 2/2 in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to SENSOR, OXYGEN, REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 7</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>CHECK RELATED PCM AND COMPONENT CONNECTIONS</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that apply.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect all PCM harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect all related in-line harness connections (if equipped).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Disconnect the related component harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Inspect harness connectors, component connectors, and all male and female terminals for the following conditions: Proper connector installation. Damaged connector locks. Corrosion. Other signs of water intrusion. Weather seal damage (if equipped). Bent terminals.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Overheating due to a poor connection (terminal may be discolored due to excessive current draw). Terminals that have been pushed back into the connector cavity. Check for spread terminals and verify proper terminal tension.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Repair any conditions that are found.</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Reconnect all PCM harness connectors. Be certain that all harness connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>Reconnect all in-line harness connectors (if equipped). Be certain that all connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">8</span><span>Reconnect all related component harness connectors. Be certain that all connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">9</span><span>With the scan tool, erase DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">10</span><span>Test drive or operate the vehicle in accordance with the when monitored and set conditions.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">11</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Did the DTC return?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">The wiring or poor connection problem has been repaired.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0069 manifold pressure/barometric pressure correlation\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0069</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">MANIFOLD PRESSURE/BAROMETRIC PRESSURE CORRELATION</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">           COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                         DTCS\n            BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                            P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n      Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                      N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n                 Sensor\n     Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger              P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n    Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                   Supercharger\n          Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                       P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n          Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                     P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:             The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:             The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously with the engine running.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">During natural aspiration operation the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor reading is greater that the BARO reading plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi), or during boost operation the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within 15 kPa (2.18 psi) of the BARO reading.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL will illuminate. Boost is disabled.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>BLOCKAGE OR RESTRICTION IN THE AIR FILTER OR INTAKE AIR FLOW</li><li>SUPERCHARGER BYPASS VALVE STICKING OR INTERMITTENT ELECTRICAL CONNECTION</li><li>THROTTLE BODY STICKING OR INTERMITTENT ELECTRICAL CONNECTION</li><li>SUPERCHARGER INLET PRESSURE SENSOR OUT OF RANGE</li><li>BARO SENSOR OUT OF RANGE</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           Diagnose and repair any 5-Volt Reference High or Low DTCs before continuing with this test procedure.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Start the engine and allow it to idle.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK FOR RESTRICTION IN THE INTAKE AIR PLUMBING OR AIR FILTER</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Visually inspect the intake air plumbing for damage or debris that could restrict air flow.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Visually inspect the Air Filter for excessive debris that could restrict air flow.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any issues found with the intake air system or Air Filter?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the appropriate repair in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE SUPERCHARGER BYPASS VALVE FOR BINDING/STICKING/INTERMITTENT CONNECTION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Check the Supercharger Bypass Valve for a physical or mechanical obstruction.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the Supercharger Bypass Valve harness connector and check the terminals for loose fit, corrosion, or pushed out terminals causing an intermittent connection.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the appropriate repair.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE THROTTLE BODY FOR BINDING/STICKING/INTERMITTENT CONNECTION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check the Throttle Body for a physical or mechanical obstruction.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Throttle Body harness connector and check the terminals for loose fit, corrosion, or pushed out terminals causing an intermittent connection.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the appropriate repair.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>CHECK THE SUPERCHARGER INLET PRESSURE SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, view the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor reading.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>With the scan tool, view the other three MAP/Pressure Sensor readings.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Is the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor reading within 5.0 kPa (0.725 psi) of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Replace the Powertrain Control Module in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Replace the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0071 ambient air temperature sensor performance\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0071</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">AMBIENT AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR PERFORMANCE</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor rationality diagnostic runs under cold start conditions, following a start to run delay time. The diagnostic monitor compares the outputs of the AAT, Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Intake Air Temperature (IAT) sensors. If the ECT and IAT Sensors agree, but the AAT Sensor does not agree, then the AAT Sensor is declared as irrational. If declared irrational a second comparison will be done after a short drive cycle.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With engine off time greater than 480 minutes. Ambient temperature, Intake Air temperature and Engine Coolant Temperature are greater than -64°C (-83.2°F).</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">After a calibrated amount of cool down time, the PCM compares the AAT, ECT, and IAT Sensor values. If one sensor value is not within 15°C (59°F) of the other temperature sensors, a fault is set.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE AAT SIGNAL CIRCUIT</li><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE AAT SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>AMBIENT AIR TEMPERATURE (AAT) SENSOR</li><li>BODY CONTROL MODULE (BCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE CONDITION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>If possible, allow the vehicle to sit with the ignition off long enough for the engine to cool to ambient temperature.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With a scan tool, compare the AAT, ECT, and IAT sensor values.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor value within 15°C (59°F) of the other two sensor values?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (G31) AAT SIGNAL CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the AAT Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the BCM D harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (G31) AAT Signal circuit between the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor harness connector and the BCM D harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (G31) AAT Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (G931) AAT SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (G931) AAT Sensor Ground circuit between the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor harness connector and the BCM D harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (G931) AAT Sensor Ground for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>AMBIENT AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect the BCM D harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (G931) AAT Sensor Ground circuit and the (G31) AAT Signal circuit at the AAT Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>With the scan tool, read the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor signal voltage.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage below 0.1 volts with the jumper wire in place?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>BODY CONTROL MODULE (BCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor and the Body Control Module (BCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the BODY VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to BODY VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Body Control Module (BCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, BODY CONTROL (BCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the BODY VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to BODY VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0072 ambient air temperature sensor circuit low\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0072</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">AMBIENT AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR CIRCUIT LOW</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor rationality diagnostic runs under cold start conditions, following a start to run delay time. The diagnostic monitor compares the outputs of the AAT, Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Intake Air Temperature (IAT) sensors. If the ECT and IAT Sensors agree, but the AAT Sensor does not agree, then the AAT Sensor is declared as irrational. If declared irrational a second comparison will be done after a short drive cycle.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The PCM detects that the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor input voltage is below the minimum acceptable value.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>AAT SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO GROUND</li><li>AAT SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO AAT SENSOR GROUND</li><li>AAT SIGNAL CIRCUIT OPEN OR HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>AMBIENT AIR TEMPERATURE (AAT) SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>BODY CONTROL MODULE (BCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           Diagnose any CAN - C Communication DTCs before continuing.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           Diagnose and repair any system voltage DTCs before continuing with this test.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Start the engine and allow it to reach normal operating temperature.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">WARNING:        When the engine is operating, do not stand in direct line with the fan. Do not put your hands near the pulleys, belts or fan. Do not wear loose clothing. Failure to follow these instructions may result in possible serious or fatal injury.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK THE (G31) AAT SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the BCM C4 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Check for continuity between ground and the (G31) AAT Signal circuit in the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between ground and the (G31) AAT Signal circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (G31) AAT Signal circuit for a short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK FOR THE (G31) AAT SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE (G931) AAT SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for continuity between the (G31) AAT Signal circuit and the (G931) AAT Sensor Ground circuit in the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between the (G31) AAT Signal circuit and the (G931) AAT Sensor Ground circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (G31) AAT Signal circuit for a short to the (G931) AAT Sensor Ground circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE (G31) AAT SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (G31) AAT Signal circuit between the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor harness connector and the BCM C4 harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (G31) AAT Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>AMBIENT AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Reconnect the BCM C4 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>With the scan tool, read the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor signal voltage.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage above 4.8 volts with the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor harness disconnected?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>BODY CONTROL MODULE (BCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor and the Body Control Module (BCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the BODY VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to BODY VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Body Control Module (BCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, BODY CONTROL (BCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the BODY VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to BODY VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0073 ambient air temperature sensor circuit high\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0073</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">AMBIENT AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR CIRCUIT HIGH</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor rationality diagnostic runs under cold start conditions, following a start to run delay time. The diagnostic monitor compares the outputs of the AAT, Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Intake Air Temperature (IAT) sensors. If the ECT and IAT Sensors agree, but the AAT Sensor does not agree, then the AAT Sensor is declared as irrational. If declared irrational a second comparison will be done after a short drive cycle.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) detects that the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor input voltage is above the maximum acceptable value.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>AAT SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>AAT SIGNAL CIRCUIT OPEN OR HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>AAT SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT OPEN OR HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>AMBIENT AIR TEMPERATURE (AAT) SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>BODY CONTROL MODULE (BCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           Diagnose any CAN - C Communication DTCs before continuing.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           Diagnose and repair any system voltage DTCs before continuing with this test.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK THE (G31) AAT SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO VOLTAGE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the BCM C4 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Measure the voltage on the (G31) AAT Signal circuit in the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage above 5.2 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (G31) AAT Signal circuit for a short to voltage.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (G31) AAT SIGNAL FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the BCM C4 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (G31) AAT Signal circuit between the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor harness connector and the BCM C4 harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (G31) AAT Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE (G931) AAT SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (G931) AAT Sensor Ground circuit between the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor harness connector and the BCM C4 harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (G931) AAT Sensor Ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>AMBIENT AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect the BCM C4 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (G31) AAT Signal circuit and the (G931) AAT Sensor Ground in the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>With the scan tool, read the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor signal voltage.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage below 0.2 volts with the jumper in place?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>BODY CONTROL MODULE (BCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Ambient Air Temperature Sensor and the BCM.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the BODY VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to BODY VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Body Control Module (BCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, BODY CONTROL (BCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the BODY VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to BODY VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p007b charge air cooler temperature sensor circuit performance\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P007B</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">CHARGE AIR COOLER TEMPERATURE SENSOR CIRCUIT PERFORMANCE</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">           COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                         DTCS\n            BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                            P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n      Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                      N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n                 Sensor\n     Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger              P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n    Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                   Supercharger\n          Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                       P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n          Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                     P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:             The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:             The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the engine off time greater than 480 minutes. Ambient temperature greater than -7°C (19.4°F).</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">After a calibrated amount of cool down time, the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) compares the Ambient Air Temperature (AAT), Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT), and Bank 1 MAP Temperature Sensor values. If one sensor value is not within 15°C (59°F) of the other temperature sensors, a DTC will set.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE BANK 1 TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT</li><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>BANK 1 MAP SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE CONDITION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Allow the vehicle to sit with the ignition off in an environment where the temperature is consistent and above -7°C (19.4°F) until the engine coolant temperature is equal to ambient temperature.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With a scan tool, compare the Bank 1 Temp Sensor value with the other temperature sensor signals.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the Bank 1 Temp Sensor value within 15°C (59°F) of the other sensor values?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K46) BANK 1 TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K46) Bank 1 Temp Signal circuit between the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K46) Bank 1 Temp Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K915) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K915) Sensor Ground circuit between the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K915) Sensor Ground for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>BANK 1 MAP SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, read the Bank 1 Temp Signal voltage.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (K915) Sensor Ground circuit and the (K46) Bank 1 Temp Signal circuit at the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>With the scan tool, read the Bank 1 Temp Signal voltage.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The voltage should read 5.0 volts with the connector unplugged and below 0.1 volts with the jumper in place.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the voltage read as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Bank 1 MAP Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Bank 1 MAP Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p007c charge air cooler temperature sensor circuit low\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P007C</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">CHARGE AIR COOLER TEMPERATURE SENSOR CIRCUIT LOW</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">           COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                         DTCS\n            BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                            P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n      Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                      N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n                 Sensor\n     Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger              P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n    Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                   Supercharger\n          Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                       P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n          Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                     P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:             The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:             The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Bank 1 Temp Sensor input voltage is below the minimum acceptable threshold.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>BANK 1 TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO GROUND</li><li>BANK 1 TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE SENSOR GROUND</li><li>BANK 1 MAP SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>BANK 1 MAP SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With a scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The P007D-CHARGE AIR COOLER TEMPERATURE SENSOR CIRCUIT HIGH DTC should be active or pending with the Bank 1 Sensor harness connector unplugged.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the Bank 1 MAP Sensor and Powertrain Control Module connectors. Replace the Bank 1 MAP Sensor if no problems were found in the connectors.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K46) BANK 1 TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Check for continuity between ground and the (K46) Bank 1 Temp Signal circuit at the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between ground and the (K46) Bank 1 Temp Signal circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K46) Bank 1 Temp Signal circuit for a short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK FOR THE (K46) BANK 1 TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE (K915) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for continuity between the (K46) Bank 1 Temp Signal circuit and the (K915) Sensor Ground circuit in the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between the (K46) Bank 1 Temp Signal circuit and the (K915) Sensor Ground circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K46) Bank 1 Temp Signal circuit for a short to the (K915) Sensor Ground circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Bank 1 MAP Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p007d charge air cooler temperature sensor circuit high\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P007D</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">CHARGE AIR COOLER TEMPERATURE SENSOR CIRCUIT HIGH</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">           COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                         DTCS\n            BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                            P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n      Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                      N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n                 Sensor\n     Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger              P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n    Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                   Supercharger\n          Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                       P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n          Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                     P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:             The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:             The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Bank 1 MAP Temp Sensor input voltage is above the maximum acceptable threshold.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>POSSIBLE CAUSES</li><li>BANK 1 TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>BANK 1 TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>BANK 1 MAP SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK THE (K46) BANK 1 TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO VOLTAGE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the voltage on the (K46) Bank 1 Temp Signal circuit at the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage above 5.2 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K46) Bank 1 Temp Signal circuit for a short to voltage.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K46) BANK 1 TEMP SIGNAL FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K46) Bank 1 Temp Signal circuit between the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K46) Bank 1 Temp Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit between the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>BANK 1 MAP SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Reconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (K46) Bank 1 Temp Signal circuit and the (K915) Sensor Ground at the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCS.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The P007C-CHARGE AIR COOLER TEMPERATURE SENSOR CIRCUIT LOW DTC should be active or pending with the jumper in place.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Bank 1 MAP Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Bank 1 MAP Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0096 intake air temperature sensor 2 circuit performance\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0096</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">INTAKE AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR 2 CIRCUIT PERFORMANCE</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">           COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                         DTCS\n            BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                            P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n      Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                      N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n                 Sensor\n     Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger              P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n    Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                   Supercharger\n          Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                       P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n          Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                     P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:             The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:             The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the engine off time greater than 480 minutes. Ambient temperature greater than -7°C (19.4°F).</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">After a calibrated amount of cool down time, the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) compares the Ambient Air Temperature (AAT), Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT), and Supercharger Inlet Temp Sensor values. If the Supercharger Inlet Temp Sensor value is not within 15°C (59°F) of the other temperature sensors, a DTC will set.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE SUPERCHARGER INLET TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT</li><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>SUPERCHARGER INLET PRESSURE SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE CONDITION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Allow the vehicle to sit with the ignition off in an environment where the temperature is consistent and above -7°C (19.4°F) until the engine coolant temperature is equal to ambient temperature.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With a scan tool, compare the Supercharger Inlet Temp Sensor value with the other temperature sensor signals.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the Supercharger Inlet Temp Sensor value within 15°C (59°F) of the other sensor values?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K7) SUPERCHARGER INLET TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K7) Supercharger Inlet Temp Signal circuit between the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K7) Supercharger Inlet Temp Signal circuit for an open circuit or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit between the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K900) Sensor Ground for an open circuit or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>SUPERCHARGER INLET PRESSURE SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, read the Supercharger Inlet Temp Sensor signal voltage.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit and the (K7) Supercharger Inlet Temp Signal circuit in the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>With the scan tool, read the Supercharger Inlet Temp Sensor signal voltage.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The voltage should read 5.0 volts with the connector unplugged and below 0.1 volts with the jumper in place.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the voltage read as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0097 intake air temperature sensor 2 circuit low\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0097</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">INTAKE AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR 2 CIRCUIT LOW</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">           COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                         DTCS\n            BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                            P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n      Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                      N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n                 Sensor\n     Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger              P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n    Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                   Supercharger\n          Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                       P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n          Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                     P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:             The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:             The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Supercharger Inlet Temp Sensor input voltage is below the minimum acceptable threshold.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>SUPERCHARGER INLET TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO GROUND</li><li>SUPERCHARGER INLET TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE SENSOR GROUND</li><li>SUPERCHARGER INLET PRESSURE SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>SUPERCHARGER INLET PRESSURE SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With a scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The P0198-INTAKE AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR 2 CIRCUIT HIGH DTC should be active or pending with the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor harness connector unplugged.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor and Powertrain Control Module connectors. Replace the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor if no problems were found in the connectors.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K7) SUPERCHARGER INLET TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Check for continuity between ground and the (K7) Supercharger Inlet Temp Signal circuit at the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between ground and the (K7) Supercharger Inlet Temp Signal circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K7) Supercharger Inlet Temp Signal circuit for a short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK FOR THE (K7) TOP TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for continuity between the (K7) Supercharger Inlet Temp Signal circuit and the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit in the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between the (K7) Supercharger Inlet Temp Signal circuit and the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K7) Supercharger Inlet Temp Signal circuit for a short to the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0098 intake air temperature sensor 2 circuit high\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0098</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">INTAKE AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR 2 CIRCUIT HIGH</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">           COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                         DTCS\n            BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                            P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n      Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                      N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n                 Sensor\n     Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger              P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n    Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                   Supercharger\n          Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                       P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n          Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                     P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:             The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:             The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Supercharger Inlet Temp Sensor input voltage is above the maximum acceptable threshold.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>POSSIBLE CAUSES</li><li>SUPERCHARGER INLET TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>SUPERCHARGER INLET TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>SUPERCHARGER INLET PRESSURE SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK THE (K7) SUPERCHARGER INLET TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO VOLTAGE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the voltage on the (K7) TOP Supercharger Inlet Signal circuit at the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage above 5.2 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K7) Supercharger Inlet Temp Signal circuit for a short to voltage.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K7) SUPERCHARGER INLET TEMP SIGNAL FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K7) Supercharger Inlet Temp Signal circuit between the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K7) Supercharger Inlet Temp Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit between the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>SUPERCHARGER INLET PRESSURE SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Reconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (K7) Supercharger Inlet Temp Signal circuit and the (K900) Sensor Ground at the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCS.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          The P0097-INTAKE AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR 2 CIRCUIT LOW DTC should be active or pending with the jumper in place.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p00a1 charge air cooler temperature sensor circuit range/performance - bank 2\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P00A1</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">CHARGE AIR COOLER TEMPERATURE SENSOR CIRCUIT RANGE/PERFORMANCE - BANK 2</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">           COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                         DTCS\n            BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                            P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n      Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                      N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n                 Sensor\n     Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger              P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n    Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                   Supercharger\n          Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                       P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n          Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                     P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:             The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:             The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the engine off time greater than 480 minutes. Ambient temperature greater than -7°C (19.4°F).</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">After a calibrated amount of cool down time, the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) compares the Ambient Air Temperature (AAT), Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT), and Bank 2 MAP Temperature Sensor values. If the Bank 2 MAP Sensor value is not within 15°C (59°F) of the other temperature sensors, a DTC will set.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE BANK 2 TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT</li><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>BANK 2 MAP SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE CONDITION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Allow the vehicle to sit with the ignition off in an environment where the temperature is consistent and above -7°C (19.4°F) until the engine coolant temperature is equal to ambient temperature.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With a scan tool, compare the Bank 2 Temp Sensor value with the other temperature sensor signals.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the Bank 2 MAP Sensor value within 15°C (59°F) of the other sensor values?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K47) BANK 2 TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Bank 2 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K47) Bank 2 Temp Signal circuit between the Bank 2 MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K47) Bank 2 Temp Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit between the Bank 2 MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K900) Sensor Ground for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>THROTTLE OUTLET PRESSURE SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, read the Bank 2 Temp Signal voltage.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (K915) Sensor Ground circuit and the (K47) Bank 2 Temp Signal circuit at the Bank 2 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>With the scan tool, read the Bank 2 Temp Signal voltage.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The voltage should read 5.0 volts with the connector unplugged and below 0.1 volts with the jumper in place.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the voltage read as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Bank 2 MAP Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Bank 2 MAP Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p00a2 charge air cooler temperature sensor circuit low - bank 2\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P00A2</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">CHARGE AIR COOLER TEMPERATURE SENSOR CIRCUIT LOW - BANK 2</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">           COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                         DTCS\n            BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                            P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n      Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                      N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n                 Sensor\n     Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger              P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n    Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                   Supercharger\n          Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                       P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n          Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                     P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:             The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:             The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Bank 2 Temp Sensor input voltage is below the minimum acceptable threshold.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>BANK 2 TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO GROUND</li><li>BANK 2 TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE SENSOR GROUND</li><li>BANK 2 MAP SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>BANK 2 MAP SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Bank 2 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With a scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The P00A3-CHARGE AIR COOLER TEMPERATURE SENSOR CIRCUIT HIGH - BANK 2 DTC should be active or pending with the Bank 2 Sensor harness connector unplugged.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the Bank 2 MAP Sensor and Powertrain Control Module connectors. Replace the Bank 2 MAP Sensor if no problems were found in the connectors.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K47) BANK 2 TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Check for continuity between ground and the (K47) Bank 2 Temp Signal circuit at the Bank 2 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between ground and the (K47) Bank 2 Temp Signal circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K47) Bank 2 Temp Signal circuit for a short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK FOR THE (K47) BANK 2 TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for continuity between the (K47) Bank 2 Temp Signal circuit and the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit in the Bank 2 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between the (K47) Bank 2 Temp Signal circuit and the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K47) Bank 2 Temp Signal circuit for a short to the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Bank 2 MAP Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p00a3 charge air cooler temperature sensor circuit high - bank 2\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P00A3</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">CHARGE AIR COOLER TEMPERATURE SENSOR CIRCUIT HIGH - BANK 2</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">           COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                         DTCS\n            BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                            P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n      Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                      N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n                 Sensor\n     Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger              P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n    Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                   Supercharger\n          Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                       P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n          Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                     P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:             The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:             The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Bank 2 MAP Temp Sensor input voltage is above the maximum acceptable threshold.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>POSSIBLE CAUSES</li><li>BANK 2 TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>BANK 2 TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>BANK 2 MAP SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK THE (K47) BANK 2 TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO VOLTAGE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Bank 2 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the voltage between ground and the (K47) Bank 2 Temp Signal circuit at the Bank 2 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage above 5.2 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K47) Bank 2 Temp Signal circuit for a short to voltage.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K47) BANK 2 TEMP SIGNAL FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K47) Bank 2 Temp Signal circuit between the Bank 2 MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K47) Bank 2 Temp Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit between the Bank 2 MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>BANK 2 MAP SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Reconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (K47) Bank 2 Temp Signal circuit and the (K900) Sensor Ground at the Bank 2 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCS.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          The P00A2-CHARGE AIR COOLER TEMPERATURE SENSOR CIRCUIT LOW - BANK 2 DTC should be active or pending with the jumper in place.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Bank 2 MAP Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Bank 2 Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p00df charge air cooler coolant temperature sensor circuit performance\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P00DF</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">CHARGE AIR COOLER COOLANT TEMPERATURE SENSOR CIRCUIT PERFORMANCE</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">To improve engine performance the Charge Air Cooler (CAC) system is used to reduce the temperature of the air entering the combustion chambers. The temperature increase of the air entering engine is caused by the Supercharger. The CAC system consists of an electric CAC Coolant Pump, Charge Air Coolers, Coolant Reservoir, Charge Air Cooler Radiator, Charge Air</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Cooler Coolant Temperature Sensor and plumbing. The CAC cooling system operates independent of the vehicle&#x27;s primary cooling system.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The CAC Pump is a smart device that contains a LIN Bus communication module and a coolant pump. To maintain a desired charge air temperature, the PCM sends a pump speed command over the LIN bus to the internal CAC pump module. The internal CAC Pump module controls the pump speed/coolant flow rate using Pulse-width modulation (PWM). The CAC Pump assembly monitors for internal system circuit and performance failures. If a fault occurs the CAC Pump module sends the fault information to the PCM through the dedicated LIN Bus. The PCM processes the information received, then sets the correlating fault.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Engine off time is greater than 480 minutes. Ambient temperature is greater than -64°C (-83°F).</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Once the vehicle has cold soaked for a calibrated engine off time the PCM compares the Ambient, Charge Air Cooler and Intake Air Temperature Sensor values. If Ambient Air and Intake Air Temperature Sensors agree with each other but Charge Air Cooler does not agree with them, the Charge Air Cooler Temperature Sensor is declared as irrational.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE CAC SIGNAL CIRCUIT</li><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>CHARGE AIR COOLER (CAC) TEMPERATURE SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHARGE AIR COOLER TEMPERATURE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Allow the vehicle to sit with the ignition off for more than 480 minutes in an environment where the temperature is consistent and above -64°C (-83°F) or until the CAC temperature is equal to ambient temperature.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, compare the CAC, ECT and IAT sensor values.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the Engine Coolant Temperature Sensor value within 15°C (59°F) of the other two sensor values?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K222) CAC SIGNAL CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the CAC Temperature Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:       Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K222) CAC Signal circuit between the CAC Temperature Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K222) CAC Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit between the CAC Temperature Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Repair the (K900) Sensor ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CAC SENSOR VOLTAGE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Reconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, read the CAC Temperature Sensor voltage.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          The sensor voltage should be approximately 5.0 volts (plus or minus 0.1 volts) with the connector disconnected.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (K222) CAC Signal circuit and the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit at the CAC Temperature Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          The sensor voltage should be approximately 0.0 volts (plus or minus 0.1 volts) with the jumper in place.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display the voltage as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the CAC Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the CAC Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals. Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the CAC Sensor and Powertrain Control Module connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were there any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p00e0 charge air cooler coolant temperature sensor &quot;a&quot; circuit low\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P00E0</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">CHARGE AIR COOLER COOLANT TEMPERATURE SENSOR &quot;A&quot; CIRCUIT LOW</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">To improve engine performance the Charge Air Cooler (CAC) system is used to reduce the temperature of the air entering the combustion chambers. The temperature increase of the air entering engine is caused by the Supercharger. The CAC system consists of an electric CAC Coolant Pump, Charge Air Coolers, Coolant Reservoir, Charge Air Cooler Radiator, Charge Air</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Cooler Coolant Temperature Sensor and plumbing. The CAC cooling system operates independent of the vehicle&#x27;s primary cooling system.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The CAC Pump is a smart device that contains a LIN Bus communication module and a coolant pump. To maintain a desired charge air temperature, the PCM sends a pump speed command over the LIN bus to the internal CAC pump module. The internal CAC Pump module controls the pump speed/coolant flow rate using Pulse-width modulation (PWM). The CAC Pump assembly monitors for internal system circuit and performance failures. If a fault occurs the CAC Pump module sends the fault information to the PCM through the dedicated LIN Bus. The PCM processes the information received, then sets the correlating fault.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Charge Air Cooler (CAC) Temperature Sensor input voltage is below the minimum acceptable value.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>CAC SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO GROUND</li><li>CAC SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>CHARGE AIR COOLER (CAC) TEMPERATURE SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CAC TEMPERATURE SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the CAC Temperature Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, read the CAC Temperature Sensor voltage.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The sensor voltage should be approximately 5.0 volts (plus or minus 0.1 volts) with the connector disconnected.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display the voltage as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the CAC Temperature Sensor and Powertrain Control Module connectors. If no problems are found, replace the CAC Temperature Sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K222) ECT SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Check for continuity between ground and the (K222) CAC Signal circuit in the CAC Temperature Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between ground and the (K222) CAC Signal circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K222) CAC Signal circuit for a short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK FOR THE (K222) CAC SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for continuity between the (K900) Sensor ground circuit and the (K222) CAC Signal circuit in the CAC Temperature Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between the (K900) Sensor ground circuit and the (K222) CAC Signal circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the short to between the (K900) Sensor ground circuit and the (K222) CAC Signal circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the CAC Temperature Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals. Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the CAC Temperature Sensor and Powertrain Control Module connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were there any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p00e1 charge air cooler coolant temperature sensor &quot;a&quot; circuit high\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P00E1</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">CHARGE AIR COOLER COOLANT TEMPERATURE SENSOR &quot;A&quot; CIRCUIT HIGH</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">To improve engine performance the Charge Air Cooler (CAC) system is used to reduce the temperature of the air entering the combustion chambers. The temperature increase of the air entering engine is caused by the Supercharger. The CAC system consists of an electric CAC Coolant Pump, Charge Air Coolers, Coolant Reservoir, Charge Air Cooler Radiator, Charge Air</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Cooler Coolant Temperature Sensor and plumbing. The CAC cooling system operates independent of the vehicle&#x27;s primary cooling system.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The CAC Pump is a smart device that contains a LIN Bus communication module and a coolant pump. To maintain a desired charge air temperature, the PCM sends a pump speed command over the LIN bus to the internal CAC pump module. The internal CAC Pump module controls the pump speed/coolant flow rate using Pulse-width modulation (PWM). The CAC Pump assembly monitors for internal system circuit and performance failures. If a fault occurs the CAC Pump module sends the fault information to the PCM through the dedicated LIN Bus. The PCM processes the information received, then sets the correlating fault.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Charge Air Cooler (CAC) Temperature Sensor input voltage is above the maximum acceptable threshold.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>CAC SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>CAC SIGNAL CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>CHARGE AIR COOLER TEMPERATURE (CAC) SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:            Diagnose and repair any system voltage DTCs before continuing with this test.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK THE (K222) CAC SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO VOLTAGE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the CAC Temperature Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the voltage on the (K222) CAC Signal circuit in the CAC Temperature Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage above 5.2 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K222) CAC Signal circuit for a short to voltage.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K222) CAC SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K222) CAC Signal circuit between the CAC Temperature Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K222) ECT Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K900) Sensor ground circuit between the CAC Temperature Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the open or excessive resistance in the (K900) Sensor ground circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>CAC TEMPERATURE SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Reconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (K222) CAC Signal circuit and the (K900) Sensor ground circuit in the CAC Temperature Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, read CAC voltage.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          The sensor voltage should be approximately 0.0 volts (plus or minus 0.1 volts) with the jumper in place.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display the voltage as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the CAC Temperature Sensor and Powertrain Control Module connectors. If no problems are found, replace the CAC Temperature Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          Remove the jumper before continuing.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the CAC Temperature Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals. Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the CAC Temperature Sensor and Powertrain Control Module connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were there any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p00e9 intake air temperature sensor 3/1 circuit performance\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P00E9</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">INTAKE AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR 3/1 CIRCUIT PERFORMANCE</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">         COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                       DTCS\n         BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                             P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n   Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                       N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n              Sensor\n  Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger               P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                 P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                Supercharger\n       Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                        P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n       Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                      P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the engine off time greater than 480 minutes. Ambient temperature greater than -7°C (19.4°F).</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">After a calibrated amount of cool down time, the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) compares the Ambient Air Temperature (AAT), Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT), and Supercharger Outlet Temperature Sensor values. If the Supercharger Outlet Temp Sensor value is not within 15°C (59°F) of the other temperature sensors, a DTC will set.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE SUPERCHARGER OUTLET TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT</li><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET MAP SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE CONDITION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Allow the vehicle to sit with the ignition off in an environment where the temperature is consistent and above -7°C (19.4°F) until the engine coolant temperature is equal to ambient temperature.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With a scan tool, compare the Supercharger Outlet Temp Sensor value with the other temperature sensor signals.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the Supercharger Outlet Sensor value within 15°C (59°F) of the other sensor values?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K49) SUPERCHARGER OUTLET TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C1 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K49) Supercharger Outlet Temp Signal circuit between the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K49) Supercharger Outlet Temp Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit between the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K900) Sensor Ground for an open circuit or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET MAP SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect the PCM C1 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, read the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor signal voltage.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit and the (K49) Supercharger Outlet Temp Signal circuit at the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>With the scan tool, read the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor signal voltage.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The voltage should read 5.0 volts with the connector unplugged and below 0.1 volts with the jumper in place.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the voltage read as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Intake Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p00ea intake air temperature sensor 3/1 circuit low\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P00EA</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">INTAKE AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR 3/1 CIRCUIT LOW</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">         COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                       DTCS\n         BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                             P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n   Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                       N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n              Sensor\n  Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger               P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                 P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                Supercharger\n       Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                        P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n       Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                      P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Supercharger Outlet Temp Sensor input voltage is below the minimum acceptable threshold.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO GROUND</li><li>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE SENSOR GROUND</li><li>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET MAP SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET MAP SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With a scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The P00EB-INTAKE AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR 3/1 CIRCUIT HIGH DTC should be active or pending with the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector unplugged.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor and Powertrain Control Module connectors. Replace the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor if no problems were found in the connectors.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K49) SUPERCHARGER OUTLET TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C1 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Check for continuity between ground and the (K49) Supercharger Outlet Temp Signal circuit at the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between ground and the (K49) Supercharger Outlet Temp Signal circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K49) Supercharger Outlet Temp Signal circuit for a short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK FOR THE (K49) SUPERCHARGER OUTLET TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for continuity between the (K49) Supercharger Outlet Temp Signal circuit and the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit in the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between the (K49) Supercharger Outlet Temp Signal circuit and the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K49) Supercharger Outlet Temp Signal circuit for a short to the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p00eb intake air temperature sensor 3/1 circuit high\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P00EB</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">INTAKE AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR 3/1 CIRCUIT HIGH</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">         COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                       DTCS\n         BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                             P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n   Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                       N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n              Sensor\n  Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger               P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                 P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                Supercharger\n       Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                        P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n       Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                      P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Supercharger Outlet Temp Sensor input voltage is above the maximum acceptable threshold.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>POSSIBLE CAUSES</li><li>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET MAP SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK THE (K49) SUPERCHARGER OUTLET TEMP SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO VOLTAGE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the voltage on the (K49) Supercharger Outlet Temp Signal circuit at the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage above 5.2 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K49) Supercharger Outlet Temp Signal circuit for a short to voltage.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K49) SUPERCHARGER OUTLET TEMP SIGNAL FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C1 harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K49) Supercharger Outlet Temp Signal circuit between the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K49) Supercharger Outlet Temp Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit between the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET PRESSURE SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Reconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (K49) Supercharger Outlet Temp Signal circuit and the (K900) Sensor Ground at the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCS.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          The P00EC-INTAKE AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR 3/1 CIRCUIT LOW DTC should be active or pending with the jumper in place.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p00f8 supercharger/turbocharger outlet pressure sensor 1 circuit\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P00F8</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">SUPERCHARGER/TURBOCHARGER OUTLET PRESSURE SENSOR 1 CIRCUIT</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">         COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                       DTCS\n         BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                             P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n   Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                       N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n              Sensor\n  Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger               P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                 P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                Supercharger\n       Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                        P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n       Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                      P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Engine not running.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor signal is above the calibrated upper operating voltage range or below the calibrated lower operating voltage range.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE SUPERCHARGER OUTLET PRESSURE SIGNAL CIRCUIT</li><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET MAP SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          Diagnose and repair any 5-Volt Reference High or Low DTCs before continuing with this test procedure.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K171) SUPERCHARGER OUTLET PRESSURE SIGNAL CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the PCM harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:         Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K171) Supercharger Outlet Pressure Signal circuit between the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K171) Supercharger Outlet Pressure Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit between the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET PRESSURE SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Replace the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Reconnect the PCM harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Reconnect the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair is complete.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p00f9 supercharger/turbocharger outlet pressure sensor 1 circuit range/performance\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P00F9</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">SUPERCHARGER/TURBOCHARGER OUTLET PRESSURE SENSOR 1 CIRCUIT RANGE/PERFORMANCE</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">         COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                       DTCS\n         BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                             P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n   Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                       N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n              Sensor\n  Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger               P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                 P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                Supercharger\n       Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                        P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n       Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                      P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on, the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor signal is compared to the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP Sensor, Bank 2 MAP Sensor and BARO Sensor signals. With the engine running, the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor signal is compared to the Bank 1 MAP Sensor and Bank 2 MAP Sensor signals.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The difference between the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor signal and the other three MAP/pressure sensor signal signals is more than 5.0 kPa (0.725 psi) with the ignition on, or the difference between the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor signal and Bank 1/Bank 2 MAP Sensor signals is more than 14.7 kPa (2.13psi) with the engine running in during natural aspiration.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>5-VOLT SUPPLY CIRCUIT OPEN</li><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE SUPERCHARGER OUTLET PRESSURE SIGNAL CIRCUIT</li><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET MAP SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           Diagnose and repair any 5-Volt Reference High or Low DTCs before continuing with this test procedure.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK THE (F856) 5-VOLT SUPPLY CIRCUIT VOLTAGE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the voltage on the (F856) 5-Volt Supply circuit at the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage above 4.9 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (F856) 5-Volt Supply circuit for an open circuit or short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K171) SUPERCHARGER OUTLET PRESSURE SIGNAL CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K171) Supercharger Outlet Pressure Signal circuit between the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K171) Supercharger Outlet Pressure Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit between the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET MAP SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Replace the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Reconnect the PCM harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Reconnect the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair is complete.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p00fa supercharger/turbocharger outlet air pressure sensor 1 circuit low\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P00FA</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">SUPERCHARGER/TURBOCHARGER OUTLET AIR PRESSURE SENSOR 1 CIRCUIT LOW</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">         COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                       DTCS\n         BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                             P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n   Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                       N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n              Sensor\n  Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger               P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                 P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                Supercharger\n       Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                        P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n       Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                      P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor input voltage is below the minimum acceptable threshold.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET PRESSURE SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO GROUND</li><li>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET PRESSURE SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE SENSOR GROUND</li><li>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET MAP SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           Diagnose and repair any 5-Volt Reference High or Low DTCs before continuing with this test procedure.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET PRESSURE SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (F856) 5-Volt Supply circuit and the (K171) Supercharger Outlet Pressure Signal circuit at the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The P00FB-SUPERCHARGER/TURBOCHARGER OUTLET AIR PRESSURE SENSOR 1 CIRCUIT HIGH should be active or pending with the jumper in place.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K171) SUPERCHARGER OUTLET PRESSURE SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Check for continuity between ground and the (K171) Supercharger Outlet Pressure Signal circuit at the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between ground and the (K171) Supercharger Outlet Pressure Signal circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K171) Supercharger Outlet Pressure Signal circuit for a short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK FOR THE (K171) SUPERCHARGER OUTLET PRESSURE SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for continuity between the (K171) Supercharger Outlet Pressure Signal circuit and the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit at the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between the (K171) Supercharger Outlet Pressure Signal circuit and the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K171) Supercharger Outlet Pressure Signal circuit for a short to the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p00fb supercharger/turbocharger outlet air pressure sensor 1 circuit high\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P00FB</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">SUPERCHARGER/TURBOCHARGER OUTLET AIR PRESSURE SENSOR 1 CIRCUIT HIGH</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">         COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                       DTCS\n         BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                             P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n   Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                       N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n              Sensor\n  Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger               P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                 P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                Supercharger\n       Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                        P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n       Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                      P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor input voltage is above the maximum acceptable threshold.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>5-VOLT SUPPLY CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET PRESSURE SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET PRESSURE SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE 5-VOLT SUPPLY CIRCUIT</li><li>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET PRESSURE SIGNAL CIRCUIT OPEN OR HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT OPEN OR HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET MAP SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK THE (F856) 5-VOLT SUPPLY CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO VOLTAGE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the voltage on the (F856) 5-Volt Supply circuit at the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage above 5.2 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (F856) 5-Volt Supply circuit for a short to voltage.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K171) SUPERCHARGER OUTLET PRESSURE SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO VOLTAGE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the voltage on the (K171) Supercharger Outlet Pressure Signal circuit at the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage above 5.2 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K171) Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor Signal circuit for a short to voltage.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK FOR THE (K171) SUPERCHARGER OUTLET PRESSURE SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE (F856) 5-VOLT SUPPLY CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Check for continuity between the (K171) Supercharger Outlet Pressure Signal circuit and the (F856) 5-Volt Supply circuit at the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between the (K171) Supercharger Outlet Pressure Signal circuit and the (F856) 5-Volt Supply circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K171) Supercharger Outlet Pressure Signal circuit for a short to the (F856) 5-Volt Supply circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>CHECK THE (K171) SUPERCHARGER OUTLET PRESSURE SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:       Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K171) Supercharger Outlet Pressure Signal between the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K171) Supercharger Outlet Pressure Signal for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>CHECK THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit between the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 7</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>SUPERCHARGER OUTLET MAP SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Reconnect the PCM harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit and the (K171) Supercharger Outlet Pressure Signal circuit at the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          The P00FA-SUPERCHARGER/TURBOCHARGER OUTLET AIR PRESSURE SENSOR 1 CIRCUIT LOW should be active or pending with the jumper in place.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 8</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">8</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor and the PCM.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform the Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0105 manifold absolute pressure sensor / barometric pressure sensor circuit\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0105</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">MANIFOLD ABSOLUTE PRESSURE SENSOR / BAROMETRIC PRESSURE SENSOR CIRCUIT</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">         COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                       DTCS\n         BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                             P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n   Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                       N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n              Sensor\n  Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger               P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                 P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                Supercharger\n       Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                        P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n       Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                      P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Engine not running.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Bank 1 MAP Sensor signal is above the calibrated upper operating voltage range or below the calibrated lower operating voltage range.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE BANK 1 MAP SIGNAL CIRCUIT</li><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>BANK 1 MAP SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          Diagnose and repair any 5-Volt Reference High or Low DTCs before continuing with this test procedure.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K37) BANK 1 MAP SENSOR SIGNAL CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:         Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K37) Bank 1 MAP Sensor Signal circuit between the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K37) Bank 1 MAP Sensor Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit between the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>BANK 1 MAP SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Replace the Bank 1 MAP Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Reconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Reconnect the Bank 1 MAP harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair is complete.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Bank 1 MAP Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0106 manifold absolute pressure sensor performance\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0106</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">MANIFOLD ABSOLUTE PRESSURE SENSOR PERFORMANCE</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">         COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                       DTCS\n         BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                             P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n   Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                       N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n              Sensor\n  Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger               P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                 P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                Supercharger\n       Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                        P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n       Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                      P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on, the Bank 1 MAP Sensor signal is compared to the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor, Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 2 MAP Sensor, and BARO Sensor signals. With the engine running, the Bank 1 MAP Sensor signal is compared to the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor and Bank 2 MAP Sensor signals.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The difference between the Bank 1 MAP Sensor signal and the other sensor signals is more than 5kPa (0.725 psi) with the ignition on, or the difference between the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor signal and Bank 2/Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor signals is more than 10kPa (1.45psi) with the engine running.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>5-VOLT SUPPLY CIRCUIT OPEN</li><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE BANK 1 MAP SIGNAL CIRCUIT</li><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>BANK 1 MAP SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          Diagnose and repair any 5-Volt Reference High or Low DTCs before continuing with this test procedure.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK THE (F856) 5-VOLT SUPPLY CIRCUIT VOLTAGE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the voltage on the (F856) 5-Volt Supply circuit at the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage above 4.9 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (F856) 5-Volt Supply circuit for an open circuit or short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K37) BANK 1 MAP SENSOR SIGNAL CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:         Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K37) Bank 1 MAP Sensor Signal circuit between the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K37) Bank 1 MAP Sensor Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit between the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>BANK 1 MAP SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Replace the Bank 1 MAP Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Reconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Reconnect the Bank 1 MAP harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair is complete.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors at the Bank 1 MAP Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, spread, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0107 manifold absolute pressure sensor circuit low\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0107</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">MANIFOLD ABSOLUTE PRESSURE SENSOR CIRCUIT LOW</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">         COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                       DTCS\n         BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                             P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n   Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                       N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n              Sensor\n  Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger               P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                 P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                Supercharger\n       Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                        P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n       Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                      P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Bank 1 MAP Sensor input voltage is below the minimum acceptable threshold.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>BANK 1 MAP SENSOR SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO GROUND</li><li>BANK 1 MAP SENSOR SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>BANK 1 MAP SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           Diagnose and repair any 5-Volt Reference High or Low DTCs before continuing with this test procedure.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>BANK 1 MAP SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (F856) 5-Volt Supply circuit and the (K37) Bank 1 MAP Sensor Signal circuit at the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The P0108-MANIFOLD ABSOLUTE PRESSURE SENSOR CIRCUIT HIGH should be active or pending with the jumper in place.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Bank 1 MAP Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K37) BANK 1 MAP SENSOR SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Check for continuity between ground and the (K37) Bank 1 MAP Sensor Signal circuit at the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between ground and the (K37) Bank 1 MAP Sensor Signal circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K37) Bank 1 MAP Sensor Signal circuit for a short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK FOR THE (K37) BANK 1 MAP SENSOR SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for continuity between the (K37) Bank 1 MAP Sensor Signal circuit and the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit at the Bank 1 MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between the (K37) Bank 1 MAP Sensor Signal circuit and the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K37) Bank 1 MAP Sensor Signal circuit for a short to the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors at the Bank 1 MAP Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Look for broken, bent, spread, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0108 manifold absolute pressure sensor circuit high\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0108</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">MANIFOLD ABSOLUTE PRESSURE SENSOR CIRCUIT HIGH</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">         COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                       DTCS\n         BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                             P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n   Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                       N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n              Sensor\n  Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger               P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                 P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                Supercharger\n       Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                        P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n       Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                      P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Bank 1 MAP Sensor input voltage is above the maximum acceptable threshold.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>5-VOLT SUPPLY CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>BANK 1 MAP SENSOR SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>BANK 1 MAP SENSOR SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE 5-VOLT SUPPLY CIRCUIT</li><li>BANK 1 MAP SENSOR SIGNAL CIRCUIT OPEN OR HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT OPEN OR HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>BANK 1 MAP SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Start the engine and allow it to idle.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">WARNING:         When the engine is operating, do not stand in direct line with the fan. Do not put your hands near the pulleys, belts or fan. Do not wear loose clothing. Failure to follow these instructions may result in possible serious or fatal injury.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK THE (F856) 5-VOLT SUPPLY CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO VOLTAGE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the voltage on the (F856) 5-Volt Supply circuit at the Manifold Absolute Pressure Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage above 5.2 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (F856) 5-Volt Supply circuit for a short to voltage.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K1) MAP SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO VOLTAGE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the voltage on the (K1) MAP Signal circuit at the MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage above 5.2 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K1) MAP Signal circuit for a short to voltage.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK FOR THE (K1) MAP SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE (F856) 5-VOLT SUPPLY CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Measure the resistance between the (K1) MAP Signal circuit and the (F856) 5-Volt Supply at the MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance above 10k Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Repair the (K1) MAP Signal circuit for a short to the (F856) 5-Volt Supply circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>CHECK THE (K1) MAP SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:       Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K1) MAP Signal between the MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K1) MAP Signal for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>CHECK THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit between the MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 7</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>MAP SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Reconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit and the (K1) MAP Signal circuit at the MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, read the MAP Sensor signal voltage.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage below 1.0 volt with the jumper in place?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the MAP Sensor in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to SENSOR, TMAP-INTAKE MANIFOLD, REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 8</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">8</span><span>CHECK RELATED PCM AND COMPONENT CONNECTIONS</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that apply.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect all PCM harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect all related in-line harness connections (if equipped).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Disconnect the related component harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Inspect harness connectors, component connectors, and all male and female terminals for the following conditions: Proper connector installation. Damaged connector locks. Corrosion. Other signs of water intrusion. Weather seal damage (if equipped). Bent terminals. Overheating due to a poor connection (terminal may be discolored due to excessive current draw). Terminals that have been pushed back into the connector cavity. Check for spread terminals and verify proper terminal tension.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Repair any conditions that are found.</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Reconnect all PCM harness connectors. Be certain that all harness connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>Reconnect all in-line harness connectors (if equipped). Be certain that all connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">8</span><span>Reconnect all related component harness connectors. Be certain that all connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">9</span><span>With the scan tool, erase DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">10</span><span>Test drive or operate the vehicle in accordance with the when monitored and set conditions.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">11</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Powertrain Control Module in accordance with the Service information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST . Test complete.</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0109-00 manifold absolute pressure sensor circuit intermittent\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0109-00</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">MANIFOLD ABSOLUTE PRESSURE SENSOR CIRCUIT INTERMITTENT</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously with the ignition on.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Barometric Sensor is functioning but exhibits an intermittent or rapidly changing barometric pressure signal.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">The BARO Sensor is located inside the PCM. Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0111 intake air temperature sensor 1 performance\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0111</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">INTAKE AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR 1 PERFORMANCE</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">         COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                       DTCS\n         BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                             P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n   Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                       N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n              Sensor\n  Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger               P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                 P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                Supercharger\n       Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                        P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n       Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                      P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the engine off time greater than 480 minutes. Ambient temperature greater than -7°C (19.4°F).</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">After a calibrated amount of cool down time, the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) compares the Ambient Air Temperature (AAT), Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT), and Intake Air Temperature (IAT) Sensor values. If one sensor value is not within 15°C (59°F) of the other temperature sensors, a DTC will set.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE IAT SIGNAL CIRCUIT</li><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>MASS AIRFLOW (MAF) SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE CONDITION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Allow the vehicle to sit with the ignition off in an environment where the temperature is consistent and above -7°C (19.4°F) until the engine coolant temperature is equal to ambient temperature.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With a scan tool, compare the IAT Sensor value with the other temperature sensor signals.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the IAT Sensor value within 15°C (59°F) of the other sensor values?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K21) IAT SIGNAL CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the MAF Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K21) IAT Signal circuit between the MAF Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K21) IAT Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit between the MAF Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K900) Sensor Ground for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>INTAKE AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, read the IAT Sensor signal voltage.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit and the (K21) IAT Signal circuit in the MAF Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>With the scan tool, read the IAT Sensor signal voltage.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The voltage should read 5.0 volts with the connector unplugged and below 0.1 volts with the jumper in place.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the voltage read as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Intake Air Temperature Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the MAF Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0112 intake air temperature sensor 1 circuit low\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0112</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">INTAKE AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR 1 CIRCUIT LOW</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">         COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                       DTCS\n         BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                             P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n   Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                       N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n              Sensor\n  Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger               P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                 P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                Supercharger\n       Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                        P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n       Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                      P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Intake Air Temperature (IAT) Sensor input voltage is below the minimum acceptable threshold.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>IAT SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO GROUND</li><li>IAT SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE SENSOR GROUND</li><li>MASS AIRFLOW SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>MAF SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the MAF Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With a scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The P0113-INTAKE AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR 1 CIRCUIT HIGH DTC should be active or pending with the MAF Sensor harness connector unplugged.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the Mass Airflow Sensor and Powertrain Control Module connectors. Replace the Mass Airflow Sensor if no problems were found in the connectors.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K21) IAT SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Check for continuity between ground and the (K21) IAT Signal circuit at the MAF Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between ground and the (K21) IAT Signal circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K21) IAT Signal circuit for a short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK FOR THE (K21) IAT SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for continuity between the (K21) IAT Signal circuit and the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit in the MAF Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between the (K21) IAT Signal circuit and the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K21) IAT Signal circuit for a short to the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the MAF Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0113 intake air temperature sensor 1 circuit high\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0113</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">INTAKE AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR 1 CIRCUIT HIGH</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">         COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                       DTCS\n         BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                             P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n   Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                       N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n              Sensor\n  Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger               P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                 P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                Supercharger\n       Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                        P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n       Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                      P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Intake Air Temperature (IAT) Sensor input voltage is above the maximum acceptable threshold.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>POSSIBLE CAUSES</li><li>IAT SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>IAT SIGNAL CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>MASS AIR FLOW (MAF) SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK THE (K21) IAT SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO VOLTAGE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the MAF Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the voltage on the (K21) IAT Signal circuit at the MAF Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage above 5.2 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K21) IAT Signal circuit for a short to voltage.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K21) IAT SIGNAL FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K21) IAT Signal circuit between the MAF Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K21) IAT Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit between the MAF Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>INTAKE AIR TEMPERATURE SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Reconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (K21) IAT Signal circuit and the (K900) Sensor Ground at the MAF Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCS.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          The P0112-INTAKE AIR TEMPERATURE 1 CIRCUIT LOW DTC should be active or pending with the jumper in place.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the MAF Sensor in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to SENSOR, AIR TEMPERATURE, INLET, REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the MAF Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0116 engine coolant temperature sensor 1 performance\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0116</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">ENGINE COOLANT TEMPERATURE SENSOR 1 PERFORMANCE</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) Sensor is a variable resistor that functions as a normal two wire, 5-Volt sensor. The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) supplies the ECT Sensor with a 5-Volt reference and a filtered ground (return) circuit. For the ECT Sensor diagnostic, the PCM internally monitors the voltage on the signal circuit, which is converted into a temperature reading. If the voltage reads above the high calibrated threshold, or below the low calibrated threshold, a circuit high or low fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Engine off time is greater than 480 minutes and the vehicle has been driven for one minute over 35 mph. Ambient temperature is greater than -64°C (-83°F).</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">After the enable conditions above are met, the PCM compares the Ambient, Engine Coolant and Intake Air Temperature Sensor values. If Ambient Air and Intake Air Temperature Sensors agree with each other but Engine Coolant Temperature does not agree with them, the Engine Coolant Temperature Sensor is declared as irrational.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE ECT SIGNAL CIRCUIT</li><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>ENGINE COOLANT TEMPERATURE (ECT) SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>ENGINE COOLANT TEMPERATURE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Allow the vehicle to sit with the ignition off in an environment where the temperature is consistent and above -64°C (-83°F) until the engine coolant temperature is equal to ambient temperature.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, compare the AAT, ECT and IAT sensor values.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the Engine Coolant Temperature Sensor value within 15°C (59°F) of the other two sensor values?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K2) ECT SIGNAL CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the ECT Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K2) ECT Signal circuit between the ECT Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K2) ECT Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE (K915) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K915) Sensor Ground circuit between the ECT Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K915) Sensor ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>ECT SENSOR VOLTAGE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Reconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>With the scan tool, read the ECT Sensor voltage.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The sensor voltage should be approximately 5.0 volts (plus or minus 0.1 volts) with the connector disconnected.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (K2) ECT Signal circuit and the (K915) Sensor Ground circuit AT the Engine Coolant Temperature Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The sensor voltage should be approximately 0.0 volts (plus or minus 0.1 volts) with the jumper in place.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display the voltage as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the ECT Sensor in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to SENSOR, COOLANT TEMPERATURE, REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the ECT Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals. Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the ECT Sensor and Powertrain Control Module connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were there any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0117 engine coolant temperature sensor circuit low\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0117</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">ENGINE COOLANT TEMPERATURE SENSOR CIRCUIT LOW</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) Sensor is a variable resistor that functions as a normal two wire, 5-Volt sensor. The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) supplies the ECT Sensor with a 5-Volt reference and a filtered ground (return) circuit. For the ECT Sensor diagnostic, the PCM internally monitors the voltage on the signal circuit, which is converted into a temperature reading. If the voltage reads above the high calibrated threshold, or below the low calibrated threshold, a circuit high or low fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Engine Coolant Temperature Sensor input voltage is below the minimum acceptable value</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate. The ETC light will illuminate. The Throttle input and vehicle speed are limited.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>ECT SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO GROUND</li><li>ECT SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>ENGINE COOLANT TEMPERATURE (ECT) SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           Diagnose and repair any system voltage DTCs before continuing with this test.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>ECT SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the ECT Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, read the ECT Sensor voltage.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The sensor voltage should be approximately 5.0 volts (plus or minus 0.1 volts) with the connector disconnected.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display the voltage as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Engine Coolant Temperature Sensor in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to SENSOR, COOLANT TEMPERATURE, REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K2) ECT SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Check for continuity between ground and the (K2) ECT Signal circuit at the ECT Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between ground and the (K2) ECT Signal circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K2) ECT Signal circuit for a short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK FOR THE (K2) ECT SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE (K915) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for continuity between the (K2) ECT Signal circuit and the (K915) Sensor Ground circuit at the ECT Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between the (K2) ECT Signal circuit and the (K915) Sensor Ground circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the short to between the (K2) ECT Signal circuit and the (K915) Sensor Ground circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the ECT Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals. Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the ECT Sensor and Powertrain Control Module connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Were there any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0118 engine coolant temperature sensor 1 circuit high\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0118</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">ENGINE COOLANT TEMPERATURE SENSOR 1 CIRCUIT HIGH</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) sensor is a variable resistor that measures the temperature of the engine coolant. The ECT Sensor functions as a typical two wire 5 volt sensor. The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) supplies 5 volts to the ECT Sensor signal circuit and supplies a ground to the low reference circuit. The Powertrain Control Module uses an internal voltmeter to read the signal circuit voltage which it then uses to calculate the engine coolant temperature.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Engine Coolant Temperature Sensor input voltage is above the maximum acceptable threshold.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate. The ETC light will illuminate. The Throttle input and vehicle speed are limited.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>ECT SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>ECT SIGNAL CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>ENGINE COOLANT TEMPERATURE (ECT) SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          Diagnose and repair any system voltage DTCs before continuing with this test.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK THE (K2) ECT SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO VOLTAGE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the ECT Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the voltage on the (K2) ECT Signal circuit in the ECT Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage above 5.2 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the short to voltage in the (K2) ECT Signal circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K2) ECT SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K2) ECT Signal circuit between the ECT Sensor harness connector to the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K2) ECT Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE (K915) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K915) Sensor ground circuit between the ECT Sensor harness connector to the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K915) Sensor ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>ECT SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Reconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (K2) ECT Signal circuit and the (K915) Sensor ground circuit in the ECT harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, read ECT voltage.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The P0117-ENGINE COOLANT TEMPERATURE SENSOR CIRCUIT LOW DTC should display as active or pending with the jumper in place.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the ECT Sensor and Powertrain Control Module connectors. If no problems are found, replace the Engine Coolant Temperature Sensor. Refer to SENSOR, COOLANT TEMPERATURE, REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           Remove the jumper before continuing.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the ECT Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals. Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the ECT Sensor and Powertrain Control Module connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were there any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0121 throttle position sensor 1 performance\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0121</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">THROTTLE POSITION SENSOR 1 PERFORMANCE</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Electronic Throttle Control (ETC) system uses two Throttle Position Sensors (TPS) to monitor the throttle blade position. TPS 1 and TPS 2 are located within the Throttle Body assembly. Each sensor has its own Signal circuit (TPS 1 or TPS 2). Both sensors share a common 5-Volt Reference circuit (Supply) and a common Sensor Ground. Each Signal circuit provides the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) with a signal voltage proportionate to throttle blade movement. Normally operating sensor signals are opposing to each other in the following manner:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">TPS 1 voltage starts low, approximately 0.5 volts at closed throttle, and increases to approximately 4.5 volts at wide open throttle . TPS 2 voltage starts high, approximately 4.3 volts at closed throttle, and decreases to approximately 0.7 volts at wide open throttle .</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the engine running. P2135 DTC is active.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">When fault P2135 is active, the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) looks at the difference between calculated MAP based on TPS 1 input and sensed MAP. The same is done for TPS</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>If the PCM determines that the TPS 1 error is larger than the TPS 2 error, this fault is set.</span></div></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate. The ETC light will illuminate. The Throttle input and vehicle speed are limited.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE TPS 1 SIGNAL CIRCUIT</li><li>THROTTLE BODY</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           Diagnose and repair any Throttle Body circuit, system voltage or 5-Volt Reference DTCs before continuing with this test.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Start the engine and allow it to idle.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">WARNING:         When the engine is operating, do not stand in direct line with the fan. Do not put your hands near the pulleys, belts or fan. Do not wear loose clothing. Failure to follow these instructions may result in possible serious or fatal injury.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:            If the DTC does not reset it may be necessary to test drive the vehicle.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>THROTTLE FOLLOWER TEST</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, perform the ETC Throttle Follower Test and monitor the TPS 1 or TPS 2 voltage.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Slowly press the accelerator pedal down.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>The voltage for TPS 1 should start at approximately 0.7 volts and increase to approximately 4.3 volts.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the TPS 1 voltage within the range of the listed specification?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K22) TPS 1 SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Throttle Body harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:         Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit between the Throttle Body harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the excessive resistance in the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE (F855) 5-VOLT SUPPLY CIRCUIT FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (F855) 5-Volt Supply circuit between the Throttle Body harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the excessive resistance in the (F855) 5-Volt Supply circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>CHECK THE (K922) TPS RETURN CIRCUIT FOR EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K922) TPS Return circuit between the Throttle Body harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the excessive resistance in the (K922) TPS Return circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>THROTTLE POSITION SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Reconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>With the scan tool, monitor the TPS 1 voltage.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit and the (F855) 5-Volt Supply circuit.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The TPS 1 voltage should change from approximately 0.0 volts with the connector unplugged to 5.0 volts with the jumper in place.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage reading within the listed specification when the jumper is installed?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the Throttle Body and Powertrain Control Module connectors. If no problems are found, replace the Throttle Body Assembly. Refer to THROTTLE BODY, REMOVAL . Disconnect the Battery when replacing the Throttle Body Assembly. After installation is complete, use a scan tool and select the ETC RELEARN function.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 7</p><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           Remove the jumper before continuing.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Throttle Body and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals. Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the Throttle Body and Powertrain Control Module connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Perform any y apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were there any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0122 throttle position sensor 1 circuit low\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0122</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">THROTTLE POSITION SENSOR 1 CIRCUIT LOW</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Electronic Throttle Control (ETC) system uses two Throttle Position Sensors (TPS) to monitor the throttle blade position. TPS 1 and TPS 2 are located within the Throttle Body assembly. Each sensor has its own Signal circuit (TPS 1 or TPS 2). Both sensors share a common 5-Volt Reference circuit (Supply) and a common Sensor Ground. Each Signal circuit provides the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) with a signal voltage proportionate to throttle blade movement. Normally operating sensor signals are opposing to each other in the following manner:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">TPS 1 voltage starts low, approximately 0.5 volts at closed throttle, and increases to approximately 4.5 volts at wide open throttle . TPS 2 voltage starts high, approximately 4.3 volts at closed throttle, and decreases to approximately 0.7 volts at wide open throttle .</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Battery voltage greater than 6.98 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The TPS 1 Sensor input voltage is below 0.45 volts for 0.07 seconds</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate. The ETC light will illuminate. The Throttle input and vehicle speed are limited.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>TPS SIGNAL 1 CIRCUIT SHORTED TO GROUND</li><li>TPS SIGNAL 1 CIRCUIT SHORTED TO TPS SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>TPS SIGNAL 1 CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE ETC MOTOR (-) CIRCUIT</li><li>TPS SIGNAL 1 CIRCUIT OPEN OR HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>THROTTLE BODY</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           Diagnose and repair any system voltage or 5-Volt Reference DTCs before continuing with this test.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>THROTTLE FOLLOWER TEST</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>With the scan tool, perform the ETC Throttle Follower Test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Monitor the TPS 1 voltage.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the voltage start at approximately 0.7 volts and increase to approximately 4.3 volts with a smooth transition?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K22) TPS 1 SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Throttle Body harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Check for continuity between ground and the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit at the TPS harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between ground and the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit for a short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE (K22) TPS 1 SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO THE (K922) TPS RETURN CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for continuity between the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit and the (K922) TPS Return circuit at the Throttle Body harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit and the (K922) TPS Return circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the short between the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit and the (K922) TPS Return circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>CHECK THE (K22) TPS 1 SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO THE (K126) ETC MOTOR (-) CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for continuity between the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit and the (K126) ETC Motor (-) circuit at the Throttle Body harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit and the (K126) ETC Motor (-) circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the short between the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit and the (K126) ETC Motor (-) circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Go To 6</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>CHECK THE (K22) TPS 1 SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:       Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit between the Throttle Body harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 7</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the excessive resistance in the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>THROTTLE POSITION SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Reconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (F855) 5-Volt Supply circuit and the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit at the Throttle Body harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The P0123-TPS 1 CIRCUIT HIGH DTC should be active or pending with the jumper in place.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the Throttle Body and Powertrain Control Module connectors. If no problems are found, replace the Throttle Body Assembly. Refer to THROTTLE BODY, REMOVAL . Disconnect the Battery when replacing the Throttle Body Assembly. After installation is complete, using a scan tool select ETC RELEARN function.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 8</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">8</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Throttle Body and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals. Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the Throttle Body and Powertrain Control Module connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were there any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0123 throttle position sensor 1 circuit high\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0123</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">THROTTLE POSITION SENSOR 1 CIRCUIT HIGH</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Electronic Throttle Control (ETC) system uses two Throttle Position Sensors (TPS) to monitor the throttle blade position. TPS 1 and TPS 2 are located within the Throttle Body assembly. Each sensor has its own Signal circuit (TPS 1 or TPS 2). Both sensors share a common 5-Volt Reference circuit (Supply) and a common Sensor Ground. Each Signal circuit provides the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) with a signal voltage proportionate to throttle blade movement. Normally operating sensor signals are opposing to each other in the following manner:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">TPS 1 voltage starts low, approximately 0.5 volts at closed throttle, and increases to approximately 4.5 volts at wide open throttle . TPS 2 voltage starts high, approximately 4.3 volts at closed throttle, and decreases to approximately 0.7 volts at wide open throttle .</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Battery voltage greater than 6.98 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Throttle Body input voltage is above 4.8 volts for 0.07 seconds.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate. The ETC light will illuminate. The Throttle input and vehicle speed are limited.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>5-VOLT SUPPLY CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>TPS SIGNAL 1 CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>TPS SIGNAL 1 CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE 5-VOLT SUPPLY CIRCUIT</li><li>TPS SIGNAL 1 CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE ETC MOTOR (+) CIRCUIT</li><li>TPS SIGNAL 1 CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE ETC MOTOR (-) CIRCUIT</li><li>TPS SIGNAL 1 CIRCUIT OPEN OR HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>TPS SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT OPEN OR HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>THROTTLE BODY</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           If P2101 is also set, make sure that the blade moves freely and does not hang up near Wide Open Throttle before trying to repair P0123.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Start the engine and allow it to idle.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">WARNING:         When the engine is operating, do not stand in direct line with the fan. Do not put your hands near the pulleys, belts or fan. Do not wear loose clothing. Failure to follow these instructions may result in possible serious or fatal injury.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>THROTTLE FOLLOWER TEST</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, perform the ETC Throttle Follower Test and monitor the TPS 1 or TPS 2 voltage.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Slowly press the accelerator pedal down.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>The voltage for TPS 1 should start at approximately 0.7 volts and increase to approximately 4.3 volts.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the TPS 1 voltage within the range of the listed specification?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K22) TPS 1 SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO VOLTAGE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Throttle Body harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the voltage on the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit at the Throttle Body harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage reading above 5.2 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit for a short to voltage.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE (K22) TPS 1 SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:         Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit from the Throttle Body harness connector to the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>CHECK FOR THE (K22) TPS 1 SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE (F855) 5-VOLT SUPPLY CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for continuity between the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit and the (F855) 5-Volt Supply circuit in the Throttle Body harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit and the (F855) 5-Volt Supply circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the short between the (F855) 5-Volt Supply circuit and the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>CHECK THE (K922) TPS RETURN CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K922) TPS Return circuit from the Throttle Body harness connector to the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 7</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K922) TPS Return circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>THROTTLE POSITION SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Reconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on, engine not running.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (K22) TPS 1 Signal circuit and the (K922) TPS Return circuit at the Throttle Body harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The scan tool should display a P0122-THROTTLE POSITION SENSOR 1 CIRCUIT LOW DTC as active or pending with the jumper in place.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display the DTC as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the Throttle Body and Powertrain Control Module connectors. If no problems are found, replace the Throttle Body Assembly. Disconnect the Battery when replacing the Throttle Body Assembly. After installation is complete, using a scan tool select the ETC RELEARN function. Refer to THROTTLE BODY, REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 8</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">8</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Throttle Body and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals. Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the Throttle Body and Powertrain Control Module connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were there any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0125 insufficient coolant temp for closed - loop fuel control\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0125</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">INSUFFICIENT COOLANT TEMP FOR CLOSED - LOOP FUEL CONTROL</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">At initial engine start up. With battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The engine temperature does not go above 10°C (50°F). Failure time depends on start-up coolant temperature and ambient temperature. Up to two minutes for a start temp of 1°C (33.8°F), up to five minutes for a vehicle with a start-up temp between 0°C - 29°C (32°F - 20.2°F) or up to 10 minutes for a vehicle with a start-up temp of -30°C (-22°F).</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>LOW COOLANT LEVEL</li><li>THERMOSTAT</li><li>ENGINE COOLANT TEMPERATURE (ECT) SENSOR</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK THE COOLANT LEVEL AND CONDITION</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:              It may be necessary to drive the vehicle to meet the conditions to set this DTC, try to repeat the conditions in which the fault originally set by reviewing the Freeze Frame data.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:              The best way to diagnose this DTC is to allow the vehicle to sit overnight outside in order to have a totally cold soaked engine.</div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">WARNING:           Never open the cooling system when the engine is hot. The system is under pressure. Extreme burns or scalding may result. Failure to follow these instructions can result in possible serious or fatal injury. Allow the engine to cool before opening the cooling system.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Inspect the cooling system for proper level and condition.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the coolant level and condition OK?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Inspect the vehicle for a coolant leak, make the appropriate repairs and add the correct amount of coolant. Refer to TECHNICAL SPECIFICATIONS .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>THERMOSTAT OPERATION</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:              This test works best if performed on a cold engine (cold soak).</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read the Engine Coolant Temperature degree value. If the engine was allowed to sit overnight (cold soak), the temperature value should be a sensible value that is somewhere close to the ambient temperature.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           If engine coolant temperature is above 82°C (180°F), allow the engine to cool until 65°C (150°F) is reached.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Start the engine.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>During engine warm-up monitor the Engine Coolant Temperature degree value. The temperature value change should be a smooth transition from start up to normal operating temp 82°C (180°F). Also monitor the actual coolant temperature with a digital thermometer at or near the ECT Sensor and Thermostat positions.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           As the engine warms up to operating temperature, the actual coolant temperature (thermometer reading) and the scan tool Engine Coolant Temperature degree values should stay relatively close to each other.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Using the appropriate Service Information, determine the proper opening temperature of the thermostat.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Did the thermostat open at the proper temperature?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Replace the thermostat in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to THERMOSTAT, REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>ECT SENSOR OPERATION</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Was the digital thermometer readings relatively close to the scan tool reading for the ECT Sensor?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Replace the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) Sensor in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to SENSOR, COOLANT TEMPERATURE, REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>COOLING SYSTEM OPERATION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check the temperature reading at the inlet and outlet of the Radiator.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           It is normal to see a lower temperature on the outlet of the Radiator, however a very large difference indicates a restriction in the Radiator core. This can cause this DTC to set due to the position on the ETC Sensor in the system.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Was the difference in the temperature readings relatively larger than expected?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Radiator in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to RADIATOR, ENGINE COOLING, REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Powertrain Control Module in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0128 thermostat rationality\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0128</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">THERMOSTAT RATIONALITY</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the engine running. Start up coolant temperature less than 65°C (149°F). Ambient temperature between -8°C (17.6°F) and 50°C (122°F). Average vehicle speed greater than 16 kph (10 mph) until coolant temperature reaches 85°C. Engine speed does not go above 5400 rpm for more than 32 seconds. The pass/fail determination point is reached when the coolant temp model reaches 88°C (190.4°F).</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The predicted coolant temperature reaches the target threshold before the actual coolant temperature and the PCM detects that the actual engine coolant temperature is too far below the what is acceptable for a good thermostat.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>LOW COOLANT LEVEL</li><li>COOLING SYSTEM ISSUES</li><li>THERMOSTAT OPERATION</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>LOW COOLANT LEVEL</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           If any ECT or AAT DTCs are present, diagnose and repair those DTCs before continuing with this test procedure.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The best way to diagnose this DTC is to allow the vehicle to cold soak until the engine is at ambient temperature.</div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">WARNING:        Never open the cooling system when the engine is hot. The system is under pressure. Failure to follow these instructions can result in personal injury including extreme burns, scalding, or fatal injury. Allow the engine to cool before opening the cooling system.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check the cooling system to make sure that the coolant is in good condition and at the proper level. Refer to DIAGNOSIS AND TESTING .</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the coolant level and condition OK?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Inspect the vehicle for a coolant leak and add the necessary amount of coolant. Refer to TECHNICAL SPECIFICATIONS .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK THE COOLING SYSTEM OPERATION</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           If engine coolant temperature is above 65°C (149°F), allow the engine to cool until it is below 65°C (149°F).</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Start the engine.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Check for proper operation of the cooling system while the engine warms up toward operating temperature. Be certain to check the Radiator Cap, flow through the Radiator and anything that can cause a lack of system pressure or lack of coolant flow in the system.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any issues found with the cooling system?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the appropriate repair in accordance with the service information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Replace the thermostat in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to THERMOSTAT, REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p012a turbocharger/supercharger inlet pressure sensor 1\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P012A</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">TURBOCHARGER/SUPERCHARGER INLET PRESSURE SENSOR 1</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">          COMPONENT                                 LOCATION                         CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                     CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                     DTCS\n         BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                           P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                 N/A\n   Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                     N/A                                        P0111, P0112, P0113\n              Sensor\n  Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger             P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the               P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                Supercharger\n       Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                      P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n       Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                    P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:              The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:              The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Engine not running.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor signal is above the calibrated upper operating voltage range or below the calibrated lower operating voltage range.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE SUPERCHARGER INLET MAP SIGNAL CIRCUIT</li><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>SUPERCHARGER INLET MAP SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          Diagnose and repair any 5-Volt Reference High or Low DTCs before continuing with this test procedure.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK FOR HIGH RESISTANCE IN THE (K1) SUPERCHARGER INLET MAP SIGNAL CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:         Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K1) Supercharger Inlet MAP Signal circuit between the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K1) Supercharger Inlet MAP Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK FOR HIGH RESISTANCE IN THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit between the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>SUPERCHARGER INLET MAP SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Replace the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Reconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Reconnect the Supercharger Inlet Pressure harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replacing the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor repaired the fault.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p012b turbocharger/supercharger inlet pressure sensor 1 circuit performance\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P012B</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">TURBOCHARGER/SUPERCHARGER INLET PRESSURE SENSOR 1 CIRCUIT PERFORMANCE</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">         COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                       DTCS\n         BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                             P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n   Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                       N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n              Sensor\n  Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger               P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                 P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                Supercharger\n       Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                        P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n       Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                      P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">With the ignition on, the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor signal is compared to the Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor, Bank 1 MAP Sensor, Bank 2 MAP Sensor and BARO Sensor signals.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">With the engine running, if the P0069 or P1613 diagnostics have failed and become active, the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor signal is compared to the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP Sensor and Bank 2 MAP Sensor signals.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The difference between the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor signal and the other Sensor signals is more than 5 kPa (0.725 psi). The difference between the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor signal and the other three MAP/Pressure Sensor signals is more than 14.7 kPa (2.13 psi) during naturally aspirated engine running conditions.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>5-VOLT SUPPLY CIRCUIT OPEN</li><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE SUPERCHARGER INLET MAP SIGNAL CIRCUIT</li><li>EXCESSIVE RESISTANCE IN THE SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>SUPERCHARGER INLET MAP SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           Diagnose and repair any 5-Volt Reference High or Low DTCs before continuing with this test procedure.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK THE (F856) 5-VOLT SUPPLY CIRCUIT VOLTAGE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the voltage on the (F856) 5-Volt Supply circuit at the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage above 4.9 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (F856) 5-Volt Supply circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK FOR HIGH RESISTANCE IN THE (K1) SUPERCHARGER INLET MAP SIGNAL CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K1) Supercharger Inlet MAP Signal circuit between the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K1) Supercharger Inlet MAP Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK FOR HIGH RESISTANCE IN THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit between the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>SUPERCHARGER INLET MAP SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Replace the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Reconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Reconnect the Supercharger Inlet Pressure harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replacing the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor repaired the fault.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p012c turbocharger/supercharger inlet air pressure sensor 1 circuit low\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P012C</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">TURBOCHARGER/SUPERCHARGER INLET AIR PRESSURE SENSOR 1 CIRCUIT LOW</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">         COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                       DTCS\n         BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                             P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n   Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                       N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n              Sensor\n  Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger               P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                 P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                Supercharger\n       Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                        P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n       Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                      P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor input voltage is below the minimum acceptable threshold.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>SUPERCHARGER INLET MAP SENSOR SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO GROUND</li><li>SUPERCHARGER INLET MAP SENSOR SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE SENSOR GROUND</li><li>SUPERCHARGER INLET MAP SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>SUPERCHARGER INLET MAP SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With a scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The P012D-TURBOCHARGER/SUPERCHARGER INLET AIR PRESSURE SENSOR CIRCUIT 1 HIGH DTC should be active or pending with the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor harness connector unplugged.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor and Powertrain Control Module connectors. Replace the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor if no problems were found in the connectors.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K1) SUPERCHARGER INLET MAP SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Check for continuity between ground and the (K1) Supercharger Inlet MAP Signal circuit at the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between ground and the (K1) Supercharger Inlet MAP Signal circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K1) Supercharger Inlet MAP Signal circuit for a short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK FOR THE (K1) SUPERCHARGER INLET MAP SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for continuity between the (K1) Supercharger Inlet MAP Signal circuit and the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit in the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between the (K1) Supercharger Inlet MAP Signal circuit and the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K21) Supercharger Inlet MAP Signal circuit for a short to the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p012d turbocharger/supercharger inlet air pressure sensor 1 circuit high\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P012D</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">TURBOCHARGER/SUPERCHARGER INLET AIR PRESSURE SENSOR 1 CIRCUIT HIGH</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><pre class=\"en-pre\">         COMPONENT                                   LOCATION                          CORRELATING PRESSURE SENSOR                      CORRELATING TEMP SENSOR DTCS\n                                                                                                       DTCS\n         BARO SENSOR                          Inside the PCM                             P0109, P2227, P2228, P2229, P3032                                  N/A\n   Intake Air Temperature (IAT)          Before the Throttle Body                                       N/A                                         P0111, P0112, P0113\n              Sensor\n  Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor Between the Throttle Body and Supercharger               P0069, P012A, P012B, P012C, P012D                         P0096, P0097, P0098\n Supercharger Outlet MAP Sensor   On the Intake Manifold directly after the                 P00F8, P00F9, P00FA, P00FB                            P00E9, P00EA, P00EB\n                                                Supercharger\n       Bank 1 MAP Sensor             Right side of the Intake Manifold                        P0105, P0106, P0107, P0108                           P07B, P007C, P007D\n       Bank 2 MAP Sensor              Left side of the Intake Manifold                      P2A0A, P2A0B, P2A0C, P2A0D                            P00A1, P00A2, P00A3</pre><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The MAF Sensor was eliminated starting in model years. It has been replaced by a stand-alone Intake Air Sensor that was previously part of the MAF Sensor.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The four MAP Sensors also have an integral air temperature sensor. The temperature sensors have a 5.0 volt signal circuit and share the sensor ground circuit with the pressure sensor. They do not share the 5-Volt Reference circuit with the pressure sensor and therefore operate as a typical two wire temperature sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The 6.2L engine uses several sensors to monitor the air pressure and temperature throughout the intake air system. The PCM uses this information to help calculate fuel delivery, ignition timing and Supercharger control. The PCM runs diagnostics to check the rationality of the sensors. The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is the centralized sensor in the system and is the main sensor used for the rationality diagnostics of the airflow before and after the Throttle Body.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Individual MAP/Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">At ignition on, engine not running, the BARO Sensor and four MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals are compared. If one of the MAP/Pressure Sensor input signals has a difference of more than a calibrated threshold from the others, a performance fault is set against that sensor. With the engine running, the input signals of the three MAP/Pressure Sensors that are downstream of the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor (Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, Bank 2 MAP) are compared. If one of the sensor input signals has more than a 10.0 kPa (1.5 psi) difference from the other two, a performance fault is set against that sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">BARO/Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal is compared to the BARO signal to check rationality of the sensors. During natural aspiration, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal cannot be greater than the BARO signal plus 10.3 kPa (1.49 psi). During boost conditions, the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor signal and BARO signal pressures should be similar. If the diagnostic fails during a drive cycle, a fault is set (P0069) and the secondary Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic is run to determine which sensor is irrational.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor rationality diagnostic check:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">If either of the first two diagnostics fail during a given trip, or if one of the downstream MAP/Pressure Sensors is determined to be irrational, the PCM runs a secondary diagnostic to determine if the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is rational. With the engine in a natural aspiration condition (not boosted), the PCM compares the signal input from the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor to the signal inputs from the Supercharger Outlet Pressure Sensor, Bank 1 MAP, and Bank 2 MAP Sensors. If the Supercharger Inlet Pressure Sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the other three MAP/Pressure Sensors, it is determined to be irrational and a fault is set (P012B).</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Temperature sensor rationality checks:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">After a cold soak of the engine, the Engine Coolant Temperature (ECT) and Ambient Air Temperature (AAT) Sensor inputs are compared to the inputs form each of the temperature sensors located in the MAP Sensors. If a temperature sensor is not within a calibrated threshold of the ECT or AAT Sensors, a performance fault is set.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ignition on. Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Supercharger Inlet Pressure MAP Sensor input voltage is above the maximum acceptable threshold.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>POSSIBLE CAUSES</li><li>SUPERCHARGER INLET MAP SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>SUPERCHARGER INLET MAP SIGNAL CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>SUPERCHARGER INLET MAP SENSOR</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT DTC diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK THE (K1) SUPERCHARGER INLET MAP SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO VOLTAGE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the voltage on the (K1) Supercharger Inlet MAP Signal circuit at the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage above 5.2 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K1) Supercharger Inlet MAP Signal circuit for a short to voltage.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K1) SUPERCHARGER INLET MAP SIGNAL FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K1) Supercharger Inlet MAP Signal circuit between the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K1) Supercharger Inlet MAP Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE (K900) SENSOR GROUND CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit between the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K900) Sensor Ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>SUPERCHARGER INLET MAP SENSOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Reconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect a jumper between the (K1) Supercharger Inlet MAP Signal circuit and the (K900) Sensor Ground at the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCS.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          The P012C-TURBOCHARGER/SUPERCHARGER INLET AIR PRESSURE SENSOR CIRCUIT 1 LOW DTC should be active or pending with the jumper in place.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the scan tool display as described above?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors at the Supercharger Inlet MAP Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Look for broken, bent, spread, pushed out or corroded terminals.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Monitor the scan tool data relative to this circuit and wiggle test the wiring and connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Look for the data to change or for the DTC to reset during the wiggle test.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0133 o2 sensor 1/1 slow response\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0133</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 SENSOR 1/1 SLOW RESPONSE</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Upstream Oxygen (O2) Sensor is usually placed close to the exhaust manifold to provide immediate O2 feedback to allow the fuel system to enter closed loop fuel control.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Upstream O2 Sensor is very important for emission control and engine performance since it can provide immediate air/fuel ratio feedback. Therefore, proper operation of this Upstream O2 Sensor is critical. An aged O2 Sensor tends to be less active and has a slower response rate to changes in the air/fuel ratio of exhaust gas. As a result, the quality of the O2 Sensor can be determined by observing the voltage activity from the sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This monitor is triggered when the engine is running at certain MAP/RPM windows, airflow is relatively stable and steady O2 control is applied. The state change for Upstream O2 signal is captured in a calibrated time block (consisting of consecutive samples), such that even the slowest (but still considered as passing) O2 Sensor should accumulate enough state changes to pass the monitor.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the ECT above 70°C (158°F). Engine speed between 1400 and 2300 rpm. Vehicle speed between 64 and 96 km/h (40 and 60 mph). Engine run time greater than three minutes.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Oxygen Sensor signal does not switch adequately during monitoring.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>EXHAUST LEAK</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/1 SIGNAL CIRCUIT</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/1 RETURN CIRCUIT</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/1</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE DTC</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           Check for contaminants that may have damaged the O2 Sensor: contaminated fuel, unapproved silicone, oil and coolant.</div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           After the repairs have been made, verify proper O2 Sensor operation. If all the O2 Sensor voltage readings have not returned to normal, follow the diagnostic procedure for the remaining O2 Sensors.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Start the engine.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Allow the engine to reach normal operating temperature.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">WARNING:        When the engine is operating, do not stand in direct line with the fan. Do not put your hands near the pulleys, belts or fan. Do not wear loose clothing. Failure to follow these instructions may result in possible serious or fatal injury.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs and record on the repair order.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Record the Freeze Frame Data.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           It may be necessary to drive the vehicle to meet the conditions to set this DTC, try to repeat the conditions in which the fault originally set by reviewing the Freeze Frame data.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the DTC active or pending?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECKING THE EXHAUST SYSTEM FOR LEAKS</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Perform the CHECKING THE EXHAUST SYSTEM FOR LEAKS test procedure. Refer to CHECKING THE EXHAUST SYSTEM FOR LEAKS .</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any exhaust leaks found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair or replace the leaking exhaust parts as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K41) O2 SENSOR 1/1 SIGNAL CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the O2 Sensor 1/1 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Measure the voltage on the (K41) O2 Sensor 1/1 Signal circuit in the O2 Sensor 1/1 harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage between 4.1 and 5.0 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Check the (K41) O2 Sensor 1/1 Signal circuit for a short to ground, open, or short to voltage. If OK, replace and program the Powertrain Control Module in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE (K902) O2 SENSOR 1/1 RETURN CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the voltage on the (K902) O2 Sensor 1/1 Return circuit in the O2 Sensor 1/1 harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage at 2.5 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Check the (K902) O2 Sensor 1/1 Return circuit for a short to ground, open, or short to voltage. If OK, replace and program the Powertrain Control Module in accordance with the Service Information.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>O2 SENSOR 1/1</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           Check for signs of contaminants that may have damaged the O2 Sensor, such as contaminated fuel, unapproved silicone, oil and coolant.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the 1/1 Oxygen Sensor and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM). Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched, or partially broken wires. Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals. Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the 1/1 O2 Sensor and Powertrain Control Module connectors.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the Oxygen Sensor 1/1 in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to SENSOR, OXYGEN, REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0135 o2 sensor 1/1 heater performance\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0135</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 SENSOR 1/1 HEATER PERFORMANCE</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The primary objective of the Oxygen (O2) Sensor Heater Control is to attain the fastest possible temperature rise, and to control the steady state temperature of the O2 Sensor. A Positive Temperature Coefficient (PTC) element inside the O2 Sensor heats up as current passes through it. This allows the system to enter Closed Loop quickly and meet the OBD II requirements for continuous monitoring and to deliver accurate readings. This is accomplished by the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) sending a Pulse Width Modulated (PWM) current to the O2 Sensors heater element. The PWM duty cycle is calculated, in response to the temperature measurements. The PCM uses the collected information to continually adjust the PWM to achieve a desired steady state temperature target.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The heater temperature is obtained by measuring the heater resistance and calculating the heater temperature using the resistance versus temperature characteristic. In order to measure the heater temperature, the transistor that controls the heater is momentarily disabled and current is passed through a precision sense resistor connected in series with the heater element. The voltage drop across the sense resistor provides the relationship to current which equates to a temperature value. Once the temperature is acquired, the sense current is turned off and normal operation is restored. The actual heater temperature is monitored continuously and the heating rate is adjusted to prevent damaging the heating element.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The circuit fault diagnostics for O2 sensors are algorithm based. This means that conditions such as a short or an open does not necessarily set a fault the same as a traditional circuit fault. The PCM determines O2 Sensor circuit faults based on pre-programmed variables that are used in a calculation to determine the state of an O2 Sensor. This requires the integrity of all circuits to be verified in order to ensure that the cause of the fault is not overlooked.</p></div></div></details><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Engine running. Heater duty cycle greater than 0%. Battery voltage greater than 11.0 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">No temperature change is detected in the O2 Sensor Heater element when the heater circuit is active.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL light will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>O2 SENSOR 1/1 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT OPEN</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/1 HEATER GROUND CIRCUIT OPEN</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/1</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>O2 SENSOR 1/1 HEATER OPERATION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           Wait a minimum of eight minutes to allow the O2 Sensor to cool down before continuing the test. Allow the O2 Sensor voltage to stabilize between 4.6 and 5.0 volts.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>With the scan tool, actuate the O2 Sensor 1/1 Heater Test.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, monitor O2 Sensor 1/1 voltage for at least two minutes.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Does the voltage stay above 4.5 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>O2 SENSOR 1/1 HEATER ELEMENT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           Allow the O2 sensor to cool down to room temperature.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the O2 Sensor 1/1 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Measure the resistance of the O2 Sensor 1/1 Heater Element across the O2 Sensor, between the O2 Heater Control terminal and the Heater ground terminal.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           O2 Sensor 1/1 Heater Element resistance values should be measured at 21.1°C (70°F). The resistance value will vary with different temperature values.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance of the O2 Sensor 1/1 Heater Element between 2.0 and 30.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the Sensor and Control Module connectors. If OK, replace the O2 Sensor. Refer to SENSOR, OXYGEN, REMOVAL</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K79) O2 SENSOR 1/1 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:         Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Measure the resistance of the (K79) O2 Sensor 1/1 Heater Control circuit from the O2 Sensor 1/1 harness connector to the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 0.5 Ohm?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the excessive resistance in the (K79) O2 Sensor 1/1 Heater Control circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE O2 SENSOR 1/1 HEATER GROUND CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance between ground and the O2 Sensor 1/1 Heater Ground circuit in the O2 Sensor harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 0.5 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the excessive resistance in the O2 Sensor 1/1 Heater Ground circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Using the wiring diagram/schematic as a guide, inspect the wiring and connectors between the Oxygen Sensor 1/1 and the Powertrain Control Module (PCM).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Look for any chafed, pierced, pinched or partially broken wires.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Look for broken, bent, pushed out or corroded terminals. Verify that there is good pin to terminal contact in the Sensor and Control Module connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that may apply.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Were there any problems found?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair as necessary.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0137 o2 sensor 1/2 circuit low\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0137</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 SENSOR 1/2 CIRCUIT LOW</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">O2 Sensor Heater Operation: For a typical Switching (4 Wire) Oxygen (O2) Sensor output signal to function the sense element must be heated to operating temperature. A resistive heater element is incorporated within the sensor to allow for rapid heating of the sensor to reach operating temperature faster and closed loop fuel control. The O2 Sensor Heater resistance is directly proportional to the heater temperature, meaning that as heater temperature increases, the heater resistance increases. The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) makes a calculation to determine the heater resistance and temperature. The heater resistance is calculate by measuring the Battery voltage and current draw on the heater control circuit, Then the heater temperature is determined using the resistance versus temperature characteristic. The heater temperature is monitored continuously and the heating rate is adjusted using Pulse Width Modulation (PWM) to prevent damaging the heating element. If the PCM detects a fault in the O2 Sensor heater circuitry the internal PCM driver is disabled during the current ignition cycle,</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">O2 Sensor Output Operation: Normal range of the O2 Sensor output is a 0 to 1.0 volt Analog to Digital (A/D) signal when the sensor is in normal operating temperature range. The output voltage is generated by comparing the Oxygen content in the atmosphere, collected in a reference chamber to the Oxygen content in the exhaust stream collected in a comparison chamber. When the oxygen content is high (caused by a lean air/fuel mixture) the sensor produces a low voltage. When the oxygen content is low (caused by a rich air/fuel mixture) it produces a higher voltage. In some instances a negative offset output of up to -1.0 volt may be introduced if the sensor&#x27;s reference chamber is contaminated. To allow for the negative voltage to be read, each O2 Sensor Return circuit has a 2.5 volt bias added to shift the signal voltage to between 2.5 volts and 3.5 volts.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">O2 Sensor Diagnostics: The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) performs diagnostics on the heater control, signal and sensor return circuits. Because of how the O2 Sensor operates, the circuit fault diagnostics for O2 Sensors are algorithm based. This means an open or short in one of the O2 Sensor circuits can cause a fault to set against another of the O2 Sensor circuits. Understanding how to interpret the combination of DTCs and O2 Sensor data provided can aid in narrowing down where the fault is in the system.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">On vehicle/engine combinations that use only the traditional switching O2 Sensors, both of the Upstream and Downstream O2 Sensor Return circuits originate from a single source within the PCM. Because of this, a short anywhere in the wiring in any of the O2 Sensor Return circuits will affect all of the Upstream and Downstream O2 Sensors. On vehicle/engine combinations that have wide-band Upstream O2 Sensors and switching Downstream O2 Sensors, the Upstream and Downstream O2 Sensor Return circuits are separate within the PCM. In this case a short anywhere in the wiring in the Downstream O2 Sensor Return circuit(s) will only affect the Downstream O2 Sensor(s). The PCM performs individual diagnostics on the O2 Sensor Return circuit for each wide-band Upstream O2 Sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">In data display on the scan tool, filtering on O2 in the search function will bring up the following info:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">O2 Sensor signal voltage - the raw signal voltage should switch between 2.5 and 3.5 volts on a normally functioning sensor. O2 Sensor (0-1) signal voltage - this is the differential signal voltage which is calculated using the raw signal voltage minus the 2.5 volt bias on the return circuit of a normally functioning O2 Sensor. Heater driver operation - displays the heater duty cycle. Heater temperature - this is determined by the total heater circuit resistance. Closed loop fuel control state - displays as open/closed.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Using the Scan Tool for Diagnostics: An open circuit or direct short to voltage or ground will cause the PCM to disable the heater PWM control. The heater will remain disabled for the remainder of that ignition cycle, even if the condition is intermittent and heals itself during that ignition cycle. Any of these conditions should set an O2 Sensor Heater Low or High DTC. Since the heater directly affects the output of sensor signal, and the heater control is disabled, this will cause the affected O2 Sensor raw signal voltage to read high (usually stays above 4.0 volts) and can also eventually set a Signal Circuit High DTC. It is not unusual to have a Heater Control Circuit High or Low DTC and a Sensor Signal High DTC to be set together. Important Note: A small amount of resistance (3-4 Ohms) may not set a Heater Control Circuit DTC but will affect heater operation and cause the sensor signal high DTC to set. Monitoring the heater duty cycle and temperature for a sensor that is reading high on the raw signal voltage can help in diagnosing this issue. A heater circuit with 4.0 - 5.0 Ohms resistance can increase the heater temperature reading on the scan tool for the affected sensor by as much as 700 degrees when compared to the other Switching (4 wire) O2 Sensors. As little as 2.0 Ohms can increase the temperature reading approximately 250 degrees on the scan tool.</p><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE: When a normally operating Switching O2 Sensor is heated to operating temperature the typical duty cycle percentage will be between approximately 30 and 50 percent. The O2 Sensor Heater Diagnosis:                       typical heater temperature will range between approximately 1200°F and 1400°F on the scan tool. If there is an issue with the O2 Sensor heater or circuitry the PCM will disable the heater driver and the duty cycle will be 0 percent. Any issues in the heater circuitry, even a small amount of resistance, will cause the temperature reading to be noticeably different. Monitoring the scan tool and starting the engine when the O2 Sensor Signal is shorted to ground, and when the O2 Sensor is cold, it will show the raw signal voltage at 0 volts but the (0-1) differential voltage signal will read -2.5 volts and increase toward O2 Sensor Signal Low Diagnosis:                   0.0 volts as the sensor heats up. When the sensor is warm the raw signal voltage reading and the (0-1) differential voltage reading will both read near 0.0 volts. It will also pull the sensor return bias voltage low through the O2 Sensor. This will cause the raw voltage signals to switch between the 0 -1 volt range on the other O2 Sensors. This may also cause the O2 Sensor Reference</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Using the Scan Tool for Diagnostics: (Return) Voltage Circuit Low DTC to set. The O2 Sensor Signal High diagnostic can fail due to several conditions. As mentioned above, an open, short or any resistance in the heater circuitry can cause the signal voltage to remain high. Looking for abnormalities in the heater duty cycle or heater temperature of the affected sensor would indicate a heater control issue causing the signal circuit high fault. An open in a sensor O2 Sensor Signal High Diagnosis: return circuit will cause the raw signal voltage to read high, near 5.0 volts, and the (0-1) differential voltage reading to be near 2.5 volts for the affected sensor or sensors. An open in the signal circuit will have the same effect on the sensor. If both Upstream or Downstream O2 Sensors are affected, it could indicate an open in the return circuit that is before the splice in the harness.</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p1621 o2 sensor reference (return) circuit the o2 sensor reference circuit low/high diagnostic is used to monitor the o2 sensor return circuits. if any of the return\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P1621</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 Sensor Reference (Return) Circuit The O2 Sensor Reference Circuit Low/High diagnostic is used to monitor the O2 Sensor Return circuits. If any of the return</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Low Diagnosis:                             circuits are shorted to ground the raw signal voltage readings for all O2 Sensors will read between 0-1 volts instead of 2.5-3.5 volts. This will happen because the bias voltage on the return circuit is pulled low for all sensors. As mentioned in the O2 Sensor Signal Low Diagnosis above, a short to ground in one of the sensor signal circuits will also pull the sensor return low for all sensors. However, this will cause the voltage reading on the sensor that has the shorted signal to read 0.0 volts on both the raw signal voltage and (0-1) differential voltage reading when the sensor is warm.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p1622 o2 sensor reference (return) circuit a short to voltage in any of the sensor return circuits will cause the fault to set. the affected o2 sensors will read 5.0 volts on\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P1622</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 Sensor Reference (Return) Circuit A short to voltage in any of the sensor return circuits will cause the fault to set. The affected O2 Sensors will read 5.0 volts on</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">High Diagnosis:                            the raw signal voltage. The (0-1) differential voltage reading will be 0.0 volts because the signal circuit voltage and return circuit voltage are both 5.0 volts. A short to voltage on one of the O2 Sensor signal circuits will cause the same fault condition and scan tool readings when the sensor is warm. If the condition is present when the engine is started and the sensor is cold, the (0-1) differential voltage reading will start at 2.5 volts and decrease to 0.0 volts as the sensor warms up.</p></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">Battery voltage between 11.0 and 15.75 volts. With the engine running.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Oxygen Sensor 1/2 signal voltage is less than 0.0 volts for 9.0 seconds.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>O2 SENSOR 1/2 SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO GROUND</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/2 SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE O2 SENSOR 1/2 HEATER GROUND CIRCUIT</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/2</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR ANY SERVICE BULLETINS OR PCM SOFTWARE UPDATES</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for any applicable Service Bulletins or Flash updates related to the DTC.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Are there any applicable Service Bulletins or Flash updates?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the applicable repair.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 2</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>READ AND RECORD DTCS AND ENVIRONMENTAL DATA - ERASE DTCS AND CHECK FOR THE DTC TO RETURN</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs in all Electronic Control Units (ECUs) and record on the repair order.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>For future reference, with the scan tool, run and save a vehicle Scan Report and all related recorded data.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>With the scan tool, erase all DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Turn the ignition off for a minimum of 10.0 seconds.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Using the When Monitored and Set Conditions above and recorded data, operate the vehicle in the conditions that set the DTC.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Did the DTC return?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 3</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE (K141) O2 SENSOR 1/2 SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the O2 Sensor 1/2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Check for continuity between ground and the (K141) O2 Sensor 1/2 Signal circuit at the O2 Sensor 1/2 harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between ground and the (K141) O2 Sensor 1/2 Signal circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the (K141) O2 Sensor 1/2 Signal circuit for a short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 4</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK FOR THE (K141) O2 SENSOR 1/2 SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO THE O2 SENSOR 1/2 (Z703) GROUND CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for continuity between the (K141) O2 Sensor 1/2 Signal circuit and the O2 Sensor 1/2 (Z703) Ground circuit in the O2 Sensor 1/2 harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is there continuity between the (K141) O2 Sensor 1/2 Signal circuit and the O2 Sensor 1/2 (Z703) Ground circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the short between the (K141) O2 Sensor 1/2 Signal circuit and the O2 Sensor 1/2 (Z703) ground circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>CHECK THE (K141) O2 SENSOR 1/2 SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR PROPER VOLTAGE AT THE O2 SENSOR HARNESS CONNECTOR</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Start the engine and allow it to idle.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, read the O2 Sensor 1/2 Signal voltage.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the O2 Sensor 1/2 voltage above 4.0 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace the O2 Sensor 1/2 in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to SENSOR, OXYGEN, REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Go To 6</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>CHECK RELATED PCM AND COMPONENT CONNECTIONS</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that apply.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect all PCM harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect all related in-line harness connections (if equipped).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Disconnect the related component harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Inspect harness connectors, component connectors, and all male and female terminals for the following conditions: Proper connector installation. Damaged connector locks. Corrosion. Other signs of water intrusion. Weather seal damage (if equipped). Bent terminals. Overheating due to a poor connection (terminal may be discolored due to excessive current draw). Terminals that have been pushed back into the connector cavity. Check for spread terminals and verify proper terminal tension.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Repair any conditions that are found.</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Reconnect all PCM harness connectors. Be certain that all harness connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>Reconnect all in-line harness connectors (if equipped). Be certain that all connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">8</span><span>Reconnect all related component harness connectors. Be certain that all connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">9</span><span>With the scan tool, erase DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">10</span><span>Test drive or operate the vehicle in accordance with the when monitored and set conditions.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">11</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Did the DTC return?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">The wiring or poor connection problem has been repaired.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p0138 o2 sensor 1/2 circuit high\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P0138</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 SENSOR 1/2 CIRCUIT HIGH</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">THEORY OF OPERATION</strong><small>نظرية العمل</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">O2 Sensor Heater Operation: For a typical Switching (4 Wire) Oxygen (O2) Sensor output signal to function the sense element must be heated to operating temperature. A resistive heater element is incorporated within the sensor to allow for rapid heating of the sensor to reach operating temperature faster and closed loop fuel control. The O2 Sensor Heater resistance is directly proportional to the heater temperature, meaning that as heater temperature increases, the heater resistance increases. The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) makes a calculation to determine the heater resistance and temperature. The heater resistance is calculate by measuring the Battery voltage and current draw on the heater control circuit, Then the heater temperature is determined using the resistance versus temperature characteristic. The heater temperature is monitored continuously and the heating rate is adjusted using Pulse Width Modulation (PWM) to prevent damaging the heating element. If the PCM detects a fault in the O2 Sensor heater circuitry the internal PCM driver is disabled during the current ignition cycle,</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">O2 Sensor Output Operation: Normal range of the O2 Sensor output is a 0 to 1.0 volt Analog to Digital (A/D) signal when the sensor is in normal operating temperature range. The output voltage is generated by comparing the Oxygen content in the atmosphere, collected in a reference chamber to the Oxygen content in the exhaust stream collected in a comparison chamber. When the oxygen content is high (caused by a lean air/fuel mixture) the sensor produces a low voltage. When the oxygen content is low (caused by a rich air/fuel mixture) it produces a higher voltage. In some instances a negative offset output of up to -1.0 volt may be introduced if the sensor&#x27;s reference chamber is contaminated. To allow for the negative voltage to be read, each O2 Sensor Return circuit has a 2.5 volt bias added to shift the signal voltage to between 2.5 volts and 3.5 volts.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">O2 Sensor Diagnostics: The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) performs diagnostics on the heater control, signal and sensor return circuits. Because of how the O2 Sensor operates, the circuit fault diagnostics for O2 Sensors are algorithm based. This means an open or short in one of the O2 Sensor circuits can cause a fault to set against another of the O2 Sensor circuits. Understanding how to interpret the combination of DTCs and O2 Sensor data provided can aid in narrowing down where the fault is in the system.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">On vehicle/engine combinations that use only the traditional switching O2 Sensors, both of the Upstream and Downstream O2 Sensor Return circuits originate from a single source within the PCM. Because of this, a short anywhere in the wiring in any of the O2 Sensor Return circuits will affect all of the Upstream and Downstream O2 Sensors. On vehicle/engine combinations that have wide-band Upstream O2 Sensors and switching Downstream O2 Sensors, the Upstream and Downstream O2 Sensor Return circuits are separate within the PCM. In this case a short anywhere in the wiring in the Downstream O2 Sensor Return circuit(s) will only affect the Downstream O2 Sensor(s). The PCM performs individual diagnostics on the O2 Sensor Return circuit for each wide-band Upstream O2 Sensor.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">In data display on the scan tool, filtering on O2 in the search function will bring up the following info:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">O2 Sensor signal voltage - the raw signal voltage should switch between 2.5 and 3.5 volts on a normally functioning sensor. O2 Sensor (0-1) signal voltage - this is the differential signal voltage which is calculated using the raw signal voltage minus the 2.5 volt bias on the return circuit of a normally functioning O2 Sensor. Heater driver operation - displays the heater duty cycle. Heater temperature - this is determined by the total heater circuit resistance. Closed loop fuel control state - displays as open/closed.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Using the Scan Tool for Diagnostics: An open circuit or direct short to voltage or ground will cause the PCM to disable the heater PWM control. The heater will remain disabled for the remainder of that ignition cycle, even if the condition is intermittent and heals itself during that ignition cycle. Any of these conditions should set an O2 Sensor Heater Low or High DTC. Since the heater directly affects the output of sensor signal, and the heater control is disabled, this will cause the affected O2 Sensor raw signal voltage to read high (usually stays above 4.0 volts) and can also eventually set a Signal Circuit High DTC. It is not unusual to have a Heater Control Circuit High or Low DTC and a Sensor Signal High DTC to be set together. Important Note: A small amount of resistance (3-4 Ohms) may not set a Heater Control Circuit DTC but will affect heater operation and cause the sensor signal high DTC to set. Monitoring the heater duty cycle and temperature for a sensor that is reading high on the raw signal voltage can help in diagnosing this issue. A heater circuit with 4.0 - 5.0 Ohms resistance can increase the heater temperature reading on the scan tool for the affected sensor by as much as 700 degrees when compared to the other Switching (4 wire) O2 Sensors. As little as 2.0 Ohms can increase the temperature reading approximately 250 degrees on the scan tool.</p><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE: When a normally operating Switching O2 Sensor is heated to operating temperature the typical duty cycle percentage will be between approximately 30 and 50 percent. The O2 Sensor Heater Diagnosis:                       typical heater temperature will range between approximately 1200°F and 1400°F on the scan tool. If there is an issue with the O2 Sensor heater or circuitry the PCM will disable the heater driver and the duty cycle will be 0 percent. Any issues in the heater circuitry, even a small amount of resistance, will cause the temperature reading to be noticeably different. Monitoring the scan tool and starting the engine when the O2 Sensor Signal is shorted to ground, and when the O2 Sensor is cold, it will show the raw signal voltage at 0 volts but the (0-1) differential voltage signal will read -2.5 volts and increase toward O2 Sensor Signal Low Diagnosis:                   0.0 volts as the sensor heats up. When the sensor is warm the raw signal voltage reading and the (0-1) differential voltage reading will both read near 0.0 volts. It will also pull the sensor return bias voltage low through the O2 Sensor. This will cause the raw voltage signals to switch between the 0 -1 volt range on the other O2 Sensors. This may also cause the O2 Sensor Reference</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Using the Scan Tool for Diagnostics: (Return) Voltage Circuit Low DTC to set. The O2 Sensor Signal High diagnostic can fail due to several conditions. As mentioned above, an open, short or any resistance in the heater circuitry can cause the signal voltage to remain high. Looking for abnormalities in the heater duty cycle or heater temperature of the affected sensor would indicate a heater control issue causing the signal circuit high fault. An open in a sensor O2 Sensor Signal High Diagnosis: return circuit will cause the raw signal voltage to read high, near 5.0 volts, and the (0-1) differential voltage reading to be near 2.5 volts for the affected sensor or sensors. An open in the signal circuit will have the same effect on the sensor. If both Upstream or Downstream O2 Sensors are affected, it could indicate an open in the return circuit that is before the splice in the harness.</p></div></div></details></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p1621 o2 sensor reference (return) circuit the o2 sensor reference circuit low/high diagnostic is used to monitor the o2 sensor return circuits. if any of the return\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P1621</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 Sensor Reference (Return) Circuit The O2 Sensor Reference Circuit Low/High diagnostic is used to monitor the O2 Sensor Return circuits. If any of the return</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">Low Diagnosis:                             circuits are shorted to ground the raw signal voltage readings for all O2 Sensors will read between 0-1 volts instead of 2.5-3.5 volts. This will happen because the bias voltage on the return circuit is pulled low for all sensors. As mentioned in the O2 Sensor Signal Low Diagnosis above, a short to ground in one of the sensor signal circuits will also pull the sensor return low for all sensors. However, this will cause the voltage reading on the sensor that has the shorted signal to read 0.0 volts on both the raw signal voltage and (0-1) differential voltage reading when the sensor is warm.</p></div></div></details><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv2 acc-dtc\" data-s=\"p1622 o2 sensor reference (return) circuit a short to voltage in any of the sensor return circuits will cause the fault to set. the affected o2 sensors will read 5.0 volts on\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">P1622</strong><small dir=\"ltr\" style=\"opacity:.8\">O2 Sensor Reference (Return) Circuit A short to voltage in any of the sensor return circuits will cause the fault to set. The affected O2 Sensors will read 5.0 volts on</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">High Diagnosis:                            the raw signal voltage. The (0-1) differential voltage reading will be 0.0 volts because the signal circuit voltage and return circuit voltage are both 5.0 volts. A short to voltage on one of the O2 Sensor signal circuits will cause the same fault condition and scan tool readings when the sensor is warm. If the condition is present when the engine is started and the sensor is cold, the (0-1) differential voltage reading will start at 2.5 volts and decrease to 0.0 volts as the sensor warms up.</p></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>متى يتحقق · <span dir=\"ltr\">When Monitored</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">This diagnostic runs continuously when the following conditions are met:</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\">With the engine running. Battery voltage greater than 10.4 volts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>شرط التسجيل · <span dir=\"ltr\">Set Condition</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The Powertrain Control Module (PCM) detects that the Oxygen Sensor 1/2 signal voltage is above 1.2 volts for 9.0 seconds.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الإجراء الافتراضي · <span dir=\"ltr\">Default Action</span></b><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\">The MIL will illuminate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ac-f\"><b>الأسباب المحتملة · <span dir=\"ltr\">Possible Causes</span></b><ul class=\"en-ul\" dir=\"ltr\"><li>O2 SENSOR 1/2 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/2 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO GROUND</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/2 HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/2 HEATER GROUND CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/2 SIGNAL CIRCUIT SHORTED TO VOLTAGE</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/2 SIGNAL CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/2 RETURN CIRCUIT OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</li><li>O2 SENSOR 1/2</li><li>POWERTRAIN CONTROL MODULE (PCM)</li><li>Always perform the PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE before proceeding. Refer to PRE-DIAGNOSTIC TROUBLESHOOTING PROCEDURE .</li></ul></div><details class=\"pcm-component-details ct-d en-sec lv3\"><summary><span class=\"ct-ttl\"><strong dir=\"ltr\">DIAGNOSTIC TEST</strong><small>خطوات الفحص</small></span></summary><div class=\"ct-body\"><div class=\"en-raw\" dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>CHECK FOR AN ACTIVE CONDITION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Start the engine and allow it to idle for at least 60 seconds.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">WARNING:         When the engine is operating, do not stand in direct line with the fan. Do not put your hands near the pulleys, belts or fan. Do not wear loose clothing. Failure to follow these instructions may result in possible serious or fatal injury.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>With the scan tool, read the O2 Sensor 1/2 signal voltage.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Is the voltage staying above 4.0 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Go To 2</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the INTERMITTENT CONDITION diagnostic procedure. Refer to INTERMITTENT CONDITION .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>CHECK THE O2 SENSOR 1/2 HEATER OPERATION</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>With the scan tool, check the O2 Sensor 1/2 duty cycle. Also monitor the O2 Sensor 1/2 heater temperature and compare to the other Downstream O2 Sensor heater temperature.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:            The duty cycle percentage and heater temperature should be relatively close to the other sensor. If there is an issue with the O2 Sensor heater or circuitry the PCM will disable the heater driver and the duty cycle will be 0 percent. Any issues in the heater circuitry, even a small amount of resistance, will cause the temperature reading to be noticeably different than the other O2 Sensor.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Is the O2 Sensor heater operating properly?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Go To 7</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Go To 3</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>CHECK THE O2 SENSOR 1/2 (Z703) GROUND CIRCUIT FOR HIGH RESISTANCE BY LOAD TESTING THE CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          Read the CIRCUIT LOAD TESTING PROCEDURE for information on building a simple load test tool and for additional load testing information and alternative methods of load testing or voltage drop testing a circuit. Refer to STANDARD PROCEDURE .</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition off.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the O2 Sensor 1/2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not load test any circuits with components still connected to the circuit.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Load test the O2 Sensor 1/2 (Z703) Ground circuit at the O2 Sensor 1/2 harness connector (A). Note: refer to the diagram below.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The bulb on the load test tool should be illuminated and bright if there is no resistance in the circuitry. Compare the brightness of the bulb in the load test tool to that of a direct connection across Battery.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Is the load test bulb illuminated and bright?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Go To 4</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Repair the O2 Sensor 1/2 (Z703) Ground circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>CHECK THE O2 SENSOR 1/2 (K299) HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO ANOTHER VOLTAGE SUPPLY CIRCUIT</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic) to the appropriate PCM connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Check for continuity between the O2 Sensor 1/2 (K299) Heater Control circuit and all other circuits at the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Is there continuity between the O2 Sensor 1/2 (K299) Heater Control circuit and any other circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Repair the O2 Sensor 1/2 (K299) Heater Control circuit for a short to the circuit that showed continuity.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Go To 5</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>CHECK THE O2 SENSOR 1/2 (K299) HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO GROUND</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Check for continuity between ground and the O2 Sensor 1/2 (K299) Heater Control circuit at the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Is there continuity between ground and the O2 Sensor 1/2 (K299) Heater Control circuit?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Repair the O2 Sensor 1/2 (K299) Heater Control circuit for a short to ground.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Go To 6</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>ISOLATE AND LOAD TEST THE O2 SENSOR 1/2 (K299) HEATER CONTROL CIRCUIT TO CHECK FOR HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:          Read the CIRCUIT LOAD TESTING PROCEDURE for information on building a simple load test tool and for additional load testing information and alternative methods of load testing or voltage drop testing a circuit. Refer to STANDARD PROCEDURE .</div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:       Do not load test any circuits with components still connected to the circuit.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Load test the O2 Sensor 1/2 (K299) Heater Control circuit.</span></div><div class=\"ct-note en-kw\">NOTE:           The bulb on the load test tool should be illuminated and bright if there is no resistance in the circuit. Compare the brightness of the bulb in the load test tool to that of a direct connection across Battery.</div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the load test bulb illuminated and bright?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Go To 10</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Repair the O2 Sensor 1/2 (K299) Heater Control circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>CHECK THE O2 SENSOR 1/2 (K141) SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR A SHORT TO VOLTAGE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Turn the ignition on, engine not running.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect the O2 Sensor 1/2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Measure the voltage on the O2 Sensor 1/2 (K141) Signal circuit at the O2 Sensor 1/2 harness connector.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the voltage above 5.2 volts?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Repair the O2 Sensor 1/2 (K141) Signal circuit for a short to voltage.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Go To 8</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">8</span><span>CHECK THE O2 SENSOR 1/2 (K141) SIGNAL CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Disconnect the PCM C2 harness connector.</span></div><div class=\"ct-warn en-kw\">CAUTION:        Do not probe the PCM harness connectors. Probing the PCM harness connectors will damage the PCM terminals resulting in poor terminal to pin connection. Install the GPEC Diagnostic Adaptor to perform the diagnosis.</div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect the (special tool #10436, Adapter, GPEC Diagnostic) to the appropriate PCM connector.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Measure the resistance of the O2 Sensor 1/2 (K141) Signal circuit between the O2 Sensor 1/2 harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Go To 9</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Repair the (K141) O2 Sensor 1/2 Signal circuit for an open or high resistance.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">9</span><span>CHECK THE O2 SENSOR 1/2 (K904) RETURN CIRCUIT FOR AN OPEN/HIGH RESISTANCE</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Measure the resistance of the O2 Sensor 1/2 (K904) Return circuit between the O2 Sensor 1/2 harness connector and the GPEC Adaptor.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Is the resistance below 3.0 Ohms?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Go To 10</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair the O2 Sensor 1/2 (K904) Return circuit for a an open or high resistance. If the O2 Sensor 2/1 Signal Circuit High DTC is also active it would indicate that the open is most likely before the splice in the return circuit.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:0px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">10</span><span>REPLACE THE O2 SENSOR 1/2 AND RETEST FOR DTC</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Replace the O2 Sensor 1/2 in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to SENSOR, OXYGEN, REMOVAL .</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Connect the O2 Sensor 1/2 and PCM harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Turn the ignition on.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>With the scan tool, erase DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Using the recorded Freeze Frame and the When Monitored conditions, operate the vehicle in the conditions that set the DTC.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Did the DTC return?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Go To 11</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Replacing the O2 Sensor repaired the fault.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:10px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">11</span><span>CHECK RELATED PCM AND COMPONENT CONNECTIONS</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">1</span><span>Perform any Service Bulletins that apply.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">2</span><span>Disconnect all PCM harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">3</span><span>Disconnect all related in-line harness connections (if equipped).</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">4</span><span>Disconnect the related component harness connectors.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">5</span><span>Inspect harness connectors, component connectors, and all male and female terminals for the following conditions: Proper connector installation. Damaged connector locks. Corrosion. Other signs of water intrusion. Weather seal damage (if equipped). Bent terminals. Overheating due to a poor connection (terminal may be discolored due to excessive current draw). Terminals that have been pushed back into the connector cavity. Check for spread terminals and verify proper terminal tension.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Repair any conditions that are found.</p><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">6</span><span>Reconnect all PCM harness connectors. Be certain that all harness connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">7</span><span>Reconnect all in-line harness connectors (if equipped). Be certain that all connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">8</span><span>Reconnect all related component harness connectors. Be certain that all connectors are fully seated and the connector locks are fully engaged.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">9</span><span>With the scan tool, erase DTCs.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">10</span><span>Test drive or operate the vehicle in accordance with the when monitored and set conditions.</span></div><div class=\"en-step\" style=\"margin-inline-start:20px\"><span class=\"ec-no\">11</span><span>With the scan tool, read DTCs.</span></div><p style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">Did the DTC return?</p><p class=\"ac-yn yes\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">نعم · Yes</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Replace and program the Powertrain Control Module (PCM) in accordance with the Service Information. Refer to MODULE, POWERTRAIN CONTROL (PCM), REMOVAL .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p class=\"ac-yn no\" style=\"margin-inline-start:30px\">لا · No</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">The wiring or poor connection problem has been repaired.</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Perform the POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST. Refer to POWERTRAIN VERIFICATION TEST .</p><p style=\"margin-inline-start:40px\">Article GUID: A00910318</p></div></div></details></div></details></div>";
